                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T21:14:07.489411065Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Scanner names are already ASCII by construction — isvar (src/scan.rs:210) accepts only ascii_alphanumeric, underscore, hyphen and dot, and issimplevar (scan.rs:204) the same minus dot — yet three sites re-validate the slice with std::str::from_utf8().expect (scan.rs:301, 385, 415) purely so Lexeme.text can be &str and Names::intern can take &str. The manifest buffer itself cannot be &str because Ninja manifests may hold non-UTF-8 paths [spec:ronin:req:compat.byte-inputs], so the names are an ASCII subset of a buffer that is not valid UTF-8 as a whole, and safe Rust has no zero-cost way to reinterpret that subslice. Callgrind on a 4,000-edge no-op fixture puts core::str::converts::from_utf8 at 614,479 instructions, 2.06% of the program, across 8,003 calls from scan::name alone — roughly 77 instructions to validate a five-byte keyword, because std's usize-chunked ASCII fast path does not engage below eight bytes. The fix is to use the byte-string type the crate already re-exports at src/util.rs:8 rather than to add unsafe: Lexeme.text and ScannedEvalPart::Variable become &BStr, Names keys on BString with borrowed &BStr probes, and env::envrule and env::poolget take &BStr. Verified that HashMap<BString, _> supports allocation-free &BStr lookup with agreeing hashes, and that BStr provides Display and Debug, which was the only thing &str was buying for diagnostics. Blast radius is 14 .text use sites. The crate has exactly three unsafe blocks, both files FFI-adjacent, so from_utf8_unchecked was rejected as out of character for a 2% win."
                    }

