                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Selected from callgrind rather than from the allocation harness, which the streamed-path-scanning result retired as a target. RawVec::grow_one is 15.52% of the 4,000-edge no-op build inclusive — 4,638,289 instructions across 20,115 growth events, itself larger than the 3,385,997-instruction total gap to C samurai. Growth is malloc plus memcpy plus free per event, a materially different cost from the fresh tcache-hit allocations whose removal bought nothing. The largest callers are scan::scanstring at 1,497,918 (5.01%, 8,002 calls), graph::nodeuse at 1,052,300 (3.52%, 4,000), parse::parse at 1,020,997 (3.42%, 4,002) and scan::scanpaths at 548,457 (1.83%, 4,013); mknode and mkedge account for only 1.51% across 22 calls and are the arena Vecs doubling, which is already amortization-optimal. Every one of the large callers is a first push onto an empty Vec — one output per build statement, one use per leaf node, one literal part per path — so inline capacity removes the allocation outright with none of the staging-buffer bookkeeping that made streamed scanning cost more than it saved. Sizing matters: with smallvec's union feature a SmallVec is 8 bytes plus max(inline array, 16), so for the 4-byte NonZeroU32 arena ids a [T; 4] inline array occupies 16 bytes and the whole value is 24 — byte-identical to the Vec it replaces, making inline capacity free for Node.uses, Node.validation_uses, Edge.out, Edge.input and Edge.validation. ScannedEvalPart is 24 bytes, so giving ScannedEvalString.parts inline capacity costs 8 bytes per string and is measured separately rather than assumed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T21:52:48.810235124Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:02:10.737129901Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented and kept — the largest result in this programme, and a direct reversal of the inference recorded on ronin-streamed-path-scanning. Node.uses, Node.validation_uses, Edge.out, Edge.input and Edge.validation became IdVec<T> = SmallVec<[T; 4]>, and ScannedEvalString.parts became SmallVec<[ScannedEvalPart; 1]>. Sizing drove the design: under smallvec's union feature a value is eight bytes plus max(inline array, pointer/length pair), so four four-byte arena identifiers occupy exactly the twenty-four bytes a Vec already spends — inline capacity is free for every adjacency list, and util::tests::id_vec_matches_vec_footprint pins both the size equality and the fifth-element spill. ScannedEvalPart is 24 bytes so one inline slot widens ScannedEvalString from 24 to 32, which was measured on its own rather than assumed. Callgrind: wide no-op 29,226,333 to 26,547,330 for the graph vectors alone and to 24,102,023 with the scanner, −17.5% total; command evaluation 54,187,522 to 48,039,297 to 42,784,164, −21.0%. Allocation requests fell 24,258 to 8,254 on wide no-op, taking requests per build statement from 6.1 to 2.1 and reaching the single-digit end state the benchmark document set as the target; command evaluation fell 80,087 to 48,084. Peak RSS fell from 11,532 to 11,288 KiB because inline storage replaced heap blocks whose per-block allocator overhead is no longer paid. Wall time moved for the first time in this programme and unambiguously: interleaved minimum-of-30 rounds on a host at load average 8 gave command-evaluation −11.1, −11.8, −11.3 and −11.9 percent and wide-noop −5.5, −4.0, −5.2 and −4.2 percent, consistent in sign and magnitude across every round. Against C samurai the same protocol gives command-evaluation 0.894x, 0.938x and 0.928x — faster than the C reference, against 1.65x at the 2026-07-29 baseline — and wide-noop 1.031x, 1.055x and 1.066x. 366 tests pass, clippy clean, allocation baseline re-recorded so the guard cannot drift back. The methodological finding: ronin-streamed-path-scanning attacked scanstring and scanpaths, the same two sites, and cost 1.1%; inline capacity on those sites saves 9.2%. The target was never wrong, the mechanism was — reusable buffers pay staging, clearing and index bookkeeping on every path to avoid an allocation, while inline capacity pays nothing because the storage is already there. That entry's inference from \"removing allocations bought nothing\" to \"SmallVec will buy nothing\" was unsound: allocation count had saturated, but growth events, which cost malloc plus memcpy plus free apiece, had not."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:51:08.261676096Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-inline-slice-hoisting
                title "Resolve inline-vs-heap once per edge in graph traversals"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:24:49.005651677Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:27:51.251995738Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Kept, at roughly a third of the predicted size. Six traversal loops paid the inline-vs-heap discriminant check per element instead of per edge, and several re-resolved the arena entry as well — graph.edge(edge).out[index] performing an arena bounds check, a discriminant branch and a slice bounds check for every element. Binding the slice once per edge resolves all three. Every hoist compiled without a borrow-checker change, confirming the per-element form was gratuitous rather than forced. Wide no-op 24,102,023 to 23,905,714 instructions, −0.81%; command evaluation 42,784,164 to 42,784,009, which is no movement at all. DirtyEvaluator::evaluate fell 1,348,434 to 1,220,413 (−9.5%) but Builder::add_target only 1,772,899 to 1,752,928 (−1.1%). The prediction was around 2%, on the theory that this would recover the 212,024 and 328,070 instructions inline capacity had added to those two functions. The shortfall is explained by loop length: the command-evaluation fixture gives each edge one input and one output, so there is nothing for a hoist to amortize over, and add_target already bound its edge to a local, leaving only a discriminant the optimizer was evidently hoisting unaided. Hoisting pays in proportion to loop length, which the estimate did not account for. Wall time was not measured because 0.81% sits well inside the established noise floor. Kept regardless: iterating a slice is also simply better code than indexing with a bounds check per element. 366 tests pass, clippy clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:51:13.324619869Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-scanner-byte-primitive
                title "Stop re-reading each manifest byte three times"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:29:17.315280052Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:32:31.181152424Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Kept at 1.35%, against an estimate of 6 to 8 percent, and the miss invalidates the model the estimate rested on. current() resolved &Arc<Source> to Vec<u8> to slice on every byte read; next() re-read the byte it was consuming to test it for a newline, then read again to return a value that all nineteen call sites discard — three bounds-checked loads per byte where one suffices. Caching the byte slice in the Scanner, changing next to return unit, and adding advance_within_line for the hot loops (all of which match the newline case in an earlier arm, so the test is provably dead there) fixes each. Wide no-op 23,905,714 to 23,584,069, −1.35%; command evaluation 42,784,009 to 42,226,722, −1.30%. scan::space fell 964,293 to 772,233 (−19.9%) because it is almost entirely singlespace calling next; scanstring fell 2,376,959 to 2,273,389 (−4.4%); scan::name rose 1,128,453 to 1,152,462, which at 2% on one function is codegen noise from changed inlining. The estimate assumed roughly 18 instructions per manifest byte and that removing two loads and a branch would halve it. The arithmetic held but the model did not: scanstring runs 16,005 times over a 129,809-byte manifest, 8.1 bytes per call, so its 142 instructions per call are dominated by per-call overhead — building and returning the fragment list, push_literal, the trailing space call on the path branch — rather than by the byte loop, which optimizing could only ever reach a fraction of. Two consequences follow. Deriving line and column lazily on error, the other half of the original proposal, is rejected without being attempted: it would mean stripping line and column from ByteSpan and recovering them in source_span, invasive across the whole error surface, to remove one increment from a loop running eight times per call — about 0.5% by the same arithmetic that just overestimated fivefold. And the escape-free fast path becomes the priority, because it attacks per-call cost directly: a path containing no dollar sign needs no fragment list, no evaluation and no copy, and in this fixture that is every path. 366 tests pass, clippy clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:51:15.304546849Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-plain-path-fast-path
                title "Intern escape-free paths straight from manifest bytes"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:27:30.527355364Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented and kept, at 3.4% against an estimate of about 10%. ScannedEvalString became an enum: Plain(&'source [u8]) when no dollar sign ever appeared, Parts(...) when the value needs expanding. Almost every manifest path is Plain, and node_for can hash and probe a Plain path that is_canonical already accepts directly against the manifest bytes — no evaluation, no copy into scratch, no canonicalization pass, and no allocation at all unless the node is new. Wide no-op 23,584,069 to 22,776,535 (−3.42%), command evaluation 42,226,722 to 40,892,549 (−3.16%). canonpath vanishes from the profile, inlined into a node_for that no longer calls it on the common path; node_for plus canonpath together fell 1,790,554 to 1,078,356, which is −712,198 of the −807,534 total. The shortfall against estimate has a different cause from the previous two nodes: the mechanical part landed exactly as predicted, but mknode did not move at all, staying at 1,388,925, and the estimate had counted it as partly addressable. Hashing a path and probing the index is irreducible — interning requires it however the bytes arrive — so roughly half the estimate was assigned to work no fast path can remove. A first cut regressed requested bytes about 4% with request count flat, the signature of a widened value rather than a new allocation: the enum is max(16, 32) plus a discriminant, 40 bytes against the previous struct's 32, so every Vec<ScannedEvalString> grew and scanpaths builds one per run of paths. The fix was recognising that the inline slot in ScannedParts had become redundant — it existed to avoid an allocation for single-fragment values and Plain now takes exactly those — so reverting ScannedParts to a plain Vec returns the enum to 32 bytes and drops bytes below where the node started (wide no-op 4,987,636 to 4,794,044, command evaluation 8,176,630 to 7,726,990). Inline capacity is worth paying for only where it is load-bearing; a better representation had displaced it. One test caught a real error: the scanner's borrow assertion was rewritten to expect Plain, but its fixture value contains an expansion and is legitimately Parts. 366 tests pass, clippy clean, allocation baseline re-recorded."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T11:26:50.580472655Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-prune-clean-subtrees
                title "Stop replanning input cones the dirty pass proved clean"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:33:17.863270689Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:38:38.30493885Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Attempted, disproved by an existing test, reverted. The premise was that a no-op build walks the graph twice — DirtyEvaluator::evaluate to compute dirtiness, then add_node to plan — and that since recompute_edge_dirty_with stamps one dirty bit onto every output of an edge (graph.rs:298-300) and folds input_dirty in, a clean edge proves its whole input cone clean. The induction is wrong. input_dirty is folded over non_order_only_inputs() only, so a clean edge can still carry a dirty order-only input: order-only dependencies are built without dirtying their consumer. ninja_build_generated_dyndep_now_wants_edge_and_dependent is exactly that shape — build $dir/tmp: touch || $dir/dd, where tmp exists and is clean, dd is missing and dirty, and tmp is reached through the non-order-only input of another clean edge. Pruning at the first clean edge never reaches dd and the build ran zero commands where three were required. Descending into the pruned edge's own order-only inputs does not rescue it, because the dirty order-only input sits further down behind an edge the prune already skipped; finding it needs precisely the traversal being avoided. A sound version exists — fold a per-edge subtree-contains-work flag during the dirty pass's Work::Finish, which does visit the full input vector — but it is not cheap to get right: restat-clean edges short-circuit at graph.rs:240 without reading their inputs, so the flag would be asserted rather than computed, and dyndep adds inputs mid-build, requiring invalidation on paths that presently need no such care. That is new state in the correctness-critical part of the planner. Decisively, it would not have shown on any fixture we have: measured with the unsound version in place, wide no-op came to 22,796,553 instructions against 22,776,535 without it, 20,018 worse, because the prune never fires and only its guard costs. Every edge in that fixture is dirty — build leaf/N: phony has no file on disk, so missing_without_inputs holds for all 4,000 leaves and for the all edge above them. The workload named wide-noop-build is therefore not a no-op but a fully dirty phony graph serving as a scanning benchmark, and the most common real workload for a build tool — running against an up-to-date tree where the whole graph is clean — is not represented in this suite at all. A clean-tree fixture with real files and current mtimes is a prerequisite for judging this node, not a nicety. Baseline conformance was run before the attempt and passed: 425 upstream Ninja tests across 33 suites, 10 differential CLI cases, and the log/deps persistence round trip. After reverting, 366 tests pass and the instruction count returns exactly to 22,776,535."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:51:20.10319646Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-clean-tree-fixture
                title "Benchmark the up-to-date tree the suite never measured"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:13:51.213615417Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:19:13.394079274Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Added, and it immediately found three things the existing suite structurally could not see. The gap it closes: every prior workload leaves the graph fully dirty. The phony fixtures name outputs that never exist, so missing_without_inputs holds for all of them, which means the suite measured parsing and planning and never once measured the shape a build tool spends most of its life in — a re-scan of an up-to-date tree. clean_tree_sources writes 2,000 real source files and a copy rule; the harness primes with one real build using the tool under measurement, exactly as dependency-log-load already does, so outputs exist, are newer than their inputs, and a real .ninja_log records each command. The whole graph then evaluates clean. First finding, and the important one: the clean-tree no-op is 62% system time — user 3.953 ms against sys 6.402 ms per run, with 4,001 newfstatat calls, one per node, issued serially through the FnMut stat closure during the dirty traversal. Every optimisation in this programme moved user-space instructions, which is 38% of this workload. Second: core::str::converts::from_utf8 is back at 772,813 instructions, 2.78%, across 8,000 calls from log::BuildLog::open — the same idiom removed from the scanner under ronin-byte-keyed-names, alive in the build-log reader, invisible until now because no other fixture has a .ninja_log. The log subsystem overall costs about 11% here (read_line 3.60%, nextfield 2.71%, open 1.52%, hydrate_runtime 0.60%, plus the from_utf8 it causes) and had never been measured at all. Third: allocation traffic is 9.1 requests per build statement against wide-noop's 2.1. Wall time against C samurai, interleaved minimum-of-9 with one warmup: ronin 10.870 ms, samurai 9.401 ms, ninja 29.729 ms — 1.16x, second-worst of the seven workloads and materially worse than the 0.87x on command evaluation this programme optimised. Peak RSS 5,236 KiB against samurai's 3,276. WORKLOAD_VERSION deliberately stays at 1: the guard covers changing an existing shape, every prior shape is byte-identical, and the CSVs are keyed by workload name, so all historical rows remain comparable and only a row is appended. The recorded ratios in baseline-v1.csv for the other six workloads are also left untouched so the improvement against the 2026-07-29 reference stays visible."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:21:49.339271332Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Validation artifact refreshed as benchmarks/performance-validation-2026-07-31.csv, generated from the committed tree so ronin_dirty=false holds, and the gate test now points at it and covers all seven workloads including clean-tree-noop. Appending to the 2026-07-29 artifact was rejected: it would have dated a measurement taken today as having been taken then. The refreshed sweep also corrects numbers that had gone badly stale — command evaluation reads 8.280 ms against the 36.954 ms recorded in baseline-v1.csv. The recorded baseline rows themselves are left untouched, since validate() compares the current Ronin-to-Ninja ratio against the recorded one and improvement shows as headroom; only the clean-tree-noop row is appended. Second measurement of the new workload on a quieter host: ronin 10.338 ms, samurai 9.753 ms, ninja 30.143 ms, so 1.06x rather than the 1.16x first observed — the ratio is host-sensitive and should be read as \"roughly at parity with C samurai, and clearly worse than the 0.87x we reach on the workloads this programme optimised\". scripts/check-performance.sh exits 0 with the new workload in the catalog. A test was added for the generator rather than bypassing the test-footprint code-quality gate, which fired because the change added harness code with no coverage: clean_tree_sources_are_buildable_and_complete pins the property the fixture depends on, namely that every edge names an input and produces one output, so a generator regression cannot silently turn the clean scan back into the dirty scan every other workload already measures."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:22:09.675904991Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:40:16.884860281Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @errata
                        text "d596481 shipped a broken example test and two gaps let it through. examples/baseline.rs's validation_rejects_runtime_and_memory_regressions has three cases; the second raises Ronin to 1.21x Ninja and asserts the absolute runtime check fires, which validate() only reaches after the recorded-ratio check passes. Both can hold at once only for a workload whose recorded ronin-to-ninja ratio exceeds one. The test selected its subject as \"the first ronin record\", which is the first key of a BTreeMap and therefore alphabetical, and adding clean-tree-noop moved that from deep-graph-evaluation — recorded ratio 16.47, comfortably loose — to clean-tree-noop at 0.37, so the assertion silently retargeted onto the ratio check and failed. Fixed by selecting the workload with the loosest recorded ratio explicitly, with a comment recording why, so a future catalog addition cannot retarget it again. On how it escaped: the nplan commit for d596481 returned an empty checks array, meaning the platform checks were witness-cached from an earlier run and never re-executed against the staged delta, so the gate did not see the failure. And every test summary in that session was read through grep 'test result: ok. 3|FAILED' piped to head -2, which matched the lib total and a literal \"FAILED: [code=130] output\" line inside a cli test's captured stdout, then truncated before the example target's genuine \"test result: FAILED\" line. The lesson is the reading, not the tooling: check the exit status of the whole run rather than pattern-matching a prefix of its output."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-drop-port-scaffolding
        title "Delete code retained only for C port fidelity"
        kind @refactor
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-30T14:21:18.405020142Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @decision
                text "Port fidelity is no longer a constraint on the Rust source (owner direction, 2026-07-30): Ronin is judged on Ninja behavioural compatibility, not on retaining a symbol per C function. Symbols kept alive only to satisfy a port rule are deleted along with the rules that covered them. Behavioural compatibility rules under docs/spec/ronin/ and the pinned Ninja conformance oracle are unaffected and remain the arbiter. This retires the dead_code allowances on env::merge and env::pathlist and the trivial-function bypass taken on graph::nodepath in c2d6b7e, all three of which existed solely for port fidelity."
