    {
        id ronin-windows-launcher
        title "Make the Windows build compile and launch as Ninja does"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:27:18.600512368Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:27:39.542985614Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The Windows build did not compile. That is the finding; the launcher backend it was opened for is the smaller half of it.\n\nThe node was to give Windows a direct-spawn backend, since `shell_command` reached for `/bin/sh` on every platform. Before writing one I installed the `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` target to type check what is there — and it does not build. Two errors, one of them mine: `spawn` on the non-Unix path still called `shell_command` with the two arguments it took before the command launcher gave it a third, so that work broke the Windows build the day it landed and nothing said so. The other was older: the parallel stat warms a shared directory descriptor that only exists on Unix.\n\nThat is worth more than the backend. A platform with no host to run on and no check to compile for it does not degrade loudly, it degrades silently, and the answer is not care but a check. `scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh` now type checks the Windows target when it is installed, and says out loud when it is skipping because it is not. It takes seconds and it says nothing whatsoever about behaviour — only that the code still compiles, which is the bar that was being missed.\n\nOn the launcher itself, the interesting part is what Windows must *not* inherit. Ninja on Windows has no shell in this position at all: it hands the whole command line to `CreateProcess` with no program argument and lets Windows find the program in it. So `--compat`, which on Unix means \"always spawn a shell\", has nothing to fall back to and asks for what already happens. More sharply, the POSIX word splitting the Unix direct path uses would be actively wrong there — it splits on spaces, and would cut `\"C:\\Program Files\\x\\cl.exe\" /c a.c` in half at the space inside the quoted path, producing a program named `\"C:\\Program`. Applying the Unix rule to Windows would have been worse than the `/bin/sh` it replaced, because it would fail quietly on exactly the paths Windows users have.\n\nRust's `Command` insists on a program separate from its arguments, so the split Windows would have done is done here instead: a quoted program ends at its closing quote, an unquoted one at the first space, an unterminated quote runs to the end of the line. That rule is a pure function of the bytes, so it is compiled and tested on every platform rather than only where it runs — five cases including the quoted path that motivates it, passing here on Linux.\n\nWhat is emphatically not verified: any of this actually spawning anything. There is no Windows machine and no CI, so the backend is type checked and its one piece of real logic is unit tested, and nothing more can honestly be claimed. Anyone with a Windows host should run the differential corpus there before trusting it. The `--shell` seam remains how a shell that runs on Windows gets used, which is where Brendan's dash port lands.\n\n409 unit tests, 17 integration tests, clippy clean on the host target, the Windows target compiles, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 32 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:15:43.454789883Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-reference-in-checkout
        title "Keep the comparison references where a reboot cannot delete them"
        kind @test
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:43:21.953539012Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-03T23:43:39.590827587Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The comparison references now live in `reference/` in the checkout instead of `/tmp`, and a missing one fails the run instead of shrinking it.\n\n`/tmp` is tmpfs here, so every reboot destroyed the pinned Ninja checkout, the Ninja build, the C samurai binary and both real-project build trees — 140 MB that takes a clone and two builds to replace. That had already cost a gate run this session. Moving them is the obvious half.\n\nThe half worth recording is what the move exposed. `baseline` defaulted its samurai path with `.exists().then_some(…)`, so when the binary was gone the comparison did not fail — it dropped to two tools, printed a perfectly well-formed table, and exited zero. Every row in it was true. What was false was the thing a reader takes from a three-way comparison table, which is that three things were compared. That is the same shape as the Windows build compiling nowhere and the dry-run log check asserting that a no-op is a no-op: not a wrong answer, an answer to a question nobody asked, wearing the clothes of the one they did.\n\nThe reference is now demanded rather than detected. `tool()` already refused a path that does not exist, so making the default unconditional was enough: a missing binary now prints `samurai-c binary does not exist: …` and exits 1. Verified by hiding the binary and checking both the message and the status. `--without-samurai` remains, so a two-way comparison is still available — as something asked for, not as something that happens.\n\n`conformance`, `check-release.sh`, the README and the benchmarks recipe all point at `reference/` now, which means both gates run with no arguments at all; the recipe in `benchmarks/README.md` builds everything into place. The directory is gitignored — it holds a Ninja checkout with its own `.git`, a 5 MB build tree and two CMake project trees, none of which belong in history.\n\nConformance green from the new location with no arguments: 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 32 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. A one-repetition baseline reports all three tools. 409 tests, clippy clean, fmt clean.\n\nStill unmeasured, unchanged by this: the command launcher's wall-clock effect, the supervisor-bookkeeping wall figure, the scheduler-barrier syscall itemisation at this revision, and the real-project three-way. Load has been between 11 and 40 all evening."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:15:45.167726092Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-reap-latency
        title "Stop sleeping ten milliseconds through every child's exit"
        kind @perf
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-04T12:15:08.371600823Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-04T12:15:42.116533152Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The supervisor slept ten milliseconds through the exit of nearly every child. Removing that is 22.7% of scheduler-barrier at -j8 and 93.1% of it at -j1.\n\nFound by finally taking the measurement this session owed. The first result was that the command launcher had made things *worse* — 128 stamp edges at -j1 took 1369 ms without a shell against 177 ms with one, a 7.7× regression from a change measured beforehand at 51% faster per spawn. Quoting the microbenchmark and shipping was wrong; the build-level number said the opposite.\n\nThe cause is not the launcher. When a child's output pipe reports end of file the supervisor tries a non-blocking `waitpid`, and if the child is not yet reapable it becomes a reap candidate and the next poll is given a fixed ten millisecond timeout. But a child that has closed its descriptors is already inside `do_exit`: it becomes waitable one scheduler hop later, on the order of tens of microseconds. The parent lost that race and then slept through the resolution, once per job.\n\nWhy it only appeared without a shell is the part worth keeping. Under `sh -c`, dash forks and waits, so two processes hold the pipe's write end and the parent is doing its non-blocking `waitpid` against a process that has already been sitting in the exit path while its own child was reaped. It won the race essentially every time. Executing the command directly removed that accidental delay, and the ten millisecond floor underneath it — which had been there the whole time — became the dominant cost. The launcher did not introduce this; it took away the thing that was hiding it.\n\nThe fix is to ask again quickly rather than to ask once, late. The interval starts at 50 microseconds — sized to the gap it is actually covering — and doubles to a ten millisecond ceiling, reset whenever a new candidate appears. A child that closes its output and keeps running is still only asked about occasionally, which is what the fixed interval was protecting; a child that is simply on its way out is collected almost immediately. Confirmed by bisecting the constant before writing anything: dropping ten milliseconds to one took the -j1 case from 1405 ms to 225 ms, which is the shape of a per-job timeout and not of anything else.\n\nMeasured, interleaved and randomised per repetition, minimum of 21, outputs and log removed between every run, on a quiet host (load 1.2–1.7 throughout, recorded at both ends):\n\n  scheduler-barrier -j8   before   44 ms   after   34 ms   1.29x  (-22.7%)\n  scheduler-barrier -j1  before 1396 ms   after   97 ms  14.39x  (-93.1%)\n\nThis reconciles something ronin-dispatch-residual-gap left open. That node recorded that Ronin's completion-to-dispatch window was *faster* than C samurai's — 1.501 ms against 1.654 ms — while its occupancy was somehow lower, and called the remaining ~2.2% unexplained. The window was measured from the completion being noticed, so it never contained the sleep that delayed noticing. The gap was in front of the thing being measured. In the catalog run just taken, scheduler-barrier is Ronin 38.090 ms against C samurai 38.262 ms and Ninja 45.691 ms — the workload Ronin had never won.\n\nWith that out of the way the launcher delivers what it promised, measured as one binary against itself so no compiler or layout difference can be in it — `--compat` forces a shell for every command, the default skips it where the shell provably has nothing to do:\n\n  128 stamps, -j1    shell 174 ms   direct  90 ms   1.93x  (-48.3%)\n  128 stamps, -j8    shell  36 ms   direct  34 ms   1.06x  (-5.6%)\n  1024 stamps, -j8   shell 272 ms   direct 262 ms   1.04x  (-3.7%)\n\nThe serial figure matches the 51% per-spawn measurement almost exactly, which is the right check on it. With eight jobs on a host with cores to spare the extra process overlaps with real work and most of the saving disappears — so the honest claim for the launcher is that it halves a serial stamp pipeline and is worth a few percent when the machine is not saturated, not that it is worth 51% of a build.\n\n409 tests, clippy clean, fmt clean, conformance green at 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 32 build-outcome cases, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks.\n\nPossible follow-up, not done: a `pidfd` registered with the poller would make this exact rather than approximate on Linux, at the cost of a path that does not exist on macOS. The backoff is portable and closes the gap; the remaining error is bounded by 50 microseconds per job."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:15:47.209022792Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id make-subsumption
        title "Subsume GNU Make into Ronin's build graph"
        kind @feat
        children (
            {
                id kati-submodule
                title "Vendor the kati fork as a submodule and build it in Ronin's workspace"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T14:37:45.39608841Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Scope of this node beyond adding the submodule, which is done.\n\nWorkspace: the root package gains a workspace with the submodule as a member, so one cargo invocation builds both and the release profile settings apply to the front end too.\n\nFork hygiene, all in our fork rather than carried as patches. criterion is in [dependencies] rather than [dev-dependencies], so plotters, rayon, clap, regex, jiff, half, and ciborium are pulled into release builds; moving it drops the tree from 74 crates toward Ronin's 19. env_logger goes, with diagnostics routed through Ronin's. The #![deny(warnings)] in lib.rs and main.rs goes, because on vendored code it turns every compiler upgrade into a build break. The jemalloc global allocator in main.rs goes; allocator choice belongs to Ronin. The rkati binary stays, because the differential harness needs the upstream-shaped tool to compare against.\n\nGovernance: kati/src-rs/** joins the nspec target-impl include list and the nplan rust platform file globs, so the vendored code is subject to the same spec coverage and quality gates as the rest of the tree rather than sitting in a blind spot. That is the point of vendoring it rather than depending on it.\n\nToolchain: it compiles clean as-is on rustc 1.97.1, a 30 second cold check. Edition 2024 with let-chains is fine while it stays a separate crate; it is a problem only if anyone tries to merge the module tree into the edition 2021 root package, which is not the plan."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T15:40:13.06282916Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the hygiene list above. The reason given for dropping jemalloc was wrong: a #[global_allocator] in a binary crate applies only to that binary, and Ronin links the library rather than rkati's main, so nothing about the vendored binary's allocator reaches Ronin's.\n\nIt still belongs on the list, for a different and smaller reason. tikv-jemallocator is a normal target-conditional dependency, not a dev one, so cargo builds it whether or not any linked target installs it: cargo check --lib on the fork compiles tikv-jemalloc-sys and its bundled C sources. That is build cost with no consumer once main.rs is out of the picture, which puts it in the same bucket as the misplaced criterion dependency rather than in the correctness bucket. Confine both to the rkati binary via optional dependencies or a feature so the library builds neither."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T16:00:36.465711862Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Wiring the workspace surfaced a constraint stronger than the hygiene note above, and it supersedes the advice there to move criterion to dev-dependencies.\n\n.cargo/config.toml applies -C target-feature=+crt-static to cfg(all(target_os = \"linux\", target_env = \"gnu\")). That cfg matches proc-macro crates as well as the final binary, and a proc-macro must be a dylib, so any proc-macro anywhere in the workspace fails to build: \"cannot produce proc-macro for serde_derive as the target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu does not support these crate types\". Ronin never hit this because its nineteen dependencies include no proc-macro. The front end pulls serde_derive through criterion, so it hits immediately.\n\nThe static link is not negotiable. The config comment records it as load-bearing for startup, measured: a no-op goes 1.37 ms to 0.98 against C samurai's 0.96, and the dependency-log fixture 3.18 to 2.58 against 2.62, which is behind-to-ahead on a workload whose deficit was startup. Removing it to accommodate a benchmark harness would be the wrong way round.\n\nSo the constraint on the fork is: no proc-macro dependencies at all, in normal or dev dependencies. Dev is not a safe harbour, because cargo test --workspace builds dev dependencies for every member and would fail the same way. Moving criterion to dev-dependencies, which the earlier note recommended, does not work.\n\nApplied for now: criterion dropped from dependencies and autobenches set false, so benches/strutil_benchmark.rs is retained but not built. cargo check --workspace --all-targets is clean. Whether that bench gets deleted, ported to Ronin's own performance harness, or left dormant is open — Ronin already has a performance gate and does not need a second one.\n\nThe escape hatch, if a proc-macro ever becomes genuinely necessary, is setting build.target so cargo separates host from target flags. It is not free: it moves every artifact under target/<triple>/, and the harnesses and scripts that reference target/release/ronin would all need updating."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:31:23.039146987Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
