            {
                id kati-global-state
                title "Replace kati's process globals with an explicit evaluation session"
                deps (kati-submodule)
                children (
                    {
                        id kati-symtab-split
                        title "Separate symbol interning from Make's global variable scope"
                        implements {
                            target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.scope-separation])
                        }
                    }

                    {
                        id kati-session-value
                        title "Thread an owned session through Make evaluation"
                        implements {
                            target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.no-ambient-state])
                        }
                        deps (kati-symtab-split kati-scoped-binding)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T16:43:19.858259904Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Findings from the split that land on this node.\n\nThe obstacle is Drop, not the call sites. ScopedGlobalVar restores its binding in Drop, which cannot take a session parameter, and it is used by foreach, foreach_sep, and call for the automatic positional variables, so it sits on the hot evaluation path. Once the scope is session-owned it must either borrow the session — poisoning every &mut Evaluator borrow across loop bodies that re-enter evaluation — or be replaced by explicit save and restore around the closure, something shaped like globals.with_bound(sym, var, |globals| ...). GlobalVars::replace was deliberately shaped to make that rewrite cheap. ScopedVar in the same file looks identical but is not affected, because it holds an Arc<Vars> rather than reaching for a global.\n\nThe variable-scope surface is smaller than expected: 16 call sites, and every one already has an &mut Evaluator in scope or is in main, so threading a session to them is mechanical. The roughly 120 context-free reads the decision worries about are almost all Symbol::as_bytes, Display, and Debug, which belong to kati-symbol-display, and they now funnel through a single with_symtab entry point rather than scattered lock sites.\n\nUSED_ENV_VARS and USED_UNDEFINED_VARS are read from get_global_var and lookup_var_global, so they want to move in this change rather than after it.\n\nOne latent hazard to remove rather than inherit. GLOBAL_VARS is a LazyLock whose initializer calls with_symtab, so it takes the interner lock while constructing the scope. Nothing currently reaches a global variable from inside a with_symtab closure, so it is unreachable today, but a thread holding the interner that first touched the scope would deadlock on a non-reentrant lock. The comment on global_var_names saying nothing takes both locks is accurate for the steady state and not for that initializer. Session ownership removes both the hazard and the comment.\n\nFor contrast, the split already removed a real one: the old Symtab::set_global_var formatted a symbol through Display while holding the interner lock it would need to re-take, reachable only on the readonly-error path, which every caller happened to avoid by passing a readonly out-parameter and raising the diagnostic outside the lock."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T18:27:23.788765808Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Strike the shape this node's earlier note proposed. It said the replacement for the Drop guard would look like globals.with_bound(sym, var, |globals| ...). That shape does not work, and the reason generalises to the whole session design rather than being local to the guard.\n\nThe closure body re-enters evaluation, and evaluation reaches for the variable scope on its own account through peek_global_var, set_global_var, and lookup_var. Passing the scope into the closure means holding it across the body. Today that is a parking_lot guard on a non-reentrant lock, so the first variable read inside any foreach body deadlocks. After session ownership it becomes a &mut borrow of the session held across a body that itself needs &mut Evaluator, which is exactly the borrow poisoning this node was already worried about. The shape that survives is scope-in-the-save-and-the-restore, never scope-in-the-closure, and that is what kati-scoped-binding shipped.\n\nThe consequence for this node is a constraint, not just a correction. Any session-owned entry point of this kind belongs on Evaluator rather than on the scope — Evaluator::with_bound(&mut self, ..., f: impl FnOnce(&mut Self) -> ...) reborrows self into the closure legally, where handing out a &mut to a field does not. Expect that to be the general pattern wherever session state has to be temporarily modified around re-entrant evaluation, not a one-off for positional variables.\n\nOne behavioural difference to keep in mind rather than fix: Drop restored the binding when the body panicked, and the closure form does not. That is deliberate. Evaluation signals failure with anyhow errors rather than panics, and a panic through evaluation ends the process, so there is no observer. If the session ever becomes recoverable across a panic boundary, revisit it.\n\nThe harness situation also improved. func.rs now has a test module that constructs an Evaluator and evaluates parsed Make text in three lines, so this node can assert session state before and after an evaluation including on the error path, which nothing could reach before."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id kati-symbol-display
                        title "Give symbol and location rendering an explicit session"
                        deps (kati-symtab-split)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T15:54:47.962863942Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Mechanism settled: a symbol stays Copy and stays an index into a session-owned interner. Rendering goes through a borrowing wrapper from an inherent display method taking the session, and the inherent Display and Debug that reach for a global are removed rather than reimplemented. Making a symbol own its bytes was rejected because it costs Copy; recorded as an alternative on the decision.\n\nTwo consequences worth separating before starting, because they are not the same problem.\n\nLoc's Display at loc.rs:27 is on the user-facing diagnostic path, not a debug path. error_loc!, warn_loc!, color_error_log, and color_warn_log all format a location into the message a user reads, and Loc holds a Symbol filename, so the session has to be reachable wherever a diagnostic is constructed. That is the constraint that shapes where the session lives, and it is a stronger one than the trace sites imply. loc.rs:33 DEFAULT_FILENAME disappears with it.\n\nDebug is the softer half. Removing Debug for Symbol silently degrades every derived Debug on a symbol-bearing type — dep.rs:46 DepNode, loc.rs:21 Loc, and the statement and expression types in stmt.rs and expr.rs — to printing an index. There are 66 format sites using it and most are behind the log! macro, which is log::trace! gated on KATI_LOG. Either keep a Debug that prints the raw index and accept that trace output loses symbol names, or convert those sites to session-carrying rendering. Prefer the first unless the traces turn out to be load-bearing during the port; it is honest about what a bare handle can say for itself, and it does not spend the session's reach on trace logging.\n\nOnly Loc and Symbol are affected. The third Display in the crate, Stats at stats.rs:155, does not touch the interner."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-05T06:40:55.551895302Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

