                    {
                        id kati-scoped-binding
                        title "Replace the scope-restoring Drop guard with explicit save and restore"
                        deps (kati-symtab-split)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T17:58:32.362626463Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Split out of kati-session-value because it is the part of it that is a design change rather than a threading exercise, and it blocks the rest.\n\nScopedGlobalVar restores a global variable binding in Drop. Drop takes only &mut self, so it cannot be handed a session, which means it cannot survive session ownership in its current shape. It is not a corner: foreach, foreach_sep, and call use it for the automatic positional variables, so it is on the hot evaluation path and its loop bodies re-enter evaluation.\n\nThe two candidate shapes. Borrow the session in the guard, which poisons every &mut Evaluator borrow for the lifetime of the guard and therefore across the loop bodies that need the evaluator — likely a non-starter, but establish that rather than assume it. Or replace the guard with an explicit save and restore around the closure, shaped like globals.with_bound(sym, var, |globals| ...), which is why GlobalVars::replace already returns the previous binding: kati-symtab-split shaped it for exactly this rewrite.\n\nNote that the guard's restore must run on the error path too, since Make evaluation propagates errors through these functions. A closure form has to be careful not to lose that property, which is the one thing Drop was genuinely buying.\n\nScopedVar in the same file looks identical and is not affected: it holds an Arc<Vars> rather than reaching for a global, so it needs nothing from a session."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-05T06:40:47.46953552Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id kati-wellknown-symbols
                        title "Intern the well-known symbols into the session instead of into statics"
                        deps (kati-session-value)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T18:10:14.208535179Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Seven statics, all LazyLock<Symbol> interned from the global interner on first use: SHELL_SYM, ALLOW_RULES_SYM, KATI_READONLY_SYM, VARIABLES_SYM, KATI_SYMBOLS_SYM and MAKEFILE_LIST in symtab.rs, and DEFAULT_FILENAME in loc.rs.\n\nThese are not globals to be relocated, which is why they get their own node rather than a line in the session work. A symbol is an index into one specific interner. The moment the interner is session-owned, a symbol interned into a static is an index into whichever session happened to touch it first, and using it against any other session is either a wrong answer or a panic — Symtab::name indexes directly. So the static form cannot survive in any shape, however the session is threaded.\n\nThe replacement is to intern them during session construction and hold them on the session, which is cheap and gives every one of them a defined interner. Symtab::new already preloads the 255 single-byte names, so a well-known set alongside them is the same idea one level up.\n\nTwo things to watch. DEFAULT_FILENAME is used by Loc's Default, so whatever holds it has to be reachable from wherever a default location is constructed, which is likely to be the same constraint kati-symbol-display hits with Loc's Display on the diagnostic path. And these six symbol statics are pub, so anything outside the crate that names them is API surface — check the binary and find_emulator before assuming they are internal."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-05T06:40:57.496333718Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
