                    {
                        id kati-no-globals-gate
                        title "Fail the build when a mutable process global reappears in the fork"
                        deps (
                            kati-session-value
                            kati-symbol-display
                            kati-flags-value
                            kati-caches-session
                            kati-observation-session
                            kati-wellknown-symbols
                        )
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-04T18:09:49.332692094Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The decision says no mutable process globals survive. Removing them once is a cleanup; keeping them gone is a gate, and without one the property decays the first time someone reaches for a convenient lazy static.\n\nWhat this has to catch is a static holding mutable or lazily-initialized state anywhere under kati/src-rs, including inside macro bodies — the statistics macros each expand to a static per call site, which is exactly the shape that slips past a reviewer reading the macro definition rather than its expansions. It must not catch const items or genuinely read-only tables such as the builtin function dispatch map, which the decision explicitly permits.\n\nA source scan is the cheap and honest implementation and can live with the differential harnesses. Rust has no lint for this, and the alternative of a trait-based ban is more machinery than the problem deserves. Whatever form it takes, it should report the offending file and line and say which node was supposed to have removed it, so a regression points at its own history.\n\nNote the ordering constraint this creates: the gate cannot be switched on until every removal node is done, which is why it depends on all of them. Landing it earlier in report-only mode is worthwhile — it gives a running count that should fall monotonically, and a count that rises is a signal worth having during the work rather than after it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-05T06:41:25.463942244Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T14:37:14.750443341Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Inventory of the mutable process globals to remove, taken from the submodule at the vendored commit.\n\nsymtab.rs:33 SYMTAB, the interner and Make's global variable table in one structure. symtab.rs:35-40 six symbols derived from it: SHELL, .KATI_ALLOW_RULES, .KATI_READONLY, .VARIABLES, .KATI_SYMBOLS, MAKEFILE_LIST. loc.rs:33 DEFAULT_FILENAME, also derived. flags.rs:32 FLAGS, lazily initialized from the process command line. var.rs:421 SHELL_STATUS. var.rs:427 USED_ENV_VARS. eval.rs:239 USED_UNDEFINED_VARS. fileutil.rs:114 GLOB_CACHE. file_cache.rs:28 CACHE. find.rs:41 NODE_COUNT and find.rs:1352 FIND_EMULATOR. func.rs:602 COMMAND_RESULTS. stats.rs:30 ALL_STATS plus a per-call-site static in each of the two stats macros. main.rs:62 installs jemalloc as the global allocator, which is a Ronin-level choice and collides with the open allocator node.\n\nfunc.rs:1421 FUNC_INFO_MAP is a read-only dispatch table built once from a const array. It is not in scope for removal; it is a lookup table, not ambient state.\n\nSizing for the interner, which is the one that sets the shape of the rest: Symbol is a NonZeroUsize index, and Display, Debug, and as_bytes all take the global lock, so none of them can be given a session by threading a parameter through their signatures. There are roughly 119 as_bytes call sites and 81 intern call sites. The display implementations need explicit session-carrying forms, which is why kati-symbol-display is separated out and blocked on the split rather than folded into it.\n\nThe reason the split comes first is that the symbol table is two things: interning a name and defining a global variable are the same call, via get_global_var and set_global_var hanging off Symbol. Until those separate, the interner cannot be owned by anything, because owning it would mean owning Make's variable scope with it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T15:39:57.391976508Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the inventory above: the jemalloc global allocator at main.rs:62 is not in scope and was never a conflict. A #[global_allocator] declared in a binary crate applies to that binary alone, and Ronin links the library, not rkati's main. Ronin's allocator choice is unaffected whatever the vendored binary does with its own.\n\nThe inventory therefore stands at twelve mutable globals rather than twelve plus an allocator, and the removal work is unchanged."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T18:09:36.596462302Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the inventory on this node: twelve was a count of line items, not of statics, and it made the job look smaller than it is.\n\nCounted individually the fork started with twenty mutable process globals, not twelve. The line reading \"six symbols derived from it\" was six statics, and the two statistics statics inside the stats macros were one line item and two more statics. kati-symtab-split then added GLOBAL_VARS, which is a legitimate intermediate — that node's scope was separability rather than removal, and the variable scope had to land somewhere once it stopped living inside the interner — but it means the count is currently twenty-one and has gone up, not down.\n\nOwnership was also incomplete. Seven had no node at all: the six well-known symbol statics and the default location symbol, all LazyLock<Symbol> derived from the global interner. They are not merely globals to relocate. A symbol is an index into one specific interner, so once the interner is session-owned these cannot exist in any form and have to become symbols interned during session construction. That is now kati-wellknown-symbols. SHELL_STATUS had no explicit owner either; it is the value behind .SHELLSTATUS and belongs with the observation state in kati-observation-session.\n\nThe larger miss is that nothing made the end state stick. make.no-ambient-state is verified by two sessions coexisting in one process, which is the right property to test, but it would not notice a twenty-second global appearing beside them. kati-no-globals-gate closes that and depends on every removal node, so it cannot pass until all of them are done."
                    }
                )
            }

