            {
                id kati-graph-sink
                title "Build Ronin's graph directly from Make dependency nodes"
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.graph-direct])
                }
                deps (
                    ronin-frontend-api
                    ronin-execute-api
                    kati-build-sink
                    kati-command-escapes
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T14:37:29.490746695Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The field correspondence this node depends on, checked against the vendored commit and the current graph.\n\nkati dep.rs:47 DepNode carries output, implicit_outputs, actual_inputs, actual_order_only_inputs, actual_validations, is_phony, is_restat, depfile_var, ninja_pool_var, cmds, rule_vars, output_pattern, loc. Ronin graph.rs:64 Edge carries out with an explicit-output count, input with explicit and non-order-only partitions, validation, pool, dyndep, rule, and bindings. Every field on the left has a destination on the right; nothing needs inventing.\n\nThe work is therefore not translation, it is redirection. ninja.rs:460 emit_node and ninja.rs:541 emit_build already compute everything an edge needs and then write it out as text. The command construction that feeds them stays where it is: gen_shell_script at ninja.rs:382, translate_command at ninja.rs:267, get_depfile at ninja.rs:431, the 100 kB response-file threshold, and escape_shell are Make concerns and do not belong in graph construction.\n\nTwo shape differences to settle when implementing rather than to discover later. kati emits a synthetic _kati_always_build_ input for phony targets when use_ninja_phony_output is off, which exists to force rebuilds through a manifest and has no reason to exist when the graph is built directly. And escape_ninja / escape_build_target only matter for serialization, so the direct path must take the unescaped symbol bytes, not the escaped ones. Both are places where the emitted manifest and the direct graph could diverge for reasons that are correct rather than defective, so make-graph-equivalence needs to be able to express them as classified differences rather than failures."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T06:57:42.983827002Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Corrections to this node's earlier field correspondence, from closing kati-build-sink.\n\nRead the wrong side of the graph and the equivalence check will compare two things that match by coincidence. The earlier note maps DepNode's actual_inputs, actual_order_only_inputs and actual_validations onto Ronin's inputs. The emitter does not read those. It reads deps, order_onlys and validations, and only that side is populated by the plan walk. They agree today because dep.rs pushes them one for one in order, but a graph sink should read the side the emitter reads.\n\ntags_var was missing from the correspondence entirely, and so were sandbox_disabled and phony_output. .KATI_TAGS is emitted per edge, has no counterpart in Ronin's Edge beyond a generic binding, and nothing consumes it. Decide explicitly whether these are dropped or carried as bindings rather than discovering them absent later.\n\nThe sink driver is still private. BuildSink, NinjaWriter and NinjaWriterOptions are public, but NinjaGenerator::emit is not, so an out-of-crate front end cannot supply its own sink yet. One signature, and it belongs here because this node knows what shape it wants.\n\nTwo cosmetic things that will look like defects and are not. Special targets consume a rule id before being filtered, so rule numbering has gaps. Pool names are written unescaped while targets are escaped.\n\nOne genuinely dead branch, worth not faithfully reproducing: the \"Environment variables used\" comment block has never been emitted, in the C++ original or this port, because the map it reads is filled after the comment that reads it. NinjaWriter does not reproduce it and the fill site records why."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:48:57.361173783Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Crate direction, which this node needs settled before any code can be placed: Ronin depends on the fork, behind an optional feature. The fork does not depend on Ronin.\n\nIt is forced rather than chosen. multicall-identity says one binary selects its front end from the invoked name and that Ronin owns the product identity, so Ronin's binary has to be able to run Make mode. That makes Ronin the depender, and the BuildSink implementation therefore lives in Ronin — it is the only side that can see both kati's trait and Ronin's graph API. The fork stays unaware of Ronin, so there is no cycle and no risk of the vendored tree acquiring a dependency on us that complicates tracking upstream.\n\nAn optional feature keeps the library honest: someone using Ronin as a Ninja library should not compile a Make evaluator, and the feature is what lets the binary turn it on without imposing it. Default it on for the binary.\n\nThe alternative considered was a third crate depending on both, leaving Ronin's library free of the fork entirely. It is cleaner layering on paper and loses on practicalities: it moves the binary out of the Ronin crate, which is the thing users install and the thing product.ronin-identity is written about, and it buys separation that the optional feature already provides.\n\nConsequence to watch for whoever implements this: the fork's Cargo.toml declares Apache-2.0 and Ronin's does not, and a dependency does not change a dependent's licence, so nothing about this touches make-licence-position. It does mean publishing Ronin to a registry would require the fork to be published too, which is a packaging question and not one this node has to answer."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-graph-equivalence
                title "Check the direct graph against the emitted manifest over the corpus"
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.manifest-equivalence])
                }
                deps (kati-graph-sink)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T09:50:27.066753493Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "This node built almost nothing, which was the right outcome rather than a shortcut.\n\nkati-graph-sink established the property and left the machinery behind: a tee sink driving both the graph and the writer from one evaluation, a total edge description, the corpus driver, and ten always-on cases covering what the corpus cannot reach. Rebuilding any of that to satisfy a node boundary would have produced a second oracle to keep in agreement with the first.\n\nSo this node supplied the two things that were actually missing. The rule now has an implementation site on the comparison itself and a verification site on the corpus test, where before it was deliberately unannotated — the sink's agent declined to claim make.manifest-equivalence because its own node did not own it, which was the correct call and left exactly this gap. And there is a script, because the corpus test is #[ignore]d: it changes the working directory, which would break the lib tests running beside it, so it has to run alone and a bare cargo test will silently skip it. A property nobody can invoke is not a gate.\n\nResult at the time of writing: 296 makefiles, 268 graphs identical, one case where both paths refuse, 85 the front end itself rejects, one classified disagreement.\n\nThe guard on the script checks the corpus is non-empty rather than probing for a named file. A submodule that is present but unchecked out otherwise reports as a clean pass over zero makefiles, which is the shape of failure this project has been bitten by before with its reference binaries.\n\nWorth knowing for whoever changes the sink next: make-phony-always-dirty will move a classification here rather than simply passing, because the manifest side carries the always-build property as a synthetic input while the direct side will carry it structurally."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-mode-cli
                title "Select the Make compiler front end and translate its command line"
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:product.make-identity])
                }
                deps (make-interface-surface)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T19:36:51.697008623Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "I reported that Make mode defaults to parallel and flagged it as a divergence from GNU Make. That was wrong, and the correction is worth recording because the code reads that way at a glance.\n\nbuild_options sets `jobs: invocation.jobs.unwrap_or(JobLimit::Auto)`, and Auto is Ninja's guess at the machine. Reading only that line says Make mode runs as many recipes at once as it has cores. It does not: Auto here means nothing was asked for, and normalize_runtime_options at cli.rs:869 resolves it — to the parent's jobserver if the environment names an inheritable one, and otherwise to the `unshared` argument, which the Make call site passes as one job with a comment saying why.\n\nMeasured against GNU Make 4.4.1 on three recipes with descending sleeps, directly and through `$(MAKE)`: serial 0.625s to Make's 0.639s, `-j4` 0.315s to Make's 0.313s, same completion order in both. The behaviour was already right, including the inheritance.\n\nPinned now by make_mode_runs_one_recipe_at_a_time_unless_asked_otherwise, which tells the two schedules apart by completion order rather than by duration, and asserts both halves — one job by default, and parallel when asked. It was only correct via a two-step path with no test on it.\n\nWhat actually races in targets/WAIT is the suite passing `-j10` itself. That is a real gap and it is make-wait-prerequisite, not a defaulting question."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-conformance-corpus
                title "Use the GNU Make corpus to discover compiler gaps"
                kind @test
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:04:45.746413879Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:13:44.928672929Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Reframed GNU Make's exact-output suite as discovery input, never a runner-conformance score. The classifier now separates 614 unclassified residues, 74 compiler-shaped candidates, 112 interface observations, and 549 deliberate narration differences across 1,349 cases. A candidate becomes a compatibility defect only through a focused build-intent reproducer comparing graph shape, selected work, normal outcome, or filesystem effects. The inventory and harness are pinned to GNU Make 4.4.1 commit d66a65ad5a0e31b287f53930b0f09e31801f1613."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:24:35.610185559Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The parked inventory drift was not a reclassification. Six of the nine moved corpus cases were a regression, and the corpus is what found it.\n\nWhat the two earlier commits handed over. `check-make-conformance.sh` reported four unclassified differences (include.mk#test1, include_glob.mk#test1, include_var.mk#test1, makefile_list.mk#test4), three recorded cases that had changed shape (makefile_list.mk#test1..3), err_include.mk#default changed as well, and the intended `.SHELLSTATUS` corpus-shape change. Each was checked against GNU Make 4.4.1 by running the same makefile under `/usr/bin/make` rather than by reading the classifier.\n\nEvery one of the include cases was GNU Make succeeding and the front end failing. `make test1` on include.mk exits 0 under GNU Make and exited 2 under the front end with `*** No rule to make target 'missing.d'.  Stop.` — a Makefile with `-include foo.d missing.d`, which is the commonest idiom there is. Generated includes (kati 00c9f36) had added the missing includes to `targets` inside `DepBuilder::build`, so they became goals, and a goal with no rule is fatal in the executor. GNU Make forgets an included file it cannot remake; it does not die on it.\n\nFixed in kati (93d72e3), not classified away. `make_dep` now returns goals and regeneration roots as two lists. `plan_regeneration` runs before default-goal selection, because GNU Make remakes an included Makefile before it picks a goal, and keeps only the roots that have a rule: an optional one with no rule is dropped, and a required one is reported the way GNU Make reports it — the read that failed, then `*** No rule to make target 'X'.` — instead of being lost behind `*** No targets.` The frontend still emits regeneration roots into the graph; `admit_regeneration_roots` in src/make.rs adds them after the goals so nothing displaces the default target, and src/make/equivalence.rs hands both paths the same roots.\n\nOne case genuinely was a reclassification and it turned out to be a second regression. autovar_assign.mk#default stopped printing anything and started refusing the Makefile with `overriding automatic variable is not implemented yet` — kati e4c114c had made `+=` copy before assigning, which is right for precedence and wrong over a `foreach` binding, where GNU Make appends through it into the file scope. Appending in place is what it did before the copy existed and it is the closer of the two, since GNU Make does not refuse. The corpus row is unchanged; the case is still `recorded`, as the annotation at the head of the corpus file says.\n\nWhere the inventory landed: 90 rows to 89. `err_include.mk#default` is gone because it now matches GNU Make byte for byte, and took the `io-error-text` family with it, being the only case that named it. `shellstatus_in_rule.mk#test` keeps `extension`/`kati-extension` with a new digest, which is the intended `.SHELLSTATUS`-in-a-rule change. Nothing else moved: the four unclassified differences and the three shape changes are not reclassified, they no longer exist. Classes are now defect 43, recorded 14, extension 31, artefact 1, and 297 of 386 runs are identical after normalisation, up from 292.\n\nThree build-intent cases pin the fixed behaviour — `feature-optional-include-glob-matches-nothing`, `feature-required-include-with-no-rule-is-refused`, `feature-generated-include-is-not-the-goal` — all recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1. Worth saying that none of them would have caught the defect: Ronin builds only the goals it was asked for, so the extra roots never reached its scheduler, and the whole divergence was visible in rkati's executor and nowhere else. The corpus reached a contract the build-intent gate structurally cannot see, which is the argument for keeping both.\n\nThe upstream inventory also drifted, from 1349 rows to 1351, and that drift is entirely someone else's: the same run against a binary built at main reproduces it row for row, so the four commits of 2026-08-09 moved it and this node's kati change moves nothing in it. Re-recorded rather than left red. The largest single movement is 38 `features/include` rows losing `io-error-text`, because generated includes replaced the Rust io::Error text with GNU Make's wording; the rest is `ninja-progress` giving way to `no-work-line` as fewer edges have work to do. Classes are now unclassified 621, narration 541, interface 116, compiler 73.\n\nResidue triage, by the script that produced it rather than by family, because the family column is mostly narration accounting. variables/MAKEFLAGS is 129 of the 621 and is not evaluation at all — it is `makeflags-content` that the recogniser misses, since GNU Make's MAKEFLAGS opens with a dashless cluster (`rR -- hello:=world`, or bare `R`, or `-w`) and `is_flag_list` requires every word to start with `-` or hold an `=`. Sized and handed to make-makeflags-residue-classification; expect it to move about a hundred cases out of the residue and change no compiler behaviour.\n\nOf the rest: options/dash-f (31) is a Makefile on standard input, already `compiler`/`no-makefile-found` and correctly named. features/patternrules (33) and features/implicit_search (34) are rule search, and the sharpest instance is a refusal — two pattern rules that can each make `%.elf` produce `multiple rules generate a.elf` and nothing is built, which is also the diagnostic that stops `check-make-project.sh` on Ninja's own CMake Makefile today (that failure reproduces unchanged on main). features/targetvars (21) is an `=` arriving by expansion being read as an assignment operator; one shape silently skips the goal's recipe and exits 0, and the other panics the evaluator at kati/src-rs/eval.rs:814. Both are now nodes with reproducers: make-pattern-rule-output-overlap and make-target-specific-separator-is-literal, three cases carrying `divergence` markers.\n\nmisc/bs-nl (17) is backslash-newline collapsing inside quotes and is a real evaluator difference, unowned. functions/word (16), variables/define (15), functions/shell (14) are single-case residues with no shared shape, and are the tail this taxonomy was built to leave alone until something makes them worth a reproducer.\n\nGates: `check-make-conformance.sh` exits 0, `check-make-equivalence.sh` passes over 296 makefiles with its one classified disagreement, 286 build-intent cases, the workspace tests, format and release Clippy all pass. `check-make-project.sh` fails, identically before and after this change, and is recorded on make-pattern-rule-output-overlap rather than here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:24:49.379503739Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T19:05:49.279613653Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-licence-position
                title "Record the combined licence position for the vendored fork"
                deps (kati-submodule)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T14:37:56.111604409Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The intended position is Apache-2.0 OR MIT for Ronin's own work, with the accurate qualification that the vendored front end cannot be moved wholesale onto that dual licence.\n\nkati is Apache-2.0, copyright Google LLC. Apache-2.0 is permissive but not sublicensable, so those files stay Apache-2.0 no matter who forks them, and the NOTICE and patent terms travel with them. Ronin's existing lineage is ISC via samurai, which folds into a permissive distribution cleanly provided the Forney notice is retained, and it already is.\n\nSo the workable arrangement is: the kati crate declares Apache-2.0 and keeps its NOTICE; Ronin's own crate declares Apache-2.0 OR MIT; our modifications to the vendored files are contributed under Apache-2.0 OR MIT, which is a superset of what Apache-2.0 requires and costs nothing; and the distribution carries all three notices. Nothing here blocks the goal, and no part of it requires pretending the Google files are MIT.\n\nWhat this node has to produce is the metadata rather than the argument: crate licence fields, a LICENSE arrangement covering all three, and a NOTICE that survives packaging."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T06:31:58.824646137Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Closed as already resolved by the crate split, rather than by anything this node needed to do.\n\nThe reason there was a question at all was the possibility of merging the fork into Ronin's crate, where one license field would have had to cover both. ronin-execute-api rejected that merge on independent grounds and used precisely this as one of its reasons, so the arrangement it preserves is the arrangement that was wanted: kati declares Apache-2.0 and keeps its own LICENSE and AUTHORS, Ronin keeps its existing notices, and neither has to describe the other.\n\nThe only thing actually missing was a declaration. kati's manifest had no license field despite shipping an Apache-2.0 LICENSE file, so cargo could not report what the crate was. Set to Apache-2.0, which states the file's existing terms and decides nothing.\n\nRonin's own LICENSE is left alone. It carries four upstream positions — ISC for samurai, Google's terms for the Ninja-derived material, and two MIT notices — and restructuring it is a deliberate act for its owner, not a side effect of vendoring a dependency that never touches it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T06:42:23.811196065Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-execute-api
                title "Expose graph execution to an out-of-crate front end"
                deps (ronin-frontend-api)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T17:58:05.995434281Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from a gap found while closing ronin-frontend-api, which this node was not part of.\n\nThat node made a graph constructible from outside the manifest parser, and its rule only requires the build, tool, and persistence paths to accept a front-end-produced graph, which they now do. But they accept it through arenas(), arenas_mut(), and into_arenas(), all pub(crate). So a BuildGraph can be built by anything in the crate and executed only by the crate. The Make front end is a separate crate in the workspace, so kati-graph-sink cannot finish without this, which is why it is now a dependency of it rather than a discovery inside it.\n\nWhat has to be decided rather than merely written: BuildOptions is a large surface, and exposing it wholesale would freeze a lot of internal shape as public API for the sake of one in-workspace consumer. Options worth weighing are a narrower execution request type built from the options a front end actually sets, a builder that a front end fills in and cannot otherwise construct, or keeping the surface crate-internal and moving the Make front end into this crate as a module instead of a workspace member. The last one is a real alternative and should be argued rather than dismissed, since the current split exists to keep the fork's licence and lints quarantined, not because the code has to live in two crates.\n\nWhichever way it goes, it wants recording against [dec:ronin:frontend-graph-boundary], because that decision says the boundary is the supported extension point for any front end and an execution surface is half of what that promises."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T18:46:00.667712007Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Chose a builder whose field set stays private, over a request type with public fields and over folding the fork in as a module.\n\nThe argument that settled it against folding in is worth keeping, because that option was the cheap one and it was rejected on substance rather than convenience. A boundary reachable only from inside the crate is an internal refactor wearing the word boundary, and the one consumer that would prove it works would be the one consumer that never uses it — which contradicts the decision it would be implementing. Licence is also a crate-level fact: cargo has one license field per crate, so merging either makes Ronin's metadata a lie or deletes the MIT option from Ronin's own work for every downstream consumer, not just for us. And edition is per-crate, so merging forces one edition on a vendored tree that tracks upstream.\n\nThe builder is what kept the surface small. None of BuildOptions became public — eighteen fields including TerminalContext, ShellMode, the jobserver transport, and OutputStyle. The CLI reaches the rest through one crate-internal constructor on the same public type and the same execution path, so a foreign front end that later needs one of those knobs gets it as a new method rather than as a breaking change.\n\nTwo behavioural changes came out of it, both toward Ninja and both verified rather than assumed. Target names now resolve through canonicalization as Ninja's CollectTarget and RebuildManifest do, so ronin ./out builds where it previously failed — checked directly against the pinned reference binary, which accepts it. And MissingRule reports the interned path rather than the path as typed, which is the same bytes in every case the old exact lookup would have accepted.\n\nWorth being explicit about what the boundary is not. Tool modes still read the arenas crate-internally, so this is build-a-graph-and-run-it, not everything the Ninja CLI can do to a graph. Inspection tooling for a foreign front end is unbuilt and unplanned, and that is fine until something asks for it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T06:40:49.919198397Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id kati-quality-gates
                title "Bring the vendored fork under Ronin's quality gates"
                deps (kati-submodule)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-04T17:58:19.888140202Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Governance gap: the vendored fork is under nspec but not under the quality gates, so \"Ronin's nplan governs it\" is currently half true.\n\n.config/nspec/config.styx now includes kati/src-rs/** and coverage works — make.scope-separation is covered and tested from the fork. But .config/nplan/config.styx still lists the rust platform's files as Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, and src/**, so function-density, trivial-function, duplication, and comment-smells do not see the fork at all. Every commit touching only kati skips the gates the rest of the tree is held to.\n\nTurning it on is not a one-line config change, which is why this is a node. Measured now: src-rs/func.rs has 59 functions against a limit of 50, and src-rs/find.rs has exactly 50, so it fires immediately and then sits one function from firing again. func.rs is Make's builtin function table, roughly one function per Make builtin, so a high count there is arguably the correct shape rather than a defect — which makes this a question about whether the gate wants an exemption, a split of the table into groups, or a raised threshold for that file. The existing config already has an exempt-paths list and a trivial-function allow-names list, so both mechanisms are available and precedented.\n\nDo this before the fork grows, not after. The longer the gates stay off, the more of the fork is grandfathered in and the more expensive the eventual reckoning. It is also worth running the gates in report-only mode first to see the full inventory rather than discovering it one blocked commit at a time."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:06:42.848490105Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The fork is now inside the quality gates. Two changes, and the second was the bigger hole.\n\nThe rust platform's file globs gain kati/Cargo.toml, kati/src-rs/** and kati/tests/**, so function-density, trivial-function, duplication, complexity, file-size and the comment sweep see the fork's sources. That was the stated gap.\n\nThe check commands were the unstated one. They ran cargo test and cargo check with no package selection, which in a workspace with a root package means the root package only — so from the moment the fork became a workspace member, no commit gate had ever built or tested it. Both are now --workspace. The three configured checks pass.\n\nfunc.rs is exempted from function-density rather than split. It is GNU Make's builtin function table: one function per builtin plus the dispatch entry naming it. A density limit asks whether a file is doing too many things, and this one does a single thing that happens to have sixty cases, so splitting it to satisfy a count would scatter the table without simplifying anything. The exemption is narrow and the reason is recorded beside it. Note find.rs sits at exactly the limit, so the next function added there fires the gate — that is the gate working, not a problem to pre-empt.\n\nFive files exceed the 1000-line warn threshold for rust: func.rs, find.rs, eval.rs, dep.rs and ninja.rs. The error threshold is 3000, so none of them blocks anything. Left alone deliberately — they are warnings about a vendored tree that tracks upstream, and churning file boundaries in a fork raises the cost of every future merge for no correctness gain.\n\nThe gates are delta-scoped, so none of this fires retroactively. It applies to the next commit that touches each file, which is the right time to pay it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:06:51.184781451Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id kati-command-escapes
                title "Separate Ninja escaping from Make command construction"
                deps (kati-build-sink)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T06:57:29.771946174Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from a hazard found closing kati-build-sink, and it blocks kati-graph-sink because getting it wrong is silent.\n\nThe sink was designed to carry unescaped data, and for paths it does: they cross as symbol handles, and escape_ninja moved into the writer. Commands are the exception, and not by oversight. translate_command at ninja.rs:312 rewrites a dollar to a doubled dollar, which is purely a Ninja escape, but it lives in gen_shell_script, which is command construction and which the sink design deliberately keeps in the front end. So the bytes crossing SinkRule::command are Ninja-escaped even though nothing else at that boundary is.\n\nWhy that is dangerous rather than merely untidy. Through the emitted manifest the escape is correct and round-trips: Ninja unescapes the pair when it evaluates the command, and the shell sees a single dollar. A graph sink writing the same bytes straight into an edge binding skips the unescaping entirely, so the shell sees two. Every Makefile using a shell variable is affected, and make-graph-equivalence cannot see it, because both sides of that comparison carry the same bytes — the whole point of that oracle is that the two paths agree, and here they would agree on being wrong.\n\nIt cannot be fixed by moving translate_command's dollar case into the writer on its own. strutil::escape_shell special-cases the doubled pair at strutil.rs:464, so the two escapes are written against each other and have to be untangled together. Both sites are confirmed present.\n\nThe gate is byte-identity of the emitted manifest, same as kati-build-sink: this is a refactor of where an escape happens, not a change to what gets written. The corpus and flag-matrix harnesses from that node are the right tool and should be reused rather than rebuilt.\n\nTwo smaller instances of the same shape are in scope. The default description is the literal \"build $out\" and the response file path is \"$out.rsp\"; both are Ninja expressions rather than strings, so a direct graph has to expand them itself or keep an evaluator alive for exactly two bindings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:29:17.026818872Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-phony-always-dirty
                title "Let an edge declare itself never up to date"
                deps (kati-graph-sink)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T09:46:47.31073908Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while closing kati-graph-sink, reported rather than hidden, and it is the largest behavioural gap in Make mode today.\n\nMake's .PHONY says a target has no file behind it and its recipe runs whenever the target is asked for. Ninja carries the same idea two ways, both of which are serialisation devices: kati emits either a synthetic _kati_always_build_ input that no rule produces, so the edge is permanently out of date, or phony_output = true. The graph sink drops both, correctly — the synthetic node exists only to smuggle the property through a manifest, and phony_output is a binding Ronin does not define.\n\nThe consequence is that the property has nowhere to live. Measured: a .PHONY target runs on the first build, gets recorded in the build log, and is reported already up to date on the second. GNU Make runs it every time. Every Makefile with a `clean`, `test`, `install` or `all` target is affected, which is close to all of them.\n\nThe fix belongs in the graph, not in the sink. An edge needs to be able to say it is never up to date, and the dirty evaluation has to honour it — probably alongside the existing generator and restat handling rather than as a new concept. The manifest front end has no way to spell it and does not need one, so this is the first property the graph carries that only one front end can produce; decide deliberately whether the Ninja side ever gets a spelling or whether it stays construction-only.\n\nTwo things to get right. The build log records these edges today, so whatever lands has to make the log's answer not matter for them rather than merely writing a different entry. And Ninja's own phony rule is a different thing entirely — it means \"this name aliases its inputs\", not \"always run\" — so do not conflate the two senses the way the emitter's field names invite.\n\nThe equivalence oracle will need to know: the manifest side will carry a synthetic input or a phony_output binding that the direct side expresses structurally, so the classification in src/make/equivalence.rs has to move rather than simply pass."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:32:28.235137884Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-package-with-fork
                title "Restore packaging now that Ronin depends on the vendored fork"
                deps (kati-graph-sink)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T09:47:04.573834345Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "cargo package now fails with \"no matching package named 'kati' found\", because Ronin gained a path dependency on the vendored fork and a path dependency needs a version resolvable from a registry once the crate is packaged.\n\nThis is the consequence the crate-direction decision on kati-graph-sink recorded and explicitly deferred rather than a surprise. Deferring it was right — the alternative was letting a packaging question dictate an architecture — but it is now a live break in tests/release_gate.rs rather than a hypothetical.\n\nCargo.toml's include list also does not mention kati/**, so even a resolvable version would ship a crate whose Make feature cannot build.\n\nThe options are genuinely different in kind and this node's job is to choose, not to patch. Publish the fork to a registry under our own name, which makes it a real dependency and means every change to it needs a release before Ronin can consume it. Vendor the fork's sources into Ronin's package at publish time, which keeps one publishable artefact but means the submodule and the packaged copy can drift. Or accept that Ronin is not published to crates.io while the Make feature exists, and say so — the feature is default-on, so this is a decision about what the product is, not only about how it ships.\n\nWorth checking before choosing: whether the release gate actually needs cargo package to succeed, or whether it is asserting a property nobody depends on yet. If nothing consumes Ronin from a registry today, the honest fix may be to change what the gate asserts and record why, rather than to contort the build."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:07:47.967868609Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Resolved by deciding what the product is rather than by making the build satisfy a gate. Ronin is a binary tool and will not be published to crates.io, so the crate is now publish = false and the release gate stops building a registry package.\n\nThat reframes the failure. Depending on the vendored fork through a path was never the problem; expecting a self-contained registry artifact was. cargo install --git builds from the checkout with the submodule present and is unaffected, so nothing anyone actually does with Ronin was broken by the dependency. The three options recorded on this node — publish the fork, vendor its sources at publish time, or accept no registry presence — collapse once the third is chosen deliberately instead of by default.\n\nThe leak check went with it. It listed the package contents and failed if planning files, legacy C sources or the old manual appeared, which was worth doing when a crate was the shipped thing. With no crate built it has no subject, and keeping it would mean maintaining an inspection of an artifact nobody consumes. If a distribution artifact is added later — a tarball, a release binary — that check belongs to it and should inspect what actually ships.\n\nA test now asserts the absence, not just the presence. A gate quietly losing a step looks identical to a gate that never had it, and the next person seeing no packaging step would reasonably read it as an oversight and add it back. The test also pins publish = false in the manifest, so the two cannot drift apart.\n\nThe include list in Cargo.toml is retained. It is inert while nothing is packaged, but it still describes what a distribution artifact would be made of, and deleting it would throw away a considered answer to a question that may return."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:32:30.487992622Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-option-coverage
                title "Support the Make options whose machinery already exists"
                deps (make-mode-cli)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:22:32.846350061Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "make-mode-cli rejected thirteen options by name rather than ignoring them, which was right — silently accepting -B would build the wrong thing. But rejecting was a statement about that node's scope, not about feasibility, and several of them are now cheap because the machinery landed for other reasons. Checked against the tree rather than assumed.\n\nCheap, because the mechanism already exists and is already exercised:\n-B / --always-make. EdgeSpec::always_dirty exists as of make-phony-always-dirty. Setting it on every edge is the whole implementation, and the property is already tested and already compared by the equivalence oracle.\n-l / --load-average. BuildOptions::maxload exists and build.rs already honours it; Ninja's own -l feeds it. Make's spelling differs but the meaning is the same.\n-q / --question. Planned::already_up_to_date answers exactly this without starting a build, which is why plan and run were separated in the first place.\n-r / --no-builtin-rules. kati's flags already carry no_builtin_rules and its bootstrap already branches on it. This is a pass-through.\n-i / --ignore-errors. kati already models ignore_error per command and already appends the shell idiom for it; -i is that flag set globally.\n-w / --print-directory. Make mode already prints Entering directory for -C. This is forcing it on unconditionally, plus the matching Leaving line, which the corpus records as a difference today.\n-e / --environment-overrides. kati already distinguishes VarOrigin::Environment from EnvironmentOverride, which is the whole semantic difference.\n\nModerate, needing real work but nothing new architecturally: -t (touch outputs instead of running recipes), -o and -W (pretend a file is older or newer than it is, which needs a per-node timestamp override the graph does not currently take), -I (include search path).\n\nExpensive and low value for now: -p (print the whole database, which is a tool mode of its own) and --debug (Make's debug categories have no counterpart and would be invented rather than matched).\n\nDo the cheap seven together. They share one shape — parse a flag, set something that already works — and splitting them across nodes would cost more in ceremony than the work. Each needs a corpus case or a CLI test; several appear in the differing column today, so the make-conformance counts should move and that movement is the evidence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:32:38.518451978Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

