                        id make-refusal-wording-is-narration
                        title "Record shared refusal wording as discovery data, not semantics"
                        priority 1
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T17:59:53.88843468Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The classifier files 200 cases as no-rule-to-make and calls all of them semantic, on the reasoning that a rule which should have matched did not. 26 of them are cases where GNU Make refuses too.\n\n  expected  ronin: *** No rule to make target 'hello.c', needed by 'hello'.  Stop.\n  got       ronin: 'hello.c', needed by 'hello', missing and no known rule to make it\n\nBoth tools decline to build, for the same reason, about the same target. Under the rule this project adopted — recipe output is Make's semantics and must match, tool narration is ours — that is narration, and io-error-text is already classified exactly this way for the same reason.\n\nThe family is triggered on our side's wording alone, at examples/make_upstream.rs:515. It needs to look at the other side as well: our refusal paired with GNU Make's `No rule to make target` is narration, our refusal alone is semantic. Match the target name across the pair rather than only the shape, so that refusing the wrong target is still counted.\n\nMeasured before writing this: 26 both-refuse, 174 ours-only. So this is an honesty correction to the number, not a way of making it smaller — the 174 are real and are what make-implicit-rule-chaining and make-second-expansion are for."
                            }

                            {
