                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T20:45:20.190343194Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "26 cases, exactly as measured before the work: semantic 722 to 696, no-rule-to-make 168 to 142.\n\nTwo families rather than one, because the 26 are not all the same thing. 22 refuse the same target and differ only in wording — shared-refusal. In 4, GNU Make walks further into a broken chain before giving up: it names `hello` needed by `hello.tsk` where we name `hello.tsk` needed by `all`. Both refuse the same build for the same reason and only the link named differs, which is narration by the same argument and is the shape the kati corpus already calls dependent-attribution. Keeping them apart means the attribution question stays visible instead of being absorbed.\n\nThe decision is made against the raw divergence rather than the residue, because the target name is what distinguishes the two cases and normalisation is free to drop the line that carries it.\n\nWhat is left in no-rule-to-make is 142, and all of it is a target GNU Make actually built."
