                    {
                        id make-io-error-wording
                        title "Report Makefile I/O failures through ordinary Ronin diagnostics"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T08:44:23.239321847Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T08:44:44.907802407Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Grouped the 76 cases by what actually went wrong rather than by the sentence. Baseline at lane branch head: 1348 failing, semantic 772, capability 54, narration 522; io-error-text 53, shared-refusal 23.\n\nio-error-text, 53, and it is five causes, not one:\n\n  35  kati's `include` of a file it cannot glob. kati/src-rs/eval.rs:958-966 formats `\"{pat}: {err}\"` with a raw std::io::Error. features/include x14, features/reinvoke x6, options/print-directory x4, features/parallelism x2, options/dash-B x2, options/dash-I x2, variables/MAKE_RESTARTS x2, features/patternrules x1, options/dash-W x1, options/dash-k x1.\n   5  kati reading a file it found but cannot open. kati/src-rs/file.rs:36 propagates `std::fs::read(...)?` into anyhow with no context at all, so the whole diagnostic is `Permission denied (os error 13)` with no name and no location — features/include 22, 23, 24, 25. functions/file.diff.4 is the same shape from `$(file >...)`.\n  10  Ronin statting a path longer than NAME_MAX. GraphError::Stat. features/patternrules x4, features/statipattrules x6.\n   2  Ronin's jobserver. ProcessError::Jobserver and ProcessError::JobserverEnvironment. features/jobserver 15, 16.\n   1  Ronin's process boundary failing to write the terminal. src/main.rs:62.\n\nshared-refusal, 23, and it is one cause exactly: BuildError::MissingInput with a `needed_by`, src/error.rs. Every one of the 23 is the same pair, differing only in the sentence:\n\n  expected  ronin: *** No rule to make target 'hello.c', needed by 'hello'.  Stop.\n  got       ronin: 'hello.c', needed by 'hello', missing and no known rule to make it\n\nfeatures/implicit_search x16, features/include x4, features/mult_rules, features/suffixrules, options/dash-q.\n\nTwo things the grouping turned up that the family names hide.\n\nThe `(os error ` marker at examples/make_upstream.rs:548 files a case as narration whenever that string appears anywhere in the residue, and it is exclusive, so it suppresses whatever else the case was. It is wrong for at least 13 of the 53. features/include.diff.10 is the clearest: GNU Make builds the missing includes and runs the recipe (`echo > inc1`, `echo > inc2`, `DONE`) where Ronin dies on the first one — a real semantic difference filed as narration because one Rust suffix happened to be in the diff. The ten ENAMETOOLONG cases are the same: GNU Make builds `bye.x` and Ronin abandons.\n\nThe `(os error N)` suffix is not Make-mode narration at all. Ronin's Ninja mode carries it too — `ronin: No such file or directory` where upstream Ninja says `ninja: error: loading 'build.ninja': No such file or directory` — so it is a Rust artifact leaking into a message that is supposed to be strerror in both front ends. src/error.rs already said so in `system_message`'s doc comment; the helper simply was not reached from anywhere but the chdir path and one parse site."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T08:45:02.623311505Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "The node's title is wrong and is not being carried out. A refusal both tools share keeps Ronin's wording; what is removed is the Rust suffix, which is nobody's wording.\n\n`[spec:ronin:req:make.narration]` settles the first half outright: \"Make mode reports progress, failures and diagnostics in the same shape as the manifest front end, rather than reproducing GNU Make's wording. This is a product decision and not a gap.\" So rewriting `'hello.c', needed by 'hello', missing and no known rule to make it` into `*** No rule to make target 'hello.c', needed by 'hello'.  Stop.` would break a spec rule to make a family the classifier already calls narration go to zero. All 23 shared-refusal cases are that one sentence, from BuildError::MissingInput, and it is Ninja's own text pinned by the upstream Ninja tests. They stay. This agrees with make-refusal-wording-is-narration rather than superseding it; the brief's premise that the description of the fault is unsettled does not survive reading make.narration.\n\n` (os error N)` is a different thing and is not a wording choice. GNU Make quotes strerror; Ninja quotes strerror; Rust's io::Error quotes strerror and appends the raw errno. Whatever the two front ends disagree about, neither of them says `(os error 2)`, and Ronin says it in Ninja mode as well. So it is removed everywhere an io::Error reaches a diagnostic rather than in Make mode alone — which also means this is a Ninja-compatibility fix that happened to be found through the Make suite.\n\n`system_message` now takes anything that displays rather than an `io::Error`, because a source that owns one renders it inside a sentence of its own and the suffix then sits in the middle: the jobserver crate's FromEnvError is exactly that. It strips every occurrence and leaves a `(os error)` with no digits alone.\n\nThe classifier is deliberately not touched. Removing the suffix re-files the cases it was masking by itself, which is the honest correction and needs no change to the tool: the ten ENAMETOOLONG cases and the two jobserver cases were never narration and now count as what they are. Expect the semantic number to rise, and read that as the measurement getting more honest rather than as a regression, the same way make-refusal-wording-is-narration read its own correction."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T08:50:07.231927474Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Measured. 12 cases moved and every one of them moved from narration to semantic, which is the intended shape of this change rather than a regression.\n\nClassifier transition, both runs on this branch with the same suite checkout:\n\n  io-error-text      53 → 41\n  shared-refusal     23 → 23   unchanged by decision\n  evaluation        576 → 588\n  narration         522 → 510\n  semantic          772 → 784\n  capability         54 → 54\n  failing cases    1348 → 1348\n\nThe 12 by name: features/jobserver 15 and 16, features/patternrules 41, 53, 59, 71, features/statipattrules 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63. Each was a case where Ronin abandoned a build GNU Make finished, wearing a narration label because one Rust suffix sat in the diff. Removing the suffix took the label off and the defect underneath is now counted where it belongs. No case moved the other way and no case outside the 12 moved at all; recipe-interleave went 2 to 1 on its own and `settled()` already ignores that one for exactly this reason.\n\nWhat is left in io-error-text is 41 and it is not ours:\n\n  35  kati/src-rs/eval.rs:958-966, the include-glob failure\n   5  kati/src-rs/file.rs:36, a file found but unreadable, plus $(file >...)\n   1  misc/close_stdout, src/main.rs:62\n\nThe last of those is left deliberately. `system_message` is pub(crate) and src/main.rs is the binary crate, so reaching it means widening the library's public API for one string; and the case fails on the sentence rather than on the suffix — the suite wants `/^ronin: write error/` where we say `writing terminal output:` — so stripping it there would change the wording without changing the count.\n\nTwo defects the change exposed, both real and neither this node's:\n\n  features/jobserver.16. GNU Make cannot open the inherited jobserver, says so, warns `jobserver unavailable: using -j1`, and finishes the build. Ronin says the same thing and exits 1. Verified against GNU Make 4.4.1 side by side. That is make-jobserver-server or make-recursive-jobserver-reach.\n\n  patternrules and statipattrules, 10 cases. A stem longer than NAME_MAX reaches a stat and Ronin abandons on ENAMETOOLONG where GNU Make builds the target. Ronin's own, GraphError::Stat.\n\nAnd one that is nobody's node yet: Ronin's Ninja mode answers a missing manifest with `ronin: No such file or directory` where upstream Ninja says `ninja: error: loading 'build.ninja': No such file or directory`. The suffix is gone now; the missing `error: loading '<path>': ` context is a separate Ninja-compatibility gap that the 425 pinned tests do not reach."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T14:21:46.869590908Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "The earlier GNU Make wording target is superseded. Preserve the I/O error and its source/path through Ronin's ordinary diagnostic surface; do not add Make reporter ceremony."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T18:21:19.534969949Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Delivered as the 14:21 decision framed it: the I/O error, the failing path and the source location of the directive that asked for the read now survive to Ronin's ordinary diagnostic surface. No Make reporter ceremony was added — `*** ` and `  Stop.` are still stripped by src/make/report.rs on the way out, which is what makes error_loc! the ordinary surface rather than a Make imitation.\n\nMeasurement first, because the recorded inventory turned out not to be the baseline.\n\ntests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv held 1349 rows and said io-error-text 44. A fresh run of the same suite at this branch's HEAD, before any of my code, gives 1351 rows and io-error-text 6. The recorded file was stale: main moved after it was last written, so 111 of the 116 rows this commit rewrites are the inventory catching up with work that had already landed and nothing to do with this node. The gate was already red at HEAD for that reason. Anyone reading the tsv diff as this node's result will be wrong by a factor of twenty.\n\nAgainst a same-tree HEAD run, exactly five cases moved and every one of them is one of the five this node's 08:44 grouping named:\n\n  io-error-text      6 → 1\n  narration        541 → 536\n  unclassified     621 → 626\n  compiler          73 → 73    unchanged\n  interface        116 → 116   unchanged\n  counted         1351 → 1351\n\nBy name: features/include 22, 23, 24, 25 and functions/file.4, each narration → semantic. Nothing else in 1351 cases moved at all, in either direction, which also settles that the two runs are reproducible — no grouped_targets-style flap this time. The move is the same shape as the earlier suffix removal and should be read the same way: the label came off and the defect underneath is now counted where it belongs.\n\nThe one io-error-text left is misc/close_stdout, deliberately, for the reason recorded at 08:50: it fails on the sentence rather than the suffix, and reaching Ronin's system_message from the binary crate means widening the library's public API for one string.\n\nWhat was actually wrong, and it was five sites rather than one. kati/src-rs/file.rs:36 was the one the brief named, but `Bytes::from(std::fs::read(filename)?)` was the shape rather than the instance: `$(file <)`, `$(file >)`, fileutil::get_timestamp, exec.rs's order-only stat and two flag-driven output files all propagated a bare io::Error the same way, and eval.rs's include-glob site named the path but interpolated the raw error, so it carried the suffix even though it already had a location.\n\nMakefile::from_file now answers `Source::Read | Absent | Unreadable(io::Error)` instead of `Option`. That is the whole fix: absence and unreadability are different answers and only the caller knows the path and the directive, so the reader stops trying to describe a failure it has no words for. It also collapses the `exists` then `read` pair into one syscall — the two asked the same question, could disagree under a concurrent writer, and `exists` reported a directory it could not search as an indistinguishable Err.\n\n`kati::strerror` strips ` (os error N)` keyed on the errno the error itself reports, not by scanning for the shape, so a diagnostic that legitimately quotes those words keeps them. Ronin's own system_message scans, because it takes anything that displays; kati's has a real io::Error to ask. Both are tested for the quoting case.\n\nProbed against GNU Make 4.4.1 side by side rather than read off the manual, seven cases: unreadable include, unreadable include with an -I fallback available, unreadable top-level makefile, include of a directory, $(file >) onto a read-only file, $(file <) of an unreadable file, and -include of an unreadable file. Six matched the message modulo kati's own `In file included from` line. The seventh did not, and it was a defect this node's code path owned: `-include` of an unreadable file refused the build where GNU Make 4.4.1 evaluates it to nothing and reports nothing. GNU's read.c records the error under RM_DONTCARE and main.c never reports one, which the probe confirms — exit 0, silence. do_include now takes `required` and returns quietly when the Makefile said it did not care. Fixed here rather than deferred because the wrong diagnostic was being emitted by the exact lines this node rewrote.\n\nTwo behaviour gaps found and deliberately not taken, both belonging to make-include-semantics rather than here.\n\nAn unreadable `include` is a regeneration root in GNU Make. read.c's eval_makefile enters the file as a goaldep whatever the open failed with, and the error is only reported if it could not be remade, so `include inc1` on an unreadable inc1 with a rule that recreates it builds successfully — features/include 24 and 25 are exactly that test. Ronin already does this for ENOENT via MissingInclude and dies for everything else. Carrying the reason into MissingInclude is the shape of the fix; the hardcoded \"No such file or directory\" in evaluate.rs is where it would go.\n\nAnd when it cannot be remade, GNU reports the I/O error and then refuses the target: `Makefile:1: inc1: Permission denied` followed by `*** No rule to make target 'inc1'.  Stop.`. Ronin emits the first line and stops. That is the residual difference in all four features/include cases, and it is why they are semantic now rather than narration.\n\nGates. cargo test --workspace --all-targets clean (20 suites); clippy clean on the workspace and on `-p kati -- -D warnings`; fmt --check clean in both crates. check-make-upstream.sh green and reproducible across two runs. check-make-equivalence.sh passes, 470 cases. check-make-conformance.sh output is byte-identical to a HEAD run of the same script — including its six pre-existing \"changed shape\" rows in tests/make_corpus_inventory.tsv, which are stale on main the same way the upstream inventory was and are not this node's to re-record; one of them, err_include.mk, is recorded as io-error-text but no longer produces the suffix at all."
                            }

