                    {
                        id make-parser-parity
                        title "Read the makefiles GNU Make reads"
                        priority 0
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T09:10:10.911260884Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T09:37:32.020402561Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "43 parse-failure cases, five causes, and the largest was 29 of them. The grouping is the durable finding, so it is recorded before what was done about it.\n\n  .RECIPEPREFIX                     29  features/targetvars ×9, variables/MAKEFLAGS ×5, features/jobserver ×3, features/double_colon ×2, features/statipattrules ×2, features/parallelism, features/recursion, functions/intcmp, functions/origin, functions/shell, functions/word, targets/ONESHELL, variables/MAKE_RESTARTS\n  undefine                           5  variables/undefine ×4, variables/special\n  a variable name is one word        3  variables/negative ×3\n  ifeq without whitespace            2  misc/failure ×2\n  -I - and include attribution       3  options/dash-I ×3\n  eight spaces where a tab was meant 1  misc/failure\n\nMethod, because it decided the size of the job. Every case was reduced to the line the diagnostic named, by reading the makefile the suite had already written into its work directory and printing that line with `cat -A`. Thirty lines of shell. Grouping them took ten minutes and showed one cause was two thirds of the family, which is what a day of reasoning about the grammar would not have shown.\n\nFour of the five are fixed, in kati df2d107 and in `-I -` here. parse-failure 43 → 6.\n\nThe classification transition, before → after:\n\n                 before   after\n  semantic          772      749\n  capability         54       55\n  narration         522      540\n  counted         1,348    1,344\n\nFour cases now pass outright and leave the count: variables/negative ×3 and variables/undefine.3. The semantic fall is not the whole story — of the 29 .RECIPEPREFIX cases, 11 went straight to narration and 18 to semantic, which is the shape this node was expected to have. A makefile that would not parse produced one refusal and hid everything else; parsed, it produces countable differences. features/targetvars is the clearest: nine cases that all said `missing separator` at line 9 are now nine cases saying what target-specific variables actually evaluated to.\n\nTwo cases moved the other way and it is an improvement, not a regression. variables/private.3 and .4 went from evaluation to parse-failure, and private.15 from narration to semantic, because the one-word rule stopped `private FOO = g` being read as an assignment to a variable called `private FOO`. That was a wrong build that happened to look right in one case — `FOO` was never defined, and the test expected `FOO=<unset>`. A refusal is the right answer until `private` is implemented.\n\nThree things about the implementation worth knowing.\n\n.RECIPEPREFIX is a parse-time fact in GNU Make and an eval-time one here, and that gap is the whole difficulty. GNU applies it in set_special_var the moment the variable is defined, so it is in force for the next line read. kati parses a file whole and then evaluates it. Reading the assignment in the parser closes 29 cases and costs three narrowings, each of which is a refusal rather than a wrong build: an assignment inside a conditional is not read, because kati parses both branches and only one runs; a `:=` value holding a `$` cannot be expanded yet; and the prefix does not reach an included file, which is parsed when the `include` runs. Applying it inside conditionals was considered and rejected — a `.RECIPEPREFIX` in a branch that does not run would make tab-led recipes below the `endif` unreadable, and worse, would make `>`-led ones readable where GNU Make refuses.\n\nA recursive value is taken verbatim. GNU reads `var->value[0]`, which for `=` is the unexpanded text, so `.RECIPEPREFIX = $(X)` sets the prefix to `$` in both tools. Checked, not assumed.\n\n`!=` is `$(shell)` in every respect but one, and the exception is not in the manual. GNU passes trim_newlines=0 to func_shell_base for `!=` and 1 for `$(shell)`, and fold_newlines reads that as: drop the whole run of trailing newlines, or fold it into spaces bar the last. `printf '6\\n\\n\\n\\n'` gives `6` through `$(shell)` and `6   ` through `!=`. Half-implementing this as plain `$(shell)` would have been a wrong value, which is why func.rs grew a Trailing enum and a second FuncInfo that no makefile can name.\n\nSix parse failures remain, in four kinds, and none of them is one more instance of something already fixed.\n\nTwo need parse and evaluation interleaved, which this architecture does not do. misc/failure.3 and .6 write `ifeq(foo,bar)` with no space; GNU refuses at line 2 with `missing separator (ifeq/ifneq must be followed by whitespace)`, and Ronin refuses at line 4 with `extraneous 'endif'` because the parser walks the whole file before the evaluator sees line 2. Both refuse; the line and the wording differ. Making the parser refuse line 2 itself would be wrong — kati parses conditional branches that do not run, and GNU skips them, so a bad line inside a false branch would become an error GNU does not raise.\n\nOne is a missing parenthetical. misc/failure.1 is eight spaces where a tab was meant, and GNU says so in the diagnostic. Both tools refuse the same makefile at the same line. Emitting the hint needs the raw line at evaluation time — RuleStmt carries an `orig` that is always empty and could hold it — and GNU gates the hint on the recipe prefix still being a tab, which is known in the parser and not in the evaluator. One case, wording only, left alone.\n\nOne is diagnostic attribution. options/dash-I.5 has both tools refusing `idir2/ifile.mk`, GNU naming it `ifile.mk` as the `include` wrote it and Ronin naming the path the search resolved, plus kati's `In file included from` line, which GNU has no equivalent of. That line is kati's own and wraps every include error, so removing it is a decision about diagnostic shape rather than about this node.\n\nTwo are `private`, which is a node and not a defect. Recorded below.\n\nFor other nodes, found while measuring.\n\n`private` is unimplemented and now refused. Sixteen cases in variables/private, of which two are parse-failure and eight are semantic. It is a modifier in three positions — `private VAR = x`, `private export VAR = x`, and `target: private VAR = x` — and it is not a no-op even on a global variable: test 4 of the suite's own file turns on a global `private F = g` making `a: F=` where a plain global would make `a: F=g`. The third position is a wrong build today: `all: private FOO = a` silently defines a target-specific variable called `private FOO` and `$(FOO)` is empty, where GNU Make answers `a`.\n\n`$(flavor)` on a `!=` variable answers `simple` where GNU Make answers `recursive` — GNU has a distinct f_shell flavor that reports as recursive, and kati has only the value's own shape to go on.\n\n`$(origin .RECIPEPREFIX)` answers `undefined` where GNU Make answers `default`, and `$(flavor)` `undefined` where GNU says `simple`. GNU defines the variable with a default origin and an empty value; kati has no default-origin bindings.\n\nA command-line variable that `override undefine` removed is back for a recipe's expansion: `$(flavor d)` inside a recipe answers `recursive` in GNU Make and `undefined` here, though both agree when the same expression is at makefile level. GNU reinstates command-line variables per recipe.\n\n`.SHELLSTATUS` cannot be read inside a rule — `Kati does not support using .SHELLSTATUS inside of a rule`. It reads fine at makefile level."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T09:42:03.079303205Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }

