                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T14:03:47.450163305Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The corpus we have answers the wrong question. It is kati's own, so it tests what kati chose to implement and is silent about everything nobody wrote a case for — vpath being the obvious example, since kati has no vpath and the corpus therefore never asks. GNU Make ships its own suite, and it is written to test an arbitrary make binary rather than its own internals, which makes it the unbiased measure.\n\nEstablished by trying it rather than reading about it, so the next person starts from facts:\n\nShape. 137 scripts under tests/scripts in six categories — features, functions, misc, options, targets, variables — carrying 1060 individual assertions. A Perl driver, run_make_tests.pl, and perl 5.40 is present. Retargeting is one flag, -make <path>, exactly as this project's own harnesses ended up.\n\nBaseline. Real GNU Make 4.4.1 against its own suite in this environment fails 2 tests in 2 categories. So the denominator is about 1058, not 1060, and any comparison has to subtract the environment's own noise before claiming anything.\n\nLicence. GPLv3. Do not vendor it. The repository already has the pattern for exactly this: reference/ is gitignored and holds a pinned upstream Ninja checkout that check-ninja-conformance.sh runs against. Fetch a pinned 4.4.1 tag the same way. Running someone's GPL test scripts against our binary does not make our code derivative; shipping their scripts would be a distribution question we have no reason to take on.\n\nTwo blockers, both in the harness rather than in Ronin, and one of them is a decision.\n\nThe driver needs tests/config-flags.pm, which GNU Make's configure generates. Either run configure once in the fetched tree or supply a stub; find out which fields the driver actually reads before generating a whole build.\n\nThe driver dies unless `make -v` starts with the literal \"GNU Make\". Ronin says \"ronin 0.1.0 / Make front end for GNU Make 4.4.1 makefiles\", by product.make-identity. This is the same shape as the fourteen self-detecting cases in the kati corpus, and it should be settled the same way — in the harness, not by making Ronin claim to be GNU Make. Impersonating upstream in --version to pass upstream's tests would be measuring a lie.\n\nNote argv[0] selects the front end, so the driver must be pointed at a symlink literally named make. Pointing it at a path whose stem is anything else selects Ninja mode and produces a confusing failure; that is the dispatch working, not a defect.\n\nExpect a large number of failures on the first run and do not treat that as a verdict. Some will be real language gaps — the function inventory already says intcmp, let and guile are absent, and vpath, private, undefine and load are unimplemented directives. Others will be things we deliberately do not do: -p database dumps, --debug categories, and the recipe echo contract the kati corpus already showed differs on 371 of 372 runs. Classify before quoting a number, the way make-conformance-corpus does, and report the raw count first."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T14:03:24.559306304Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Baseline, taken with scripts/check-make-upstream.sh at ronin 99f1507 / kati 5ce8e0e: 27 of 1370 assertions pass, across 128 categories, 7 of which are N/A. No category passes outright.\n\nThe number to compare against is 14 of 1334 before $(MAKE) became one word, the diagnostics took the program's name, .FEATURES answered anything, and an abandoned build exited 2. So that work bought thirteen assertions and, more importantly, thirty-six more assertions reached — the suite gets further in before dying.\n\nTwo causes dominate, counted over the 1338 per-test diffs rather than inferred:\n\n  587 show Ronin's Ninja progress line where GNU Make echoes the recipe. That is make-recipe-echo, still the single largest item, and 219 of those diffs contain nothing else at all — the whole failure is one line of the wrong tool's output.\n\n  202 refuse an option the test passed and GNU Make accepted, printing usage. That is make-option-coverage, and it was invisible until $(MAKE) was fixed because the suite never got far enough to pass these options. Ranked: --no-print-directory 45, -I 31, -R 28, -O 20, --debug=* 17, --no-silent 12, --shuffle=* 11, -W 7, --warn-undefined-variables 7, -S 5, --trace 4, -d 4, -t 3, --eval 3, -E 2.\n\nDo not read the residue past those two yet. The pattern established twice now is that one defect is being counted hundreds of times, and both of these are large enough to hide whatever is behind them.\n\nA method note worth keeping. The driver prints only its failure count and never the total, so the pass rate has to be derived by summing the per-category \"(x/y passed)\" lines. That derivation is only trustworthy when the failures it implies equal the driver's own count — here 1370 - 27 = 1343, which is exactly what the driver printed. Check that identity before quoting a rate; the log embeds test output containing the word \"passed\" and a careless parse reads it.\n\nThe kati corpus is not the measure and its counts do not belong in the same sentence as these. It was written by kati's authors to describe kati, so it can only say how closely Ronin tracks what kati already did. This suite was written by people who have never heard of us."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T15:13:21.88733187Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The suite is a semantic oracle, not an output oracle, and its pass rate is not a goal.\n\nRonin narrates a build the way Ninja does, because that is the product. GNU Make's suite compares stdout byte for byte against GNU Make's narration, so a Ronin that passed it would be a Ronin that had stopped looking like Ninja. Chasing the pass rate was chasing a number we have decided not to want, and every figure quoted before this — 27 of 1370, 233 of 1370 — was measuring the wrong thing.\n\nWhat the suite is uniquely good for is the other question: given the same Makefile, did we evaluate it to the same thing, run the same recipes, and finish with the same status. examples/make_upstream.rs answers that by subtraction rather than by matching whole diffs. Each side has its own narration — ours is Ninja's progress line and FAILED: block, Make's is its own name in front of a diagnostic — so both are removed and the residue is compared. A case whose residue matches differs only in how the two tools talk; a case whose residue does not is a defect.\n\nThree classes. `narration` is by design and will never be zero. `capability` is a Make feature that is somebody's node. `semantic` is the number that matters, and it is the only one worth reporting as progress.\n\nBaseline at ronin 3a40f7c: 1132 failing cases — semantic 851, capability 203, narration 78.\n\nTwo details that were wrong on the first attempt and are worth not rediscovering. The name has to be peeled off a line before its shape can be recognised, because the suite writes the name it probed for into its own expectations, so `ronin: [Makefile:3: all] Error 1` is a line GNU Make produced and Ronin never does; reading the shape first leaves it looking like recipe output. And a refused option has to explain the whole diff exclusively, because the build never ran — letting the missing output also count as an evaluation difference filed two hundred cases under the family that means \"we do not know why\".\n\nA diagnostic keeps its body and loses only its name, so two tools saying the same thing cancel while two tools saying different things still show. Deleting the whole line would hide a wrong message behind a right prefix.\n\n`evaluation` at 851 is still too coarse to prioritise from. It is the residue nothing else recognised, and splitting it is the next use of the tool rather than a defect in it: `--explain N` prints the residues that justify the classification, and each recognisable pattern becomes a family."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T15:38:02.918031069Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The reframing paid for itself on the first use, which is the strongest argument for the classifier existing.\n\nReverting the recipe echo moved the suite's own count from 1132 failing cases to 1338 — a loss of 206 by the measure we had abandoned. The classifier says what actually happened: narration 78 to 266, semantic 851 to 869. So the echo was worth eighteen cases, and the other one hundred and eighty-eight were the suite rewarding us for adopting output we do not want. Without the classification that revert would have looked like a regression and would probably not have been made.\n\nOne known imprecision, and those eighteen are it. When Make echoes a recipe line and Ronin prints a progress line instead, the classifier recognises our progress line as narration but cannot recognise Make's recipe line as its counterpart — it has no way to know that an unmatched expected line is the text of the command rather than something the recipe printed. So a handful of cases read as semantic that are really narration.\n\nFixing it needs the recipe text at classification time, which the diff does not carry. The options are to give the classifier the graph, or to accept a bounded overcount. For now it is an overcount of roughly two per cent on the number that matters, stated here rather than silently carried, and semantic counts should be read as an upper bound until it is closed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T16:29:03.893321681Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The harness is now usable as a work queue rather than as a number. Three changes, all of them removing a guess.\n\nIt reads the makefile. The driver leaves a `.run` beside each `.diff` holding the exact command line, so the makefile is found by `-f` rather than inferred from the case's name — which matters, because one script writes several makefiles and the two numberings do not agree. A recipe line is written with variables and echoed with them expanded, so the two are never equal; what survives expansion is the literal text around each `$…`, and a recipe that is nothing but an expansion matches nothing rather than everything. This is the `recipe-echo` family, and it is small — 32 cases — because the suite overwhelmingly writes `@echo`, which Make does not echo either. That is the tool reporting a true thing rather than failing to find something.\n\nNarration is side-aware. A shape counts only on the side that can produce it. `[1/2] ` is Ninja's counter from us and a recipe's own output from Make, and the symmetric version silently deleted the latter — faking agreement on any test that echoes something bracket-shaped.\n\n`evaluation` is being split. It fell from 869 to 537 by naming three shapes that were sitting in it: unsupported-feature at 204 (the evaluator says outright it does not support a construct, and .SECONDEXPANSION is most of it), no-rule-to-make at 201 (a rule that should have matched did not — implicit, vpath, or suffix), no-makefile-found at 27. A named residue no longer also falls into `evaluation`, or the naming would undo itself.\n\nStanding at ronin 2c0d8ff: 1338 failing cases — semantic 765, capability 300, narration 273.\n\nThe two semantic families that are named are the obvious next work, and no-rule-to-make at 201 is the largest single defect in the Make front end by this measure. What remains in `evaluation` is genuinely unrecognised and wants the same treatment: run with --explain, find a shape, name it, and the count moves from unknown to somebody's node."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:00:13.957981629Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed the two buckets left standing after vpath, and both split into named defects rather than staying one number.\n\nno-rule-to-make, 200 cases. 26 of them are cases where GNU Make refuses as well and only the wording differs — narration by our own rule, and make-refusal-wording-is-narration corrects the classifier. The remaining 174 are real, and shaping them by what we refused to build:\n\n  100  an ordinary filename          make-implicit-rule-chaining\n   52  a name still containing $     make-second-expansion\n    9  a name containing a wildcard  make-prerequisite-globbing\n    6  .WAIT                         make-wait-prerequisite\n    4  a name beginning with a dot   make-dot-prefixed-target-nodes\n    3  a -lname library              no node yet, three cases\n\nunsupported-feature, 221 cases, counted by the message rather than by the family:\n\n  123  .SECONDEXPANSION              make-second-expansion\n   33  .DEFAULT                      make-default-target-rule\n   20  .SUFFIXES with prerequisites  make-suffixes-with-prerequisites, and the message is wrong\n   18  $| isn't supported yet        make-order-only-variable\n   16  .SECONDARY                    make-implicit-rule-chaining\n   12  .INTERMEDIATE                 make-implicit-rule-chaining\n   10  .ONESHELL                     make-recipe-one-shell-per-line\n    7  .SILENT .NOTPARALLEL .PRECIOUS .IGNORE .EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES  make-special-target-noise\n\nMethod, since it is the third time it has paid: every one of these came from a four-line makefile run against GNU Make 4.4.1 and against a make-named symlink to Ronin, comparing captured output. Reading the diffs said which shapes to try; the probes said which of them are actually broken. Two of the guesses the diffs suggested were wrong — statipattrules looked like a stem-substitution bug and is a dropped dot-named node, and implicit rule *selection* looked broken and is correct, including match-anything patterns, double-colon patterns, and preferring the rule whose prerequisite exists.\n\nNo count on this node is a prediction of how far the failing total will fall. The populations overlap — 43 of the 52 literal-$ refusals are in scripts that need chaining too — and the vpath estimate was wrong by a factor of twenty for exactly that reason."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:56:55.994615373Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Second source of gate flap seen, and unlike the first it is not settled.\n\nfeatures/grouped_targets cases 15 and 18 swapped families with each other on one run — 15 took `ninja-progress+evaluation` where 18 had it and vice versa — then held steady across four more runs. Both are semantic in either reading, so the class did not move and the gate's contract is intact; only the family list flipped.\n\nTwo candidates and it was not chased: two grouped-target builds racing, or the suite's per-script diff numbering shifting when an earlier case in the same script changes outcome. The exact swap, each taking the other's families, points at the second.\n\nWorth knowing before someone reads a one-case family change in grouped_targets as a result."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T14:21:33.05985455Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "GNU Make's suite is discovery input for language and graph gaps. Exact runner output, scheduling, statuses, directory banners, recursion, and jobserver choreography are never a conformance gate."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-deferred-output-portability
                title "Emit deferred info, warning and error output portably"
                kind @fix
                deps (kati-graph-sink)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T14:20:56.100464105Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by pointing GNU Make's own suite at Ronin, which is exactly the kind of thing the vendored corpus cannot see: no case in it puts info in a recipe and then reads the result back.\n\nWhen evaluation defers output rather than printing it — which is the path the graph sink uses, since the sink sets avoid_io the way the manifest writer does — info_func, warning_func and error_func at func.rs:831, 845 and 862 each build a shell command reading `echo -e \"...\"`, escaped by strutil::echo_escape. That is a bashism. Debian's /bin/sh is dash, whose echo has no -e, so the flag is printed as part of the output.\n\nDemonstrated, and it is not cosmetic. GNU Make answers `make -sf` on a makefile reading `all:;$(info $(MAKE))` with `/usr/bin/make`. Ronin answers `-e /home/.../ronin --make -s`. The value itself is correct — a recipe printf, a warning and an info on its own line all report it cleanly — so the corruption is entirely in how deferred output is spelled.\n\nThis is the blocker for the upstream suite, not merely a defect it found. run_make_tests.pl asks make for `$(MAKE)` and then uses the answer as the path to every subsequent invocation, so a corrupted value makes every later probe run a nonexistent program and the run dies before any test executes.\n\nThe fix is not a smaller flag. dash's echo interprets backslash escapes with no flag at all, and echo_escape exists to feed exactly that interpretation — it turns a newline into a backslash-n so that echo will turn it back. GNU Make's info prints its argument literally and interprets nothing, so the current arrangement is wrong twice over and only looks right under a shell whose echo takes -e and where nothing in the text has a backslash. printf with a %s format is the portable spelling and the one that matches Make's semantics, and it wants the shell escaping strutil::escape_shell already does rather than the echo escaping.\n\nExpect the emitted manifest to change, and that is correct rather than a regression: kati/tools/sinkcmp.py will report differences on any case whose recipe defers output, and those differences are the fix. Byte-identity is the wrong gate here — compare behaviour against GNU Make instead, and check the make-conformance counts move in the right direction.\n\nWorth checking whether the C++ original does the same thing before deciding whether to send it upstream."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:15:42.2258627Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:26:43.282978062Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The earlier echo-to-printf change fixed dash's unsupported `echo -e` but left Makefile data active inside double quotes: `$(info $$HOME)` expanded the user's HOME and backticks executed commands. GNU Make prints both literally. Keep POSIX `printf %b` rather than the originally proposed `%s`, because embedded newlines must be encoded to keep a Ninja command binding on one line; make it safe by escaping backslashes for both interpreters and escaping `$`, backticks, and quotes for the shell. Route each complete info/warning/error message, including its Makefile location, through one encoder. Verified with a shell-executed unit test, a direct Ronin-vs-GNU probe, and the 296-file direct-vs-emitted graph corpus. Kati commit: b0dc8502e54cbb2520c195297e61a04d217eb401."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:31:45.900095641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Validation exposed a separate upstream-harness reproducibility defect. scripts/check-make-upstream.sh runs in the persistent reference checkout; leftover outputs such as tests/foo.q and tests/xxx.o changed 28 case rows between the recorded inventory and a clean detached 4.4.1 worktree. Clean-run counts were unclassified 606, compiler 74, interface 110, narration 559. This does not alter the deferred-output fix or the build-intent gate; record it as a separate harness-isolation node rather than mixing it into this compiler commit."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:16:05.136970432Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-variable-is-one-word
                title "Expose MAKE as one compiler-recognizable invocation path"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-subninja-recursion)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T17:11:02.581433822Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "One defect, and it is worth roughly the whole upstream score.\n\nGNU Make's suite scored Ronin at 65 assertions of 543, eleven per cent. That number is almost meaningless as a measure of Make support: 243 of 261 categories died before a single assertion passed, and they died for one reason. The driver asks make for $(MAKE), adopts the answer as the path for every later invocation, and execs it — as a path, not through a shell. GNU Make answers /usr/bin/make, one word. Ronin answers three: the executable, --make, and whatever switches propagate. Every exec then fails with \"Cannot execute /home/.../ronin --make -s: No such file or directory\", before the test runs.\n\nConfirmed rather than inferred: functions/if produces byte-identical output to GNU Make when its makefile is run by hand, in both the symlinked and the explicit-flag form, and still logs nothing under the driver. Running one category with -verbose prints the ENOENT.\n\nThe multi-word MAKE was a deliberate choice by make-mode-cli and the reasoning holds for what it was about: switches cannot go in MAKEFLAGS because that variable is carrying the jobserver budget a child draws on, and rewriting it would cost the child the budget. What it did not weigh is that MAKE is not only read by recursive makefiles, which tolerate several words, but by anything treating it as the make program's location — of which upstream's own suite is the most demanding example.\n\nThe fix that matches GNU Make is for MAKE to name a single executable that already selects Make mode by its name, so no flag has to ride along. When the invocation arrived through a make-named path, that path is the obvious answer, and the current code discards it: recursive_command uses current_exe, which resolves the symlink back to a ronin-named binary and therefore needs --make to undo the resolution. Reporting the invoked path instead would make recursion re-enter by name exactly as GNU Make does. The case with no such path — someone running `ronin --make` directly — still needs an answer, and shipping a make-named symlink beside the binary is the obvious one.\n\nDo this before quoting any upstream number. Until it is fixed the suite measures one defect 243 times, and the eleven per cent will move a long way for reasons that have nothing to do with the Make language."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:48:01.459303702Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:48:37.228282836Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The compatibility defect was delivered by bf4d549951d73ed086c3900573b6a872c3306b7f, which carries this node's nplan-id under the retired `done:` type, and was tightened by b60d5c6da881755b960090d0dc5e9ee949cab7d7 so Make mode is reached only through a make-named path. The node itself omitted a change kind; it is explicitly `fix` because a multi-word MAKE breaks consumers that exec it as a program path. Current make_variable_is_invoked_path and recursive_make_compiles_as_subninja tests pass, and both 1,349-case upstream discovery runs completed through the one-word path. Switches and assignments remain interface inputs in MAKEFLAGS; recursive work compiles as semantic subninja rather than spawning a Make executor."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T22:48:37.306379932Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

