            {
                id make-fatal-diagnostic-shape
                title "Identify fatal Makefile evaluation diagnostics as compiler errors"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-diagnostic-prefix)
                children (
                    {
                        id make-diagnostic-identity
                        title "Route Kati diagnostics through the evaluation session's source identity"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T12:46:36.8908699Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "The line between a GNU Make diagnostic and kati refusing to do something is already written in the source, and it is the `*** ` that opens the message.\n\nEvery bare error! whose text starts with `*** ` has a GNU Make counterpart: implicit output on a phony target (dep.rs), No rule to make target twice over (exec.rs), the recipe-failure line (exec.rs), cannot assign to a readonly variable (var.rs). Every one that does not is kati telling the caller it will not do something and has no counterpart to be shaped against: \"$(value X) is not implemented yet\", \"overriding automatic variable is not implemented yet\", \"appending to $X is not supported\", \"Cannot find the depfile\" (ninja.rs), \"Unknown flag in findleaves.py\" (find.rs), \"body failed\" (eval.rs). Those keep raising bare and keep printing bare.\n\nSo the conversion is mechanical rather than a judgement per site: a `*** ` message goes through error_loc! with no location, which reaches color_error_log and picks up the program name; anything else is left alone.\n\nOne exception, established by running GNU Make rather than by reading it. The recipe-failure line is `make: *** [fail.mk:2: all] Error 3` — it takes the prefix like the others but is not a fatal it dies on, so it must not take the Stop. suffix that make-diagnostic-stop adds in color_error_log. It therefore keeps raising bare and takes the prefix at the site."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T12:46:51.337695077Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T13:15:36.643044908Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Measured together with make-diagnostic-stop and make-abandon-exit-status, because none of the three can move the corpus alone: the same cases carry all three differences, so fixing one leaves the case differing on the other two and the count does not budge. Doing all three took the corpus from 227 identical of 386 to 293, and from 159 differing to 93.\n\nThe families the corpus names moved as follows. identity-prefix 43 to 9, stop-suffix 84 to 18, exit-status 98 to 32.\n\nWhat is left in identity-prefix is the residue the classification decision predicted: sites raising through the bare error! macro that are kati refusing rather than Make failing, and one that is Make failing but cannot reach the flags — var.rs assign takes an Interner rather than a Context, and readonly variables are a kati extension GNU Make has no counterpart to, so the signature was left alone rather than widened for a message nothing can measure against.\n\nOne thing the work turned up that was not visible from the corpus. Ronin's Make mode does not use kati's exec path at all: \"no rule to make target\" and the recipe-failure line come from Ronin's own scheduler with Ninja's wording. So the kati changes here are measured by the corpus, which runs rkati, and Ronin's Make mode needed its own fix for the same defect. Anything that reasons about Make-mode output from the corpus alone is reasoning about the wrong binary."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-08T14:21:32.540980993Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Only evaluation-time compiler identity and Makefile source locations belong here. Stop. suffixes, recursive level prefixes, and GNU Make runner wording are excluded."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T17:16:08.181512296Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T18:37:48.297126886Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The mechanism landed with make-diagnostic-prefix; what is left is applying it. Flags::program_name exists, color_error_log uses it when there is no location, and rkati fills it from argv[0]. Only the \"No targets\" message actually goes through that path.\n\nThe corpus measures what remains: identity-prefix 43, stop-suffix 84, out of 159 differing cases. Both families have one cause. Almost every fatal error in the evaluator is raised through the bare `error!` macro, which expands to anyhow::bail! and has no session argument, so it never reaches color_error_log and cannot ask for a name. There are roughly twenty such sites — eval.rs, dep.rs, find.rs, exec.rs, var.rs, ninja.rs.\n\nThe work is to convert each site that raises a GNU Make diagnostic to the session-aware path, and to end it as GNU Make ends it. GNU Make's fatal() is one function with two shapes: with a location it writes \"file:line: *** msg.  Stop.\", without one \"program: *** msg.  Stop.\". The \"  Stop.\" is on both, which means it belongs in color_error_log rather than at each site; the prefix is the only part that differs.\n\nNot every bare error! is a Make diagnostic — \"$(value X) is not implemented yet\" and friends are kati telling the caller it will not do something, and GNU Make has no counterpart to shape them against. Those should keep raising bare, and deciding which is which is most of the judgement in this node.\n\nMeasure it the same way afterwards: scripts/check-make-conformance.sh reports the families by name, so the two counts either fall or the change did not do what it claims."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-option-parity
                title "Implement graph-affecting GNU Make option inputs"
                kind @feat
                children (
                    {
                        id make-option-negations
                        title "Answer the negating spellings of switches Make mode already has"
                        priority 0
                        kind @feat
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T14:07:49.200538015Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "62 failing tests, the largest group here and the cheapest: --no-print-directory 45, --no-silent 12, -S 5.\n\nAll three negate a switch Make mode already has, so none needs new machinery — only the recognition that GNU Make's switches come in pairs and that the later spelling wins. --no-print-directory withdraws -w, --no-silent withdraws -s, -S withdraws -k.\n\nTwo things to get right rather than guess. The negation has to survive MAKEFLAGS into a sub-make, which means `propagated` and `adopt_inherited` have to carry it rather than only carrying the positive form — a tree where the parent said --no-print-directory and the children announce their directories anyway is the defect these tests are looking for. And -w is implied by -C, so --no-print-directory has to withdraw the implication as well as the request; `announcing` already encodes that relationship for -s and is where the answer goes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T20:05:13.207391861Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-06T20:19:59.118075842Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and every value in the tests was read off GNU Make 4.4.1 rather than reasoned about: a makefile printing `$(MAKEFLAGS)`, run once per spelling. Three of the answers would not have been guessed.\n\n`-S` is a letter and travels in the letter group, so `make -k -S` hands a child `MAKEFLAGS=S` with no `k` left to argue with. `--no-print-directory` and `--no-silent` have no letter and follow the group: `make -k --no-print-directory` gives `k --no-print-directory`. And `-C .` alone gives an empty MAKEFLAGS, so the announcement it implies is not propagated as a flag — a child gets the implication from its own `-C` or not at all.\n\nThe shape needed a second bitset rather than clearing the first. Refusing a switch is not the same state as never having asked for it, because `-w` is asked for by `-C` as well as by name and GNU Make's help is explicit — \"Turn off -w, even if it was turned on implicitly\". So `add` and `withdraw` each clear the other's bit, which is also what makes the last spelling win in both directions.\n\nAll fourteen shapes now answer as GNU Make does, checked side by side including the Entering/Leaving pairs, with only the progress line differing.\n\nThe measurement, and it is the pattern this suite keeps producing rather than a regression. option-refused 203 to 147, capability 316 to 265, and three tests now pass the suite outright. Semantic went 675 to 720 — every case that moved was capability before, 45 to semantic and 6 to narration, and nothing that was already narration or semantic got worse. The refusal had been standing in front of a difference; taking it away is what makes the difference countable.\n\nWhat is behind it, by script: variables/MAKEFLAGS 21, features/jobserver 6, options/print-directory 5, options/dash-s 4, options/dash-C 3. By family it is 35 evaluation, which is the unnamed bucket and wants the same --explain treatment the others got. Read that before assuming MAKEFLAGS content is one defect: makeflags-content is only 4 of them."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T17:16:10.019054316Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

