                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "202 of the 1338 failing tests in GNU Make's suite refuse an option the test passed and GNU Make accepted, printing usage and abandoning. Ranked by failing tests, from the diffs rather than from the logs — the logs are per-category and overwritten, so they undercount badly, which is what made this look like eight cases on first inspection instead of two hundred:\n\n  --no-print-directory 45, -I 31, -R 28, -O 20, --debug=* 17, --no-silent 12,\n  --shuffle=* 11, -W 7, --warn-undefined-variables 7, -S 5, --trace 4, -d 4,\n  -t 3, --eval 3, -E 2.\n\nThis was invisible before `$(MAKE)` became one word. The suite never got far enough to pass these options at all, so the whole family was hidden behind a defect counted 243 times. It is the second thing that has behaved that way and it will not be the last: read the residue only after the thing in front of it is gone.\n\nChildren are ranked by how many failing tests each would reach, so the frontier offers the biggest first. That ranking is a starting order and not a claim about difficulty — `--no-print-directory` leads on count and is nearly free, while `-O` is worth twenty and needs output grouping in the scheduler.\n\nDepends on make-recipe-echo-line for measurement rather than for mechanism. An option can be implemented at any time, but until the progress line is gone every one of these diffs also contains it, so a test fixed here will still fail and nobody can tell whether the option work was right.\n\n`make-option-coverage` is Done and is not this: it took the options whose machinery already existed. These are the ones that need machinery."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T14:21:32.856436054Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "This container now covers only graph-affecting compiler inputs. The authoritative complete interface table and every accepted no-op are owned by make-interface-surface."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-one-shell-per-line
                title "Share one shell across a recipe under .ONESHELL"
                priority 3
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:05:44.589481378Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:14:02.334106954Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The defect this node described does not exist, and the one next to it did. Retitled to match what was actually wrong.\n\nIt said a `cd` persists across the `&&`-joined script because Make runs each line in its own shell and we run one. That is not what happens: gen_shell_script already wraps every line of a multi-line recipe in `( )`, so the isolation is there. Seven shapes checked against 4.4.1 and all matched before any change — `cd`, a shell variable, `exit`, `set -x`, a failing line stopping the rest, and a syntax error in a later line still letting the earlier one run.\n\nWhat was missing is the opposite. `.ONESHELL` asks for the lines to share a shell and was being declined, so a Makefile that uses it got the isolation it had explicitly turned off. Under it: a `cd` carries, a variable set in one line is visible in the next, and a failing line does not stop the rest — only the script's final status counts.\n\nSo the implementation is three conditions on the flag rather than new machinery: no subshell, lines separated by a newline instead of ` && `, and no `; true` for `-` since the intermediate statuses stop mattering.\n\nTen cases moved, 6 capability to semantic and 4 to narration.\n\nLeft: the `-` prefix and `@` under `.ONESHELL` apply to the first line only in GNU Make, which is not modelled — every line still carries its own. It did not show up in any shape probed, because `@` is narration here and `-` stops mattering once the intermediate statuses do."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:17:03.02829473Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-dry-run
                title "Map Make dry-run spellings onto Ninja dry-run execution"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T22:10:10.717750281Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T22:28:07.061979516Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Done for the `+` exception, and it moved nothing in GNU Make's suite — no case there exercises `-n` with a `+` line. It was a real defect regardless: `make -n` silently did nothing where GNU Make runs the line, so a Makefile using `+$(MAKE) -C sub` to recurse under `-n` saw an empty answer.\n\nProbed against 4.4.1 first, which is what set the shape:\n\n  plain recipe        neither tool runs it\n  `@` prefixed        both still print it — `-n` overrides `@`\n  `+` prefixed        GNU runs it, we did not\n  mixed lines         GNU runs ONLY the `+` line and prints all three\n\nThat last one ruled out the cheap version. Running the whole recipe when any line is `+` would have created the two files GNU leaves alone, and a wrong build under the one flag whose entire purpose is to touch nothing is the worst place to take that shortcut.\n\nSo the `+` subset is assembled as its own script beside the full one, in the same pass and with the same joining, and carried as a rule binding named `dryrun_command`. Under `-n` an edge that has one runs it instead of being skipped; every other edge is skipped as before. A Ninja manifest never binds the name, so `graph.names().lookup` answers None and nothing changes for Ninja — no plumbing through BuildOptions was needed.\n\nTwo things left, both separate:\n\n`$(MAKE)` gets the same exception in GNU Make, and this does not give it. Make decides by scanning the *unexpanded* recipe line for `$(MAKE)` or `${MAKE}`; kati has already expanded the recipe by the time it has lines, so the information is gone. Verified the distinction is real rather than incidental: `MAKE_ALIAS = $(MAKE)` then `$(MAKE_ALIAS) --version` is printed and NOT run by GNU Make, so it is the reference that matters and not the value. Doing it properly means walking the recipe Value for a VarRef to the MAKE symbol before expansion.\n\nWhat `-n` prints is still ours rather than Make's: `/bin/sh -c \"(a ) && (b )\"` where Make prints each line as written. That is make-recipe-one-shell-per-line, and this node deliberately left it alone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T14:21:32.376052063Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Dry-run spellings are interface controls mapped onto the ordinary Ninja dry-run path. Plus-prefixed recursive Make lines are compiler inputs for subninja composition, not an exception that launches a child Make during dry-run."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:12:46.993204181Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Done, and it reverses what ed9f7ca landed. That commit contradicted this node's own 2026-08-08 decision: DRY_RUN_COMMAND in src/build/command.rs was an executor-side Make exception that launched a child Make during -n. It is gone — the const, CommandSpec::dry_run_command, the spawn branch in src/build.rs, the sink binding in src/make/sink.rs, and kati's dry_run_command / residual_dry_run_command / dry_buf that fed it. Dry run is now Ninja's dry run for every graph: print the commands, run none of them.\n\nProbed against 4.4.1 first, and the probes are what settled the shape.\n\n  `+` line under -n        GNU runs it; `@` is overridden and it prints anyway\n  -n --just-print          all four spellings set the same MAKEFLAGS letter,\n  --dry-run --recon        `n`, and it descends into the child\n  recursive -n             GNU starts the child process, the child inherits `n`\n                           through MAKEFLAGS, prints its own recipe and writes\n                           nothing; net filesystem effect of the whole tree is\n                           nothing\n\nThat last one is the whole argument. GNU Make starts a child under -n because starting it is the only way it can learn what the child would do. Ronin compiled the child already — bcd200e's subninja composition — so `-n` walks a graph that has the child's edges in it and prints them without starting anything. Verified: a parent/sub pair under `ronin -n` prints both edges and writes no file, where the same tree under `make -n` prints both and also writes no file. The behaviour GNU needs a process for, we get from the graph, which is what \"compile it, don't become it\" means here.\n\nThe `+` prefix on a line that is not Make loses its GNU effect, and that is the decision rather than an oversight: `+echo plus > plus` writes under `make -n` and writes nothing under `ronin -n`. Recorded as tests/make/dry-run-skips-a-plus-line and listed discovery-only, beside dry-run-skips-a-make-reference-line; recursive-dry-run-writes-nothing is the same question where the two tools agree and it gates.\n\nThe decided-but-never-built piece is built. GNU Make classifies a recipe line recursive from its unexpanded text, and 4.4.1 settles that it is the reference and not the value: with `MAKE_ALIAS = $(MAKE)`, `$(MAKE) --version` and `${MAKE} --version` both wrote their files under -n and `$(MAKE_ALIAS) --version` did not; `$(info $(MAKE))` did; `$(MAKE:x=y)` and `$($(V))` with `V = MAKE` did not. Walking the unexpanded recipe Value for a SymRef naming MAKE answers exactly that set — both spellings parse to the same SymRef, and every declined form parses to something else — so the walk is GNU's textual scan structurally rather than by imitation. The one divergence found is `$${MAKE}`, which GNU matches by plain substring search inside an escaped literal; nothing observable follows from it here.\n\nWhere the classification earns its place is not dry run at all, and that took a wrong turn to find. My first attempt refused every classified line whose invocation could not be lifted out — `test -d sub && $(MAKE) -C sub` — and it broke feature-makelevel-increments, which had been quietly working by spawning a nested Ronin. Refusing a shape that builds correctly trades a working build for a principle; the guard was wrong at that width. Narrowed to the invariant the recipe splitter already claimed for itself: \"a sink must never receive a plausible-looking prefix while another recursive line remains hidden in the discarded executor script\". Such a line carries no recursive_make, so a recipe that split its other lines into child graphs would have read this one as ordinary residual work and left it to start a nested Make beside them. A recipe holding one is now left whole. A recipe that only holds one is unchanged — still one edge, still a nested Make, and that gap belongs to whoever widens composition next.\n\nVerified: full workspace tests all-targets, strict all-feature all-target Clippy, fmt --check, scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh (272 graphs compared, one pre-existing classified disagreement), the 268-case build-intent gate, nplan check, lint and spec validate clean, coverage still 218/218 with no under-verified rule. kati change is 32c0e8e on kati's nplan/make-recipe-dry-run."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:12:53.216236028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-vpath-search
                title "Search vpath and VPATH directories for a prerequisite"
                priority 0
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T16:44:04.328704769Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The largest single defect in the Make front end: 117 of the suite's 1338 failing cases, across thirteen makefiles and thirteen scripts.\n\nIt is not a search that goes wrong. `vpath` and `VPATH` appear nowhere in the evaluator at all — grep the whole of src-rs and there are no hits — so the directive is not a directive as far as the parser is concerned, and a line beginning `vpath ` is read as a rule without a colon. What the suite sees is `*** missing separator.  Stop.`, and every target that would have been found through the search then reports having no rule.\n\nThat is why it was hard to see in the family counts. One cause splits across two families — parse-failure for the makefiles that use the directive form, no-rule-to-make for the ones that set the variable — and neither number looks like 117 on its own. It took a minimal probe to find, which is the argument for keeping probes minimal:\n\n  vpath %.bar sub\n  %.tsk: %.bar\n  \t@echo made $@ from $<\n  all: hello.tsk\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 says `made hello.tsk from sub/hello.bar`; Ronin says `Makefile:1: *** missing separator.  Stop.`\n\nTwo halves to implement and they are not the same size. `VPATH` is a variable holding a colon or space separated list of directories searched for any prerequisite. `vpath` is a directive with three forms: `vpath pattern dirs` adds a search path for names matching the pattern, `vpath pattern` clears that pattern's, and bare `vpath` clears all of them. Both need the search to happen where a prerequisite is resolved to a file, and the resolved path — not the name as written — is what `$<` and `$^` must expand to, which is the part a naive implementation gets wrong.\n\nWorth knowing before starting: the pattern and suffix rule machinery around it already works. A minimal `%.tsk: %.bar` pattern rule and a `.bar.tsk` suffix rule both build correctly, verified against 4.4.1. So this is an addition to prerequisite resolution rather than a repair of rule matching, and the 201 no-rule-to-make cases should be re-read after it lands rather than assumed to be one thing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T16:56:50.577719384Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:12:11.860008921Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented, and the estimate on this node was wrong in a way worth recording.\n\nI claimed 117 cases. It moved six: semantic 710 to 704, parse-failure 41 to 35. The 117 counted makefiles that *mention* vpath or VPATH, not cases whose only problem was vpath, and those makefiles hit other gaps as well — GPATH, the `vpath+` form, and unsupported constructs the evaluator declines out loud. Counting the mentions of a feature is not counting the cases it blocks, and I will not make that inference again without checking a sample.\n\nThe feature itself is right, established against GNU Make 4.4.1 on four shapes: the directive, the variable, a colon-separated list with a miss before the hit, and the clearing form where both tools then refuse. features/vpath.diff and .2 are now classified narration — they differ from GNU Make only in the progress line we chose to print, which is as passed as a case can be under the narration rule.\n\nThree things about the implementation worth knowing.\n\nThe rewrite happens to the node's inputs, not only at the point of asking whether a file exists. The inputs are what `$<` and `$^` expand to and therefore what the recipe is handed, so a search that found the file and passed on the name as written would build with a path that is not there. That is the part a naive implementation gets wrong and it is why `resolve_vpaths` exists beside `exists`.\n\nResolution and interning are separate passes because the session is needed immutably to find the file and mutably to name the result. Symtab has no non-interning lookup, so there is no way to do both at once.\n\nOrder is the semantics, which is why the paths are a list of pairs rather than a map: the first pattern matching a name decides which directories are searched, a repeated pattern extends rather than replaces, and `VPATH` is consulted only for a name no pattern matched."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:17:05.231528665Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-suffixes-with-prerequisites
                title "Stop warning about .SUFFIXES declarations that work"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T16:44:17.361364795Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Twenty cases fail on a warning about something that works.\n\ndep.rs:518 warns \"kati doesn't support .SUFFIXES with prerequisites\" whenever `.SUFFIXES:` is given any, which is the ordinary way to declare a suffix. The build then proceeds and produces exactly what GNU Make produces — verified against 4.4.1 on both a plain `.bar.tsk` suffix rule and one preceded by `.SUFFIXES:` to clear the defaults, where the recipe ran and the output matched. The only difference is the warning itself.\n\nSo the message is wrong on its own terms rather than merely noisy: the support it denies is present. Either the warning goes, or it is narrowed to whatever case actually is unsupported — and establishing which that is, if any, is the work here. The bare `.SUFFIXES:` form with no prerequisites is handled a few lines above and clears the list, so the branch that warns is the one that should be doing the declaring.\n\nSmall, and worth doing early for a reason out of proportion to its size: these twenty are counted as `unsupported-feature`, so the capability number is overstated by that much and the family reads as a bigger gap than it is."
                    }

