            {
                id make-implicit-rule-chaining
                title "Chain implicit rules through intermediate files"
                priority 0
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:58:41.883742508Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The largest real gap left in Make mode, and it is one feature rather than a family: an implicit rule whose prerequisite does not exist but could itself be made by another implicit rule. GNU Make calls this step 5c of the Implicit Rule Search Algorithm and creates the missing prerequisite as an intermediate file; we stop and refuse.\n\nEstablished by probe against GNU Make 4.4.1 rather than by reading, with hello.f the only file present and -r to silence the built-in rules:\n\n  all: hello.x\n  %.x: %.o ; $(info from o)\n  %.o: %.f ; $(info from f)\n\nGNU says `from f`, `from o`, `Nothing to be done for 'all'.` Ronin says `'hello.x', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`. Every non-chaining shape in the same probe set matches: match-anything `%:` patterns, suffixed `%.o:` patterns, double-colon patterns, and choosing the `%.f` rule over the `%.c` rule because hello.f exists and hello.c does not. So rule selection is right and only the recursion is absent.\n\nSize, counted from the per-case diffs and not from mentions — the lesson make-vpath-search paid for. 174 of the suite's failing cases refuse a target GNU Make built. Of those, 100 name an ordinary file, and the three makefiles read in full so far are all chains: features/patternrules case 4 wants `test.x` from `%.x:`, patternrules.1 wants `foo.out` from `%.out: %` from `%: %.in`, patternrules.2 wants `.o` from `.c` from `.y`. The 100 spread over features/implicit_search 43, features/patternrules 31, and nine other scripts. Beyond that, targets/INTERMEDIATE 12, targets/SECONDARY 16 and targets/NOTINTERMEDIATE 8 are counted as capability today and are all about what happens to a chained intermediate once it has been made, so they can only be reached from here.\n\nDo not turn that into a single predicted number. The honest claim is that the 100 are the population this can move and that some of them will turn out to need something else; measure after.\n\nNothing to repair first: grep for `intermediate` or `chain` across kati/src-rs/dep.rs returns one hit, the string \".INTERMEDIATE\" in the list of special targets it declines. The machinery has never existed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:18:33.011028888Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T21:24:48.860635896Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-dot-prefixed-target-nodes
                title "Build a target whose name begins with a dot"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:58:53.777850841Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Ten cases, and the fix is one predicate. Any target whose name begins with a single dot is silently dropped on the way into the build graph, so a prerequisite naming one becomes an input nothing produces and Ronin refuses it.\n\nkati/src-rs/ninja.rs:533, in sink_node: `if is_special_target(&session, &node.output) { return Ok(None) }`. is_special_target at dep.rs:1413 is a shape test — starts with `.` and not with `..` — so it catches `.1` and `.WAIT` along with `.PHONY`. The node is built and then thrown away, which is why the diagnostic blames the parent rather than the missing rule.\n\nProbed against GNU Make 4.4.1. All three of these build for GNU and are refused by us:\n\n  all: .1 ; @echo built $@ from $^\n  .1: ; @echo made .1              # explicit rule with a recipe\n  .1:                              # explicit rule, empty\n  .PHONY: .1                       # declared phony, no rule\n\nThe phony declaration is registered correctly — handle_special_targets does insert `.1` into the phony set — so this is entirely the sink filter, not a lookup failure.\n\nThe shape test is right in its other two uses and must stay there. dep.rs:839 uses it to keep a dot-named rule from becoming the default goal, which is GNU Make's documented behaviour, and dep.rs:565 and :582 use it to keep specials out of --gen_all_targets. Only the sink needs the narrower question, which is whether the name is one of the built-in special targets kati has already consumed: .PHONY, .SUFFIXES, .KATI_RESTAT and the declined list at dep.rs:529, plus the .KATI_* names.\n\nWatch the suffix rules when narrowing it. `.c.o` passes the shape test today and would stop being filtered under a name list, so check whether populate_explicit_rule leaving the suffix rule in self.rules can put one in front of the sink.\n\nWhere the ten are: features/statipattrules and variables/automatic refuse `.1` and `.x` produced by an empty static-pattern stem, targets/WAIT refuses `.WAIT` in six. The WAIT cases declare `.WAIT:` themselves — the suite says \"this is just here so we don't fail with older versions of make\" — so several of them come back from this alone, but the undeclared form needs make-wait-prerequisite."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:03:45.521405477Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T20:01:04.458125837Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
