            {
                id make-second-expansion
                title "Expand prerequisites a second time under .SECONDEXPANSION"
                priority 1
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:59:09.273978944Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The largest single declined feature: 123 of the suite's failing cases carry `kati doesn't support .SECONDEXPANSION`, and a further 52 refuse a prerequisite whose name still contains a `$` — the unexpanded text taken literally as a filename. They are the same defect counted from both ends, concentrated in features/se_explicit, features/se_implicit, features/se_statpat and features/statipattrules.\n\nProbed against 4.4.1:\n\n  .SECONDEXPANSION:\n  PRE = dep\n  all: $$(PRE) ; @echo got $^\n  dep: ; @echo made dep\n\nGNU says `made dep`, `got dep`. Ronin warns and then refuses `'$(PRE)', needed by 'all'`.\n\nThe declining is at dep.rs:529, in the list of built-in targets handle_special_targets warns about and otherwise ignores. Implementing it means keeping the prerequisite list of every rule in its once-expanded form and expanding it again when the rule is used, with $@ and the stem in scope — which is the point of the feature and also why it cannot be done at parse time.\n\nRanked below make-implicit-rule-chaining despite the larger count, because it is a much bigger change and because the se_* scripts fail on chaining as well: 43 of the 52 literal-`$` refusals are in files that also need step 5c, so the two populations overlap and this one will not move all 175."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T21:04:16.989425862Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T21:15:34.566128367Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Explicit rules only, and the boundary was found by the first attempt going wrong rather than by planning.\n\nThe mechanism is cheaper than it looks. GNU Make's first expansion already turns `$$(PRE)` into `$(PRE)` — nothing has to be kept unexpanded through the parser. So the rule keeps the once-expanded prerequisite text, and dep.rs expands that text again at fill time with `@` bound as a scoped simple variable, then reads the result as prerequisites. `$@` is unbound during dep building because the AutoCommandVar table is not registered until CommandEvaluator::new, so a scoped binding is enough and none of that machinery is needed.\n\n`.SECONDEXPANSION` applies only below its own declaration, so the flag lives on the Evaluator and is read as each rule is evaluated, not gathered afterwards like the other special targets.\n\nWhere it stopped: deferring a pattern rule's prerequisites leaves it with none, and a pattern rule with no prerequisites matches anything. The probe built `foo.o` out of nothing where GNU Make refuses — a wrong build in place of a refusal, which is worse than the gap. So rule.rs defers only when output_patterns is empty, and make-second-expansion-patterns has the rest.\n\nMeasured: 14 cases genuinely fixed — 10 capability to narration, 3 semantic to narration, 1 now passing the suite outright. 32 more moved capability to semantic, which is the warning no longer standing in front of them; most of that is the pattern half.\n\nFour shapes verified against 4.4.1: `$$(VAR)`, `$$@.dep`, an order-only `| $$(VAR)`, and a rule written above the declaration correctly not getting it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:17:08.792369908Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-special-target-noise
                title "Stop denying support for special targets that already behave"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:59:17.119777603Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The same shape as make-suffixes-with-prerequisites, found by probing the rest of the declined list: we warn that we do not support special targets whose behaviour we already produce. The warning is the entire difference, and it inflates the capability count.\n\nProbed against GNU Make 4.4.1:\n\n  .SILENT: / all: ; echo quiet          GNU prints `quiet`; we print the warning and then `quiet`.\n  .PRECIOUS: out / out: ; @echo > $@    GNU prints nothing; we print the warning and then nothing.\n\nSeven cases across .SILENT 2, .NOTPARALLEL 2, .EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES 1, .PRECIOUS 1, .IGNORE 1. Establish for each whether the behaviour actually matches before deleting its warning — .IGNORE and .NOTPARALLEL are the two that could plausibly be real, since one changes how a failing recipe is treated and the other constrains the scheduler, and a warning that is merely early is better than silence that is wrong.\n\nDo not fold in .DEFAULT, .INTERMEDIATE, .SECONDARY, .SECONDEXPANSION or .ONESHELL. Those five are genuinely unimplemented and have nodes of their own.\n\nSecond thing to fix here, cheaper still: the message says `kati doesn't support .SILENT` in a program called ronin. Every one of the 216 declining lines in the suite names the wrong product. make-diagnostic-identity settled the shape for fatal diagnostics and left the warnings alone; this is the rest of it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T20:21:38.785688554Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T20:36:02.699997768Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Three of the six turned out to be noise, not seven as this node estimated. Each was probed against GNU Make 4.4.1 rather than assumed:\n\n  .SILENT               no-op here, we never echo a recipe            warning removed\n  .PRECIOUS             neither tool deletes a target whose recipe\n                        failed; all four combinations of the\n                        declaration leave the partial file            warning removed\n  .LOW_RESOLUTION_TIME  obsolete and ignored in 4.x too               warning removed\n  .IGNORE               we abandon where GNU Make continues           kept\n  .NOTPARALLEL          ignored under -j                              kept\n  .EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES recipe sees nothing                           kept\n\nSo half of what this node called noise is a real gap wearing a warning, which is the correct outcome for a node whose whole job was to establish which was which. .IGNORE, .NOTPARALLEL and .EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES want nodes of their own.\n\nThe naming half is done for every message the Make front end can emit: the text takes Flags::program_name rather than the literal `kati`, so a program called ronin says so. rkati's own Entering/Leaving line in main.rs still says `kati` and is left alone — that is rkati's binary rather than the product.\n\nMeasured: four cases moved, all from capability. Two to narration and two to semantic.\n\nWorth passing on: both of the semantic ones are a classifier gap rather than a Ronin defect. targets/SILENT declares `.SILENT: b` on `a b: ; echo $@`, so GNU Make echoes `echo a` and not `echo b`; we echo neither, by the narration decision. The residue is therefore a recipe echo, which `recipe-echo` exists to name, and it landed in `evaluation` instead — the family reads the makefile to recognise the echo and does not see through `$@`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:17:13.257325738Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-default-target-rule
                title "Fall back to .DEFAULT for a target with no rule"
                priority 2
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:59:25.834455191Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "33 cases. `.DEFAULT` supplies the recipe for any target that has no rule of its own and is not made by an implicit rule. We decline it at dep.rs:529 and then refuse the target.\n\n  all: dep\n  .DEFAULT: ; @echo default made $@\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 prints `default made dep` and exits 0. Ronin warns, then `'dep', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`, exit 2.\n\nIt belongs to the same population as make-implicit-rule-chaining — both are steps in what GNU Make does before giving up on a target — and .DEFAULT is the last of them, consulted only after the implicit search has failed. Worth doing after chaining so the two are not fighting over the same fallback path, and so that a target reached by a chain does not accidentally take .DEFAULT's recipe."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T20:56:02.797047373Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T21:03:45.262091784Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented as Make's step 7, after the implicit search and only for a target with no rule of its own that is not already on disk. Five shapes checked against 4.4.1 and all five now match semantically:\n\n  a target with no rule                 takes it\n  a rule with no recipe                 does not — Make says Nothing to be done\n  a file that exists                    does not\n  a later bare `.DEFAULT:`              cancels the earlier one\n  a failed implicit search              falls through to it\n\nThe cancelling form is why the rule is read as \"the last `.DEFAULT` that has a recipe\" rather than as a merge.\n\n26 cases moved: 13 to narration, 12 from semantic to capability, 1 the other way. The twelve are the pattern this suite keeps producing — the build now gets past `.DEFAULT` and reaches the next declined feature, so an unexplained difference becomes a named one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T17:17:15.155683557Z
