            {
                id make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent
                title "Decide what a sub-make with its own -j publishes to its children"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:48:02.75524123Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:56:30.135808563Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "A build that declines an inherited job budget serves its own, sized to the `-j` it was given, and publishes that to everything below it. Both front ends. In Make mode it also says so, in GNU Make's words.\n\nThe rule in one line: a build serves a jobserver unless it is drawing on one. Today's rule is \"unless one exists\", which is why `$(MAKE) -j4` under a parent jobserver publishes nothing at all and the whole subtree below it runs at -j1.\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1, verified by probe rather than recalled:\n\n  make[1]: warning: -j3 forced in submake: resetting jobserver mode.\n\nand the sub-make then serves a fresh fifo sized to 3, which the level below it draws on. It warns even when the forced N equals the parent's N — there is no same-N exemption. `-j` unbounded warns as `-j0 forced` and publishes ` -j` with no auth. `-j1` warns and publishes no auth either. Without a parent jobserver, an explicit `-j` in a sub-make serves normally and says nothing.\n\nThis is not a diagnostic we can decline: GNU's own suite asserts the exact line four times in tests/scripts/features/jobserver, once specifically for its ordering against `Entering directory`.\n\nNinja mode gets the same budget rule and no warning. Upstream Ninja's `-j` sets `disable_jobserver_client` and it says nothing, because upstream Ninja has no jobserver server to reset; Ronin does, and `ronin -jN` at the top of a tree already serves N. The parent's presence suppressing that was the accident, not the rule.\n\nThe price is over-subscription: the machine now runs the parent's budget plus N. That is exactly what the user asked for by typing `-jN` on a sub-make, and it is what GNU's warning exists to announce.\n\nRejected, keep the inherited auth and pass it on unchanged. This is what the comment in `normalize_runtime_options` claims already happens — \"the commands still reach the parent's jobserver the way every other child does\" — and in Make mode it is false, because `flag_environment` rewrites MAKEFLAGS from this invocation's own switches and the auth goes with it. Made true it would still be wrong: `-jN` would bound this build's own recipes and nothing below them, so a subtree under a `-j2` sub-make could run as wide as the grandparent's `-j64`. The comment gets deleted rather than repaired.\n\nRejected, nest the budget — draw N tokens from the parent and re-serve them. The protocol has no sub-allocation. Holding N of the parent's tokens for the lifetime of a subtree starves the rest of the tree whether the subtree spends them or not, and a parent with fewer than N free tokens would block before the subtree ran anything. GNU does not do it, for the same reason.\n\nRejected, serve N and go on drawing on the parent as well. Double-counts every slot.\n\nTwo deliberate narrowings against GNU, both about propagating a budget that is not a budget. `-j` unbounded and `-j1` have nothing to serve, so Ronin serves nothing and each level below falls back to its own default, where GNU writes ` -j` or ` -j1` into MAKEFLAGS and carries the unboundedness down. Matching would mean `flag_environment` writing a job count it does not currently know. Under-parallel, never wrong, and it is the same gap as the one below.\n\nNot this node: what a makefile sees in its own `$(MAKEFLAGS)`. GNU answers ` -j3 --jobserver-auth=fifo:...` for the invocation reading it; Ronin's fifo does not exist until the build runs, long after evaluation, so `$(MAKEFLAGS)` reports no budget at any level. Also not this node: `splice` appends the budget after the long switches, giving `--no-print-directory -j3 --jobserver-auth=...` where GNU writes `-j3 --jobserver-auth=... --no-print-directory`. Children parse either, but the suite compares strings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T02:14:05.88387789Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Implemented as decided, and the whole change is one line of rule plus one line of diagnostic.\n\nThe rule: `options.serve_jobserver = options.jobserver.is_none()`, replacing `= !inheritable`. Serving and spending stay mutually exclusive, and nothing has to ask who is at the top of the tree any more. `build.rs`'s serving branch already reads `serve_jobserver` and `JobLimit::Fixed`, so it needed no code change at all — only its doc comment, which described the old rule.\n\nThe diagnostic: `normalize_runtime_options` now reports the forced count (zero for an unbounded `-j`) instead of `()`. Make mode turns that into GNU's line and says it through the existing `say` helper, straight after the directory announcement, which is where the suite pins it. Ninja mode drops it.\n\nVerified against /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 side by side on the same makefiles, through a make-named symlink. All four shapes agree exactly, level number included:\n\n  -j4 in a submake under -j2   ronin[2]: warning: -j4 forced in submake: resetting jobserver mode.\n  -j  in a submake under -j2   ronin[2]: warning: -j0 forced in submake: resetting jobserver mode.\n  -j1 in a submake under -j2   ronin[1]: warning: -j1 forced in submake: resetting jobserver mode.\n  -j4 in a submake, no parent  silent, both tools\n\nThe budget reaching the bottom, on a four-level tree whose only work is at the deepest level, top invoked at -j2, `-j4` forced at level one, six 0.2s units at level three. Peak simultaneous units from start/end stamps: GNU Make 4, Ronin 4. Before the change the level-two makefile saw MAKEFLAGS with no auth at all, which is peak 1. `a_forced_job_count_replaces_the_budget_a_sub_make_inherited` asserts `peak == FORCED` exactly, not a ceiling — a ceiling is met by a tree running one recipe at a time, which is the defect.\n\n`rust_cli_serves_a_jobserver_only_at_the_top_of_the_tree` encoded the superseded answer and is now `rust_cli_serves_a_jobserver_unless_it_is_spending_one`. Its two `!serve_jobserver` assertions for an explicit `-j` under a parent become `(true, Some(4))`, and it gained the unbounded case.\n\nThe warning surfaces on stderr when Ronin is the outermost process and on stdout below that, because a recipe's output is captured and replayed by its parent — Ninja's output model, which every recipe diagnostic already travels through. The test reads both streams.\n\nGNU's own suite moved three cases in features/jobserver and nothing anywhere else: .2 and .3 lost `no-work-line+product-name`, and .7 — the case that pins the warning's ordering against Entering directory — went from semantic to narration. Semantic differences 767 to 766. The rest of that file still fails on the two things this node did not do.\n\n.NOTPARALLEL is untouched: it clamps local maxjobs and not the budget, and serving reads the budget, so a `.NOTPARALLEL` sub-make with `-j8` still hands 8 down. `make_mode_serialises_only_the_makefile_that_declared_notparallel` still passes.\n\nGates: cargo test --release, clippy --all-targets --release silent, fmt clean, check-ninja-conformance (425 upstream + 10 differential + 32 build outcome), check-make-conformance (44 defects, unmoved), check-make-upstream stable against the re-recorded inventory.\n\nFor other nodes, found while measuring. A makefile cannot see its own budget: GNU answers `$(MAKEFLAGS)` with ` -j3 --jobserver-auth=fifo:...` for the invocation reading it, and Ronin's fifo does not exist until the build runs, long after evaluation, so `$(MAKEFLAGS)` reports no budget at any level. `splice` appends the budget after the long switches — `--no-print-directory -j3 --jobserver-auth=...` where GNU writes `-j3 --jobserver-auth=... --no-print-directory`; children parse either but the suite compares strings, and it is three lines in `jobserver.rs`. And the directory announcement carries no level: Ronin says `ronin: Entering directory` at every depth where GNU says `make[1]:`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T02:15:17.536561966Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T14:21:31.420692308Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Publishing a replacement jobserver to child Make processes is obsolete. The composed parent and subninja graphs use one Ninja scheduler; an outer inherited budget may constrain that scheduler but is never republished as a nested Make runtime."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

