                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T09:28:13.183474663Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "`tests/make_port.rs` over `tests/make/`. Each case is a makefile written here; `expected` records what GNU Make 4.4.1 did with it; the test replays it against Make mode and asserts the same result. A failure is a sentence naming the file and what about it, and it fails the build.\n\nThis replaces the upstream classifier as the measure. `examples/make_upstream.rs` sorts a diff into 18 families and reports counts — a report, not a gate, and nothing fails when a fixed thing regresses. It stays as a discovery tool, which is what it is good at: it found the option family, second expansion, and the recursive jobserver. It should not be quoted as a score again.\n\nWhat is compared: exit status, and for every file the build could have touched, its contents and whether the build wrote it. Not stdout — Make mode narrates the way the manifest front end does (`[spec:ronin:req:make.narration]`, reaffirmed 2026-08-08), so comparing output means deciding line by line which lines were narration, and that decision is what a classifier is. Observable effect needs no such judgement. A case that means to test output writes it to a file; the corpus is ours, so that is how a case is written rather than a limitation.\n\nExpectations are recorded from the oracle and never written by hand — `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` on a `--test make_port` run, which asserts the oracle's version before it writes anything. Recording needs `/usr/bin/make`; running the test does not, so it is a plain `cargo test` and works anywhere.\n\nThe makefiles are ours. GNU Make's own scripts are GPLv3, which is why `reference/gnumake` is a pinned checkout and not vendored (`make-licence-position`), so a case is written here from behaviour probed off 4.4.1 rather than copied.\n\nA known divergence is a `divergence` file beside the case, holding the defect. The suite fails on an unrecorded difference **and** on a recorded one that has started passing, so the list cannot become a place differences go to be forgotten.\n\nFirst run: 17 cases, 16 passing, and it found two defects the classifier never saw — see `make-timestamp-dirtiness`. The 18th case was written for the larger of them.\n\nGrow it from what each lane verifies. Every lane this week probed `/usr/bin/make` side by side to check its work and then threw that away; each of those probes is a case."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-timestamp-dirtiness
                title "Call a Make target up to date on its timestamps alone"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T09:28:01.524638529Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Ronin's Make mode rebuilds a tree GNU Make calls up to date, whenever the build log has no entry for the output. Probed:\n\n  $ make            builds out\n  $ make            'out' is up to date.\n  $ ronin (as make) [1/1] build out\n\nAn output with no build-log entry is dirty here. That is Ninja's rule and correct in Ninja, where the log carries the command hash and a missing entry means the command is unknown. Make has no build log at all: a file newer than its prerequisites is up to date whether or not this tool has ever seen it. So the first Ronin run in any tree GNU Make has built redoes the entire build — which is the ordinary way somebody would try this.\n\nInvisible to GNU Make's own suite, because every case there starts in an empty directory and there is never a pre-existing artefact without a log entry. Found by the first run of make-ported-correctness-suite, whose cases can start from a populated tree because we write them.\n\nIt also reaches `-t`: `make-option-touch-and-what-if` routes the touch through `finish_edge` so the log entry gets written, so `-t` touches every edge Ronin thinks is dirty. GNU touches only what is out of date, so a prerequisite that already exists is left alone. Both cases are recorded as known divergences in the ported suite (`up-to-date-without-log`, `touch-option`) and the suite fails when either starts passing, so the markers cannot be forgotten.\n\nThe fix is a Make-mode dirtiness rule that reads timestamps and not the log. Care is needed about what the log is still for in Make mode — the command hash still catches a changed recipe, which GNU Make does not do at all but which is not wrong to keep. Decide that explicitly rather than by deleting the check."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:37:31.701266228Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:44:52.396267511Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "What the build log is for in Make mode: it is read for what Ronin itself recorded, and its silence is not evidence.\n\nAn output the log names carries a command hash and a build-time mtime, and Ronin acts on both: a recipe whose text changed rebuilds, and an input newer than the mtime the build recorded rebuilds. An output the log does not name says nothing at all, and the question falls back to GNU Make's rule — a file newer than its prerequisites is up to date.\n\nThis is the whole of the fix. `refresh_command_hash` treated a missing entry as a changed command, which is Ninja's rule and correct in Ninja: there the log is the only record of what produced an output, so silence means the command is unknown, and an unknown command is a changed one. Make has no build log, so there is nothing for an output to be absent from, and reading absence as evidence made the first Ronin run in any tree GNU Make had built redo the entire build.\n\nRejected: dropping the command hash in Make mode as well, so that dirtiness is timestamps and nothing else. It is the tidier rule and it is what GNU Make does, but it throws away knowledge Ronin holds — that the recipe it recorded is not the recipe it is about to run — to reproduce a limitation nobody defends. `StatePlacement::OutsideTheTree` already says in as many words that the state is kept precisely for \"the two things GNU Make cannot do at all — noticing a changed command, and carrying a compiler's header dependencies to the next build\"; deleting the first of those would leave the state being kept for half a reason. A stale artifact after a recipe edit is a wrong build, and a wrong build is worse than an extra one.\n\nRejected: seeding the log from the tree on a first run, so that the missing-entry rule could stay. That would mean writing a command hash for a command Ronin never observed — a fabricated record, which would then make the second run's recipe-change check lie in the other direction.\n\nRejected: reading `StatePlacement::OutsideTheTree` as \"this graph came from a Makefile\" and hanging the dirtiness rule off it. Two unrelated consequences sharing one field is how a field stops meaning what its name says. The graph carries each consequence under its own name.\n\nNote the asymmetry this leaves, deliberately: whether a recipe change rebuilds depends on whether Ronin built that output before. That is not a rule GNU Make has and not one Ninja has, but it is the honest statement of what Ronin knows — it acts on positive knowledge and declines to guess from silence.\n\nThe port gate cannot see the second half of this. A case runs the tool once over a tree its `setup` script built, so there is never a log entry when Ronin is measured against the oracle, and a divergence that needs two Ronin runs is structurally invisible. `recipe-changed-without-log` pins the half the gate can hold: no entry plus a changed recipe is up to date, the same answer GNU Make gives."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:09:00.325995641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T14:21:31.578962876Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Make-specific unrecorded-output dirtiness violates the compiler boundary. make-executor-boundary restores ordinary Ninja dirtiness for every graph and removes frontend-selected timestamp semantics."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-failure-line
                title "Name the makefile line, the target and the status when a recipe fails"
                priority 1
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T08:32:22.381383582Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "78 cases. GNU Make names the makefile line, the target and the recipe's status in one line; Ronin reports the failure Ninja's way, with a `FAILED:` block and its own stopped line. A further 90 cases are tagged for the block itself.\n\n  Makefile:3: recipe for target 'all' failed\n  make: *** [all] Error 1\n\nis Make 4.2's shape; 4.4.1 says\n\n  make: *** [Makefile:3: all] Error 1\n\nVerify against /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 rather than either of those.\n\nNow reachable because `make-option-debug-and-trace` wired the rule's makefile:line across from kati as an edge binding (`recipe_location`) — kati had been handing it to the sink as `SinkEdge::loc` and nothing was reading it. That binding is the input this node needs.\n\nThe distinction that governs the work: a failed recipe's own output is Make's and must match. The `FAILED:` block is Ronin narrating a build the way Ninja does, and in Ninja mode it stays. This is Make mode choosing Make's shape, not a change to Ronin's identity — `[spec:ronin:req:product.ronin-identity]` still applies to the name in front."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T08:32:27.675612998Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T09:02:23.725484987Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Done. Make mode now says what GNU Make 4.4.1 says about a failed recipe, and nothing else about it.\n\nEvery shape below was read off /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 side by side through a make-named symlink, not from the manual. The line is `name: *** [file:line: target] Error N`, and the whole of it is fixed by Make except the name in front, which stays Ronin's and carries the level exactly as `program_at` already formats it for the forced-jobserver warning: `ronin[1]: *** [Makefile:2: all] Error 1` under a parent, `ronin: *** [top.mk:2: all] Error 2` above it. `-s` does not withdraw it. `-O` brackets it with the block, inside the Entering/Leaving pair, which is where GNU puts it. Verified against 4.4.1 on all of these.\n\nThree decisions, in order of how much they mattered.\n\nThe `FAILED:` block goes in Make mode and stays in Ninja mode. It is one flag, `BuildOptions::recipe_failure`, holding the name to lead with — `Some` only where the Make front end builds the options, `None` for every manifest build, so the Ninja path is not even branched on. `Reporter::failure` is untouched.\n\nThe stopped line goes with it, and only for the stops the recipes already described. `BuildStop::is_recipe_failure` is `SubcommandFailed | CannotMakeProgress`; `Interrupted`, `Stuck` and `Failed(error)` still say `ronin: build stopped: …`, because no command narrated those and dropping them would lose the only account of what went wrong. This is what makes the change one shape rather than two: emitting Make's line and keeping Ninja's summary would have produced output that is neither tool's.\n\nThe order reverses. Ninja opens a failure with its banner and shows the recipe's output under it; Make shows the output and then says what became of it. `command_finished` now emits the failure before the output or after it depending on the same flag.\n\nMeasured on GNU Make's own suite, before → after, at the same 1348 counted cases:\n\n                 before  after\n  semantic          772    773\n  capability         54     54\n  narration         522    521\n\n  recipe-error-line       78 →  59\n  ninja-failure-block     90 →   0\n  product-name           291 → 346\n\nPer case: 4 narration → semantic, 3 semantic → narration. The 3 gained are targets/POSIX and targets/ONESHELL.8 and .9, which now agree line for line. Of the 4 lost, three are the same defect and it was there before: features/errors and .1 and misc/general3.10 are `-`-prefixed recipes, where kati compiles ignore-errors as `cmd ; true` inside a subshell. `exit 1` leaves the subshell before `true` runs, so the build fails where GNU Make succeeds — a real semantic difference that our own `FAILED:` block had been hiding, since the classifier subtracted it and the residue then matched. Making the line Make's shape exposed it. The fourth, features/errors.2, is the one-shell-per-line limit below.\n\n`product-name` rising 55 is not a regression and is not new output. Those are cases where Ronin fails and GNU Make does not, or fails somewhere else; the line we now print is a residue in the diff where the `FAILED:` block used to be subtracted as narration. Every one of them was already semantic for the reason it disagreed.\n\nWhat this deliberately does not do, and why.\n\nThe line named is the recipe's *first* line; GNU names the line that actually failed. Ronin runs a recipe as one shell script, so the failing line is not observable — `recipe_location` is the only place kati records, one per rule. They agree for every one-line recipe and whenever the first line fails, which is most of the suite; features/errors.2 is the one case where it does not. That is `make-recipe-one-shell-per-line`'s ground and cannot be fixed here.\n\nThe line goes to stdout where GNU writes stderr. Every other thing Ronin's Make mode says about a build — the directory announcement, the stopped line, the progress line — is on stdout, and splitting one line onto the other stream would reorder it against the recipe output it follows. The suite compares the two merged, so nothing is measured either way.\n\nA recipe's shell killed by a signal other than INT, TERM or HUP reports `Error 139` where GNU reports `Segmentation fault (core dumped)`. GNU only prints a signal description when the shell itself dies, and Ronin's direct child is a shell too, so the two agree for every recipe whose *command* was signalled. No case in the suite reaches the difference, and the strings are glibc's `strsignal`, so a hand-rolled table would be a platform-specific guess with nothing to measure it against.\n\nHandoffs, none of them this node's.\n\nkati compiles a `-` prefix, `-i` and `.IGNORE` into `cmd ; true`, which is wrong twice: it does not survive `exit`, and it destroys the status before the runner sees it, so Ronin can never print GNU's `[file:line: target] Error N (ignored)`. The fix is for kati to mark the line rather than rewrite it. 9 diffs carry `(ignored)`.\n\nkati names its bootstrap makefile `*bootstrap*:0` where GNU Make says `<builtin>`, so a failing built-in rule reports `ronin: *** [*bootstrap*:0: hello.o] Error 1`. `recipe_location` already has a `<builtin>` fallback and it is dead, because kati supplies a location rather than none. This reaches `--trace` too. Not worked around here: it is a Make defect and it belongs in the submodule.\n\n`make: Target 'x' not remade because of errors.` under `-k`, 18 diffs. GNU says it per goal, at the point that goal finishes, so it is not a line the front end can append at the end — it needs the goal list inside the build loop. `make: *** Waiting for unfinished jobs....`, 1 diff, is the same kind of thing.\n\nkati's own exec path has the identical defect against the vendored corpus: `error-location`, 5 cases, \"GNU Make 4.0+ names the makefile line inside *** [Makefile:N: target] Error N; kati writes only the target\". Ronin's Make mode does not use that path, so this node did not touch it and did not move it.\n\n`recipe-error-line`'s description in the classifier said Ronin reports the failure Ninja's way, which stopped being true. It now says GNU Make named the line and ours is missing or names something else, which is what the 59 are. Nothing else in the classifier moved; `ninja-failure-block` keeps its recogniser because `build stopped:` still fires for the stops no recipe described.\n\nGates: cargo test --release (465 unit + 56 cli, two new), clippy --all-targets --release silent, fmt clean, check-ninja-conformance (425 upstream, 10 differential, 32 build-outcome, persistence and log content) — Ninja mode is unmoved and the flag it branches on is never set there. check-make-conformance 44 defects, unmoved, since it runs rkati and not Make mode. check-make-upstream re-recorded and then stable against the recording."
                    }

                    {
