            {
                id make-private-variables
                title "Read the private keyword on a variable assignment"
                priority 1
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:36:07.460550581Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "16 cases in variables/private, and two of them are a wrong build rather than a refusal.\n\n`make-parser-parity` found this while implementing the one-word variable-name rule: without `private`, `private FOO = g` reads as an assignment to a variable literally named `private FOO`. The one-word rule now refuses it, which is correct until the keyword exists, and moved two cases into parse-failure on purpose.\n\n`all: private FOO = a` gives an empty `$(FOO)` today where GNU Make gives `a`.\n\nThree positions to cover, and it is not a no-op in any of them. Globally, `private FOO = a` defines FOO but withholds it from the target-specific inheritance chain. On a target, `target: private FOO = a` gives FOO to that target's recipe and not to its prerequisites' recipes — which is the whole point of the keyword. On a pattern rule the same. `override private` and `private override` are both accepted by GNU and mean the same thing.\n\nVerify each against /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 rather than from this note, including what `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` say about a private variable and whether it is exported.\n\nkati's parser and variable table. Two other lanes are in kati this wave and neither is in these files."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:37:49.342691377Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:29:44.84250931Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Done. `private` is read in all three positions, every value below taken off /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 side by side through a make-named symlink rather than from the node's description.\n\nWhat it is, in GNU Make's own terms (src/variable.c, confirmed against the binary). `lookup_variable` walks the variable-set chain carrying an `is_parent` flag, and a set whose `next_is_parent` is set turns it on. `initialize_file_variables` sets `next_is_parent` on every file's own set, unconditionally, so the global set is a parent of every rule's scope. A private binding is returned only while `is_parent` is still clear — from the scope that defined it and from nothing that reaches that scope through a parent. That single rule is all three positions:\n\n  - `private F = g` globally: `$(F)` is `g` while the makefile is being read and empty in every recipe, where `$(origin F)` and `$(flavor F)` both say `undefined`. It is also empty in a target-specific assignment's right-hand side.\n  - `a: private F = a`: a's recipe sees it, a's prerequisites' recipes do not, and a non-private `a: Q = b` beside it still is inherited.\n  - `%.out: private F = pat`: the target the pattern matched sees it, its prerequisites do not.\n\n`override private` and `private override` are both accepted and both give `$(origin)` = override. `export`, `unexport`, `override` and `private` are all keywords in front of a target-specific name and GNU takes them in any order.\n\nImplementation, kati 663eb65 on lane2/kati.\n\nparser.rs: `private` joins `override` and `export` as a directive, all three now guarded by `!starts_assignment(rest)` — the guard `undefine` already had. `private = g` assigns to a variable called `private` (GNU's parse_var_assignment asks whether the line is a variable definition before asking whether the word is a modifier), and the same guard incidentally fixes `override = g` and `export = e`, which were refused with \"empty variable name\" and now assign. `take_assign_modifiers` takes the keywords off a target-specific name: a name with no space left in it is the name however it is spelled, which is what keeps `a: private = a` assigning to `private`.\n\neval.rs: one clause in `lookup_var` — when a rule scope is current, a private global is not there. `$(origin)`, `$(flavor)` and `$(value)` all route through it, so they answer undefined without knowing about the keyword. A global's flag is sticky (`private F = a` then `F = b` stays private) because GNU redefines the variable in place rather than replacing it; a target-specific one is settled per assignment, which Ronin gets for free since Vars::assign replaces the binding.\n\ndep.rs, six lines, and the only reason this node touched lane 4's file: the inheritance boundary lives in the dependency walk and nowhere else. kati models Make's chain of variable sets as one flat scope with ScopedVar shadowing as build_plan descends, and the node's snapshot was taken after its prerequisites were planned. A private binding now goes in a second ScopedVar list, the snapshot is read directly after the scope is built, and that list is dropped before the prerequisite loop. The flag is read off the declaration rather than the value because `+=` resolves to a fresh simple variable and would otherwise leave the keyword behind — `bar1: private DEFS += 1` leaked into bar2 until that was fixed.\n\nsrc/make.rs: `exported_environment` skips a private global. `private export F = g` reaches `$(shell)` and reaches no recipe's environment, which is `target_environment`'s `if (!islocal && v->private_var) continue;`.\n\nSeven cases in tests/make, each recorded from 4.4.1 with MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1: private-variable-global, -not-inherited (the boundary, with a non-private control in the same file), -pattern-rule, -appended, -modifiers, -is-a-name, -not-exported.\n\nGates: 470 unit + 62 integration + 35 make_port + 76 kati unit, clippy --all-targets --release silent in both crates, fmt clean. kati's vendored corpus unmoved at 44 defects, 259 raw / 296 normalised identical — the same three numbers as before the change.\n\nThe upstream suite, measured as baseline-vs-mine rather than against the committed inventory, which is stale: it was recorded before the last three commits on this branch and reports 1348 where HEAD produces 1341. Reverse-applying the change, running, recording, re-applying and running again gives 785 semantic → 776, narration 535 → 544, capability 21 → 21, counted 1341 → 1341. Nine cases moved out of semantic and none moved in; per category the movement is variables/private 5, features/targetvars 2, features/export 1, features/shell_assignment 1, and no category moved the other way.\n\nFour handoffs, all confirmed present before this change and none of them this node's.\n\nPattern-specific variables are only applied when a pattern rule is picked. `%.out: F = pat` with `a.out: b.out` beside it gives a.out nothing, private or not, because pick_rule returns the explicit merger's vars without calling merge_implicit_rule_vars. Two of variables/private's remaining six semantic diffs are this, and it is dep.rs.\n\nTarget-specific `export` is not implemented at all: `a: export EE = yes` leaves EE unset in the recipe's environment. Three more of the six are this, and it is make-export-to-recipes' ground.\n\nA target-specific variable outranks a command-line assignment. `a: X = a` with `make X=c` gives `a` here and `c` in GNU; with `override` in front GNU gives `a`. Ronin now strips `override` from the name without honouring it, which happens to agree with GNU on the second of those for the wrong reason. Target-var precedence is make-target-variable-order's.\n\nTwo corners where a modifier is not literal. GNU parses target-specific modifiers before expansion and the name after, so `a: private $(T) = 1` is private (Ronin agrees) and `a: $(P) F = 1` with P=private is refused as a rule with no rule for `private` (Ronin builds it — before this change as a variable named `private F`, after it as a private F). The neighbouring `a: X Y = 1` is the one-word name rule, which make-parser-parity applied to global assignments and not to target-specific ones; GNU refuses it and Ronin defines `X Y`. Both are the parser, both were already a wrong build, and neither is reachable without a variable that expands to a keyword.\n\n`$(shell)` at makefile level does not see exported variables at all — `export F = g` then `$(info $(shell echo $$F))` gives nothing here and `g` in GNU. Not private-specific, found while checking that a private export reaches the shell function and not a recipe."
                    }

