            {
                id make-ignore-errors-status
                title "Keep a recipe's status when its errors are ignored"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:36:14.813157958Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "kati compiles an ignored-error recipe as `cmd ; true` inside a subshell (`kati/src-rs/ninja.rs:474`, decided at `command.rs:334`). That is wrong twice over, and `make-recipe-failure-line` found both while making the failure line match GNU's:\n\nIt does not survive `exit`. `exit 1` leaves the subshell before `true` runs, so we genuinely fail where GNU Make continues. Three cases that looked like narration differences were this — the classifier had been subtracting our `FAILED:` block and finding the residue matched, so the defect hid behind our own output.\n\nIt destroys the status before the runner sees it. GNU Make reports `make: [Makefile:3: all] Error 1 (ignored)`, which needs the real exit code; `; true` makes it zero, so `(ignored)` can never be printed at all.\n\n9 diffs. Covers the `-` recipe prefix, `-i`, and `.IGNORE` — all three compile the same way.\n\nThe fix has to keep the status and let the runner decide, rather than making the shell lie about it. Ronin's side already knows how to report it: `make-recipe-failure-line` put the failure line in place and left the `(ignored)` half unreachable.\n\n`kati/src-rs/ninja.rs` and `kati/src-rs/command.rs`. Two other lanes are in kati this wave; neither is in these files."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:38:13.76355757Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:22:15.501601719Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Done. Both halves, and every shape below read off /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 side by side through a make-named symlink rather than reasoned about.\n\nThe first defect was real and was hiding a build difference. `all: ; -exit 1` then `echo reached > out`: GNU writes `out` and exits 0; Ronin exited 2 and wrote nothing, because `( exit 1 ; true )` leaves the subshell before `true` runs and the `&&` chain stops. Confirmed before touching anything.\n\nkati now compiles it in two shapes, decided per recipe rather than per line.\n\nA recipe where *every* line ignores errors chains with `;` and keeps the status it ends on. That is the only shape where a nonzero status is unambiguous: nothing can break the chain, so the status can only have come from an ignored failure. It says so through a new `SinkRule::ignore_errors`.\n\nAny other ignored line is muted where it stands, `{ ( cmd ) ; true ; }`. The subshell is inside so `exit` reaches the `true`; the group is outside so the mute is local — a bare `... && ( cmd ) ; true` would swallow the chain's own break as well, and `false` then `-exit 3` would wrongly succeed. `;` and `&&` have equal precedence in the shell, so the group is what makes the difference. There is a case for that (`ignore-errors-last-line-counts`).\n\n`; true` had exactly one caller, `ninja.rs:474`. kati's own exec path (`exec.rs:174`) evaluates ignore-errors per command against a shell per line, so it was already right and is untouched — which is why the vendored corpus does not move.\n\nThe manifest writer answers for the flag rather than passing it on: `( <script> ) ; true`, the subshell containing an `exit`. Ninja has no notion of an edge allowed to fail, so this follows what `SinkEdge::intermediate` already documents — the writer answers what a manifest cannot say. Verified with the pinned reference ninja on a generated manifest: both an ignored `exit 1` and a wholly-ignored two-line recipe exit 0, matching GNU. `gen_shell_script` returns `false` for an empty script, so a recipe absorbed entirely (a lone `mkdir -p` of the output's directory) cannot produce `( ) ; true`, which is a shell syntax error.\n\nRonin's side: 22 lines, listed in the commit. The flag crosses as a rule binding, `CommandSpec` reads it like `restat`, `make_failure` drops the stars and gains `(ignored)`, and the build loop does not turn the failure into `SubcommandFailed`. Output now: `ronin: [Makefile:2: all] Error 4 (ignored)`, against GNU's `make: [Makefile:2: all] Error 4 (ignored)`.\n\nSeven cases in `tests/make/`, recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1`. Six fail without the change; the seventh (`ignore-errors-last-line-counts`) passes both ways by design, since it guards against over-ignoring. Verified by stashing the change, rebuilding and running the suite: exactly those six diverge.\n\n  ignore-errors-exit             the `-` prefix over `exit 1`, which is the defect itself\n  ignore-errors-switch           `-i`, with `false` and `exit 3` before the line that writes\n  ignore-errors-per-target       `.IGNORE: one` with a second target that must still build\n  ignore-errors-prerequisite     an ignored failure leaves the target made, so its dependent runs\n  ignore-errors-keep-going       `-k` across an ignored target and a failing one\n  ignore-errors-mixed-recipe     a failure that counts after an ignored one still stops the build\n  ignore-errors-last-line-counts an ignored *last* line does not rescue an earlier real failure\n\nThe `(ignored)` line itself cannot be a ported case — the harness compares effect, not output — so it has a unit test, `make_build_reports_an_ignored_status_and_carries_on`.\n\nVerified side by side and agreeing on effect: the `-` prefix on one line of a multi-line recipe; `-i`; `.IGNORE` global and per-target; a recipe calling `exit N`; an ignored `cd` to a directory that does not exist (the subshell contains it, and `pwd` after it agrees); an ignored `$(MAKE)` of a makefile that does not exist; `.PHONY`; `-k` across an ignored and a failing target; and `.ONESHELL` with every line ignored, which now reports `Error 7 (ignored)` where it used to fail.\n\nThree things found while measuring, none of them this node's.\n\nA recipe killed by a signal reports `Error 139 (ignored)` where GNU says `Segmentation fault (core dumped) (ignored)`. That is the wording `make-recipe-failure-line` already decided against a hand-rolled strsignal table, and the `(ignored)` half of it now works.\n\nAn ignored line in a *mixed* recipe is muted, so its `Error N (ignored)` is never printed even though the effect is right. GNU prints one per failing line and Ronin has one status for the whole recipe. That is `make-recipe-one-shell-per-line`'s ground and cannot be fixed here. The same limit is why a signal sent with `kill -SEGV $$` differs at all: in one shell per recipe `$$` is the outer shell, so the recipe kills itself rather than the line.\n\n`.ONESHELL` with `-` on the first line only: GNU applies the prefix to the whole recipe, kati keeps it per line. Pre-existing by construction — under `.ONESHELL` nothing was ever appended and the separator was already `\\n`, so the script is byte for byte what it was. It belongs with whoever owns `.ONESHELL` prefix hoisting.\n\n`b: ; false` with a second recipe line reports `Makefile:5` where GNU says `Makefile:4`: kati records the node's location as the last recipe line rather than the first. Also `make: Target 'all' not remade because of errors.` under `-k`, which is its own node already.\n\nGates: 471 unit + 62 cli + 35 ported cases, clippy --all-targets --release silent, fmt clean, kati 79 unit, check-make-conformance 44 defects unmoved, check-ninja-conformance unmoved (425 upstream, 10 differential, 32 build-outcome, persistence, log content)."
                    }

