            {
                id make-switch-precedence
                title "Let the command line outrank an inherited MAKEFLAGS"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:36:21.372364297Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "An inherited MAKEFLAGS currently outranks the command line, for every switch.\n\nFound by `make-directory-announce-level`, which confirmed it is pre-existing by building without its own change: `--no-print-directory` at the top with `$(MAKE) -w -C b` below announces in GNU Make and does not here.\n\nGNU decodes the environment's MAKEFLAGS first and then decodes the command line, so the command line wins. Ronin calls `adopt_inherited` after `parse`, so the inherited value overwrites what was typed.\n\nThe ordering is not the whole fix: `adopt_inherited` sets switches unconditionally, so simply moving it earlier would make the environment lose even where GNU keeps it — a switch present only in MAKEFLAGS must still take effect. What is needed is that a switch the command line spoke about, in either direction, is not then overwritten. `Invocation` already tracks withdrawal separately from assertion (`given`/`refused`/`withdraw`), which is the machinery for saying \"the command line had an opinion here\".\n\nVerify against /usr/bin/make 4.4.1 across: a switch only in MAKEFLAGS, only on the command line, in both agreeing, in both disagreeing in each direction, and a negating spelling on the command line against an asserting one in MAKEFLAGS.\n\n`src/make/cli.rs`. No other lane is in that file this wave."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:38:55.663317603Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:19:27.029262445Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The ordering was the whole fix after all, once `parse` reads the inherited MAKEFLAGS into the invocation it is about to fill rather than being handed a fresh one. `parse(arguments, inherited)` calls `adopt_inherited` first and the command line then writes over whatever it spoke about, in either direction, because `add` and `withdraw` each clear the other bit. A switch nobody typed keeps what arrived. No second \"the command line had an opinion here\" mechanism was needed — `given`/`refused` already are that, and reading the environment into the same two words is what makes them one question rather than two.\n\nThe node's warning that moving `adopt_inherited` earlier would make the environment lose was about moving the call, not about moving the read: `parse` built its own `Invocation::new()`, so anything adopted before it was thrown away. Threading `inherited` into `parse` is the difference.\n\nTwo further things fell out of the reorder rather than being separate work. `-O` in an inherited MAKEFLAGS was guarded by `if self.output_sync.is_none()` to stop it beating the command line; with the read moved that guard makes the *first* `-O` in MAKEFLAGS win where GNU takes the last, so it goes. And `parse`'s closing \"GNU Make forgets -d once the words have had their say\" now runs after both sources, which is where GNU decides it: `MAKEFLAGS=d` with `--debug=n` typed leaves nothing, as it does upstream.\n\nValue-carrying switches need no different rule from the boolean ones, for the three that travel as switches. `-O`, `--debug=` and `--shuffle=` are last-word-wins on the merged read, and `--debug` accumulates in read order so the environment's word comes first and a typed `--debug=n` takes it back. Verified against 4.4.1 across the whole matrix — only there, only here, agreeing, disagreeing each way, and a negating spelling against an asserting one each way — through the MAKEFLAGS each tool hands a recipe, which is the merged view rather than the raw environment.\n\n`-j` and `-l` are the exception and it is not a precedence exception: neither is read from an inherited MAKEFLAGS at all, and neither is written into the one a child gets. `-j` is deliberate — the budget travels through the jobserver transport, and adopting an inherited `-j` into `invocation.jobs` would make every sub-make look like it forced its own and emit `make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent`'s warning. If it is ever wanted, the clean shape is to map the inherited count onto `normalize_runtime_options`'s `unshared` argument, which is exactly GNU's `arg_job_slots = env_slots` fallback and leaves the forced-warning keyed on the command line alone. `-l` has no such reason and is simply missing at both ends. Both belong on a node about carrying an option, not about which of two sources wins.\n\nCommand-line variable assignments do not have the precedence defect — a typed `FOO=cmd` already beat an inherited `FOO=env`, because `session_for` read the inherited ones first and appended its own. They had a propagation defect instead, which is the same root cause seen from the other side: `flag_environment` wrote only this invocation's own assignments, so `make FOO=top` reached a sub-make and vanished at the one below it. Fixed by adopting the words after the `--` into `invocation.variables` alongside the switches, which lets `session_for` drop its second parse of the same string and lets `flag_environment` hand the whole set down. Deduplicated by name keeping the last, in name order, which is what MAKEOVERRIDES being a variable table means. Goals have no defect: GNU does not put them in MAKEFLAGS.\n\nOne spelling defect found by the first case rather than by reading: `-S` was appended after the letter group where GNU writes it inside the group, in switch-table order — `make -i -S -w -B -r` gives `BirSw` and Ronin gave `BirwS`. `propagated`'s table gained a third column saying whether the entry reads `given` or `refused`, and `-S` sits between `-s` and `-t`.\n\nNot fixed and belonging elsewhere: `$(MAKEFLAGS)` read inside a sub-make's own makefile still reports the parent's raw value, not the merge. `record_invocation` declines to define it when the environment has one, and even without that guard kati's `read_invocation_state` imports the process environment during `evaluate` and would overwrite it. Fixing it needs either a kati change or Ronin setting the process variable before evaluation. `make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent` already carved out the budget half of this question as not-that-node; the switch half is the same shape."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:27:05.809377999Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:56:34.445375239Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "A later multi-lane merge kept this node's append-only completion log and close commit but reverted plan/.state.styx to Todo while resolving the generated ledger. Restore the ledger state; the implementation and verification remain on main."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:56:34.515784999Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-option-carrying
                title "Carry graph-affecting invocation inputs into subninja compilation"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                deps (make-subninja-recursion)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:11:34.418431875Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Four ways a sub-make is told something GNU Make would have told it, or told it differently. All found by `make-switch-precedence` and `make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent` while doing adjacent work, and all deliberately left.\n\n`-l` is neither adopted from an inherited MAKEFLAGS nor written into a child's. Unlike `-j`, there is no reason for it — `-j` travels through the jobserver transport on purpose, and adopting it would make every sub-make look like it forced its own.\n\n`-j`'s inherited count is not read at all. The clean shape, if wanted, is to map it onto `normalize_runtime_options`'s `unshared` argument — exactly GNU's `arg_job_slots = env_slots` fallback — which leaves `make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent`'s warning keyed on the command line alone. That decision is recorded there; do not undo it.\n\nA propagated assignment's value is not escaped: GNU writes `FOO=a\\ b`, Ronin writes `FOO=a b`, so a value with a space arrives as two words.\n\n`splice` appends the budget after the long switches — `--no-print-directory -j3 --jobserver-auth=...` where GNU writes `-j3 --jobserver-auth=... --no-print-directory`. Children parse either; the suite compares strings. Three lines in `jobserver.rs`.\n\n`src/make/cli.rs` and `src/jobserver.rs`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T15:20:37.311220711Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Make invocation variables are compiler inputs for semantic subninja composition, not a recursive Make runtime. Ronin now distinguishes explicit `-j` from a count inherited through MAKEFLAGS: the explicit count overrides the outer Ninja scheduler, while the inherited count is only the unshared fallback when no usable outer jobserver exists. `-j` and propagated `-l` spellings remain visible to child Makefile evaluation, including the distinct interface states of bare `-l` and `-l0`, without creating another scheduler. Command assignments are escaped using MAKEFLAGS environment syntax (backslashes, dollars, and whitespace), and Kati installs GNU-compatible recursive MAKEFLAGS/MAKEOVERRIDES variables with their observable origin/flavor relationship. After evaluating a parent unit, its evaluated MAKEFLAGS plus command-line environment values seed descendant compilation, so a Makefile can deliberately change or clear MAKEOVERRIDES before the child graph is compiled. The old note's proposed jobserver splice-order edit is intentionally not implemented: exact recursive jobserver text is Make-runner choreography superseded by [dec:ronin:make-compiles-to-ninja], while accepted syntax and graph-affecting values remain interface-compatible. Refactored the prior same-shell nested-Make fixture into actual semantic subninja composition, removed its divergence marker, and added GNU Make 4.4.1-recorded cases for spaced assignments across two child levels, recursive `-j`/`-l` replacement, and clearing MAKEOVERRIDES while retaining environment-origin values. The full build-intent gate, 296-file direct-graph/manifest equivalence gate, workspace tests, format, release Clippy with `-D warnings`, and `nplan check` all pass. The separate upstream conformance inventory still reports its existing generated-include and `.SHELLSTATUS` reclassifications under the claimed make-conformance-corpus node. Kati commit 029cbb9 is pushed on origin/nplan/make-option-carrying."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T15:20:43.227171217Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-refusal-reaches-further
                title "Preserve compiler refusals and source locations without Make runner wording"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                deps (make-executor-boundary)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:11:47.03355994Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Places Ronin abandons a build GNU Make finishes, or refuses without saying what it was reading. Found by `make-io-error-wording` while removing Rust's `(os error N)` suffix, and left because they are behaviour rather than wording.\n\nA stem past `NAME_MAX` makes Ronin abandon on ENAMETOOLONG where Make builds the target — 10 cases, `GraphError::Stat`. Statting a path that cannot exist is not an error; it is the answer \"no\".\n\n`jobserver unavailable: using -j1` — GNU Make warns and finishes; Ronin says the same thing and exits 1. Verified side by side.\n\nNinja mode answers a missing manifest with `ronin: No such file or directory` where upstream Ninja says `ninja: error: loading 'build.ninja': No such file or directory`. The missing `error: loading '<path>': ` context is a real gap the 425 pinned tests do not reach — check it against the pinned reference binary, and note this half is Ninja's contract, not Make's, so `[spec:ronin:req:compat.upstream-conformance]` governs rather than the Make narration rule.\n\n`src/main.rs:62` reports `writing terminal output:` where the suite expects `/^ronin: write error/`. `system_message` is `pub(crate)` and main.rs is the binary crate, so reaching it means widening the library's public API — decide whether that is worth it or whether the message moves instead.\n\n`src/error.rs`, `src/graph.rs`, `src/main.rs`. Do not touch `src/build.rs` or `src/make/**` — other lanes own them this wave."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T15:40:22.065979806Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Pinned-Ninja invocation differential exposed a narrower interface requirement: stale inherited jobserver setup is non-fatal (exit 0 and local scheduling), but the reference emits its jobserver-detected line on stdout and initialization error on stderr. Preserve that Ninja-facing diagnostic surface while refusing to turn the stale transport into a build failure."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:04:27.471695554Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Refusals are split at the compiler/executor boundary established by [dec:ronin:make-compiles-to-ninja]. A stale inherited jobserver is optional outer scheduling state: Ronin now reports the pinned Ninja information/error lines, discards the unusable transport, and continues with its local Ninja scheduler; dry runs and explicit `-j` do not join it. A missing selected Ninja manifest now gains `error: loading '<path>':` only at the executable boundary, preserving the reusable library API's raw I/O error and source chain. The proposed shared ENAMETOOLONG/stat relaxation is rejected because the pinned Ninja reference also refuses the literal 66 KiB node; the remaining GNU-compatible build-intent gap is compiler lowering and is tracked by `make-unaddressable-phony-aliases`, which must synthesize safe aliases without changing executor stat behavior. The proposed `write error` change is also rejected as GNU Make runner narration: pinned Ninja reports nothing for the measured `/dev/full` terminal-close case, so `src/main.rs` remains unchanged. Permanent process differentials cover selected missing manifests, stale jobserver fallback, and dry-run jobserver avoidance. `nplan check`, release Clippy with `-D warnings`, all 425 pinned Ninja tests, 10 CLI/tool cases, 32 build outcomes, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence/log round trips, the 296-file direct graph/manifest equivalence gate, and the 296-case Make build-intent test all pass."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:04:35.96362172Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-include-semantics
                title "Build a missing include and go on where GNU Make does"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:11:54.762848733Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "40 of the 53 cases `make-io-error-wording` grouped are kati's, and they are one behaviour and one rendering.\n\nkati treats a failed `include` as fatal. GNU Make **warns, tries to build the named file as a target, and re-reads the makefile if it succeeded** — `features/include.diff.10` shows Make building `inc1` and `inc2` and then running the recipe. That is the remaking-makefiles pass, which Ronin has no equivalent of, so decide how far to go: warning and continuing is most of the value and does not need the pass; building the include does.\n\n`kati/src-rs/eval.rs:958-966` renders a raw `std::io::Error` into `\"{pat}: {err}\"`, which is 35 of the 40. `kati/src-rs/file.rs:36` reads with `std::fs::read(...)?` into anyhow with no context at all, so the whole diagnostic is `Permission denied (os error 13)` — no name, no location. That is 5.\n\nRonin's side already has `system_message` for exactly this shape; `make-io-error-wording` applied it everywhere an `io::Error` reaches a Ronin diagnostic.\n\nAlso here, from `make-parser-parity`: `-I -` restarts the include search path, and `dash-I.5` still differs because we name the resolved path and add kati's `In file included from` line, which GNU has no equivalent of.\n\n`kati/src-rs/eval.rs` and `kati/src-rs/file.rs`. Other lanes own `dep.rs`, `parser.rs` and `ninja.rs` this wave."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T13:27:08.729567224Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T13:49:04.746614759Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Missing included Makefiles are compiler source dependencies, not a reason to add Make execution behavior. Kati keeps evaluating so later rules can emit a provisional Ninja graph in which every buildable missing include is an additional root selected only after the Makefile's default goal. Ronin builds those roots through the ordinary Ninja scheduler and persistence layer, creates a fresh Kati session, and recompiles until the graph no longer has compiler-input roots. Recursive Make units contribute their roots to the same composed graph, preserving the semantic subninja boundary. Required unbuildable includes remain compiler errors; optional unbuildable includes remain absent. The two recorded include-remake divergences now match GNU Make's build effects and their markers are removed; all 257 build-intent cases pass."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T13:49:10.814797475Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-assignment-modifiers
                title "Read an assignment's modifiers where GNU Make reads them"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:12:00.9362117Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two corners where kati reads an assignment differently from GNU Make, both already a wrong build, both found by `make-private-variables` and left because they are the parser's rather than the variable table's.\n\nGNU treats `override`, `export`, `private` and `undefine` as **literal words**, not as the result of expansion. `a: $(P) F = 1` with `P=private`: GNU refuses, Ronin builds — before that node it made a variable named `private F`, after it a private `F`. Neither is right.\n\nBeside it, `a: X Y = 1` — the one-word variable-name rule was applied to global assignments and not to target-specific ones, so a two-word target-specific name is still accepted.\n\nAlso here, from `make-parser-parity`'s residue: `$(flavor)` on a `!=` variable says `simple` where GNU says `recursive`; `$(origin .RECIPEPREFIX)` says `undefined` where GNU says `default`; a command-line variable removed by `override undefine` is back inside a recipe in GNU; and `.SHELLSTATUS` cannot be read inside a rule.\n\nNeither of the two main corners is reachable without a variable expanding to a keyword, which is why they survived this long — but both are wrong builds rather than refusals, and that ranks them.\n\n`kati/src-rs/parser.rs`, `kati/src-rs/stmt.rs`, `kati/src-rs/strutil.rs`, `kati/src-rs/func.rs`. Other lanes own `dep.rs`, `eval.rs` and `ninja.rs` this wave."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T14:22:58.959466882Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Assignment syntax remains compiler work. Kati now recognizes `::=` as immediate-simple and `:::=` as immediate-recursive, preserves the operator on `define` directives, applies append precedence before mutating a value, and captures target-specific modifiers from literal source words before expanding the variable name. Expanded text that happens to spell `private`, `override`, or `export` is therefore part of the name and a multiword target-specific name is refused. Shell assignments retain recursive flavor and expansion, `.RECIPEPREFIX` is a default-origin simple builtin, `.SHELLSTATUS` is readable during recipe compilation, and command-line bindings are retained in session-owned recipe-environment form so `override undefine` matches GNU Make without executor state. Kati commit e4c114c is pushed. All eight build-intent divergences owned by this node now match and their markers are removed. Six new expectations were recorded from `/usr/bin/make` GNU Make 4.4.1; all 263 cases pass. The 296-file direct-vs-manifest graph gate passes. `check-make-conformance.sh` still reports four generated-include inventory changes from the prior node and the intended `.SHELLSTATUS` corpus-shape change; its separately claimed `make-conformance-corpus` owner must reclassify those, so this node does not edit that inventory."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T14:24:45.243432158Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

