            {
                id make-pattern-rule-output-overlap
                title "Let two pattern rules offer the same output"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-conformance-corpus)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:19:05.057192557Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from the GNU Make corpus triage in make-conformance-corpus, with a reproducer already recorded: tests/make/feature-two-pattern-rules-make-the-same-output, marked as a known divergence.\n\nA pattern rule with several targets is a rule that can make any one of them, not a rule that makes them together — that is what `&:` is for. So two pattern rules may both offer `%.elf`, and GNU Make simply chooses one per goal:\n\n    .SECONDARY:\n    all: a.elf a.dbg\n    %.elf %.lnk: %.c ; ... touch $*.elf $*.lnk\n    %.elf %.dbg: %.lnk ; ... touch $*.elf $*.dbg\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 runs both recipes and produces a.elf, a.lnk and a.dbg. Ronin refuses the whole graph with `multiple rules generate a.elf` and builds nothing, so this is a refusal rather than a wrong answer.\n\nTwo reasons to take it seriously beyond the one case. It is 33 of the upstream suite's features/patternrules residues, the largest compiler-shaped group after MAKEFLAGS. And the same diagnostic is what stops scripts/check-make-project.sh today: building Ninja's own CMake-generated Makefile ends at `ronin: multiple rules generate build/browse_py.h` before any recipe runs. Whether the project gate's instance has this cause or another one is unestablished and worth establishing first — that failure reproduces unchanged on main, so it is not new.\n\nA near neighbour is worth checking in the same pass rather than separately. With both rules taking the same prerequisite (`%.elf %.lnk: %.c` and `%.elf %.dbg: %.c`) Ronin does not refuse; it builds a.elf and a.dbg and never makes a.lnk, where GNU Make applies both rules and makes all three. Same misreading of what a multi-target pattern rule means, showing as missing work instead of a refusal."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T21:47:20.331373514Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Correction to the opening note: in GNU Make 4.4.1, a multi-target pattern rule is always grouped whether written with : or &:; &: is not what makes a pattern rule grouped. The manual states that all pattern rules with multiple targets are treated as grouped, and implicit.c represents the peer targets through also_make. The defect here is therefore candidate selection when two grouped pattern rules overlap one output, not independent recipes. The opening note also associated the real-project build/browse_py.h refusal with this cause; inspection shows that target is explicit and the same generated CMake build.make is compiled through two recursive child units, so that failure remains a separate subninja-composition defect. Finally, bare .SECONDARY: exposes a distinct direct-graph no-work behavior; the focused fixture now scopes it to .SECONDARY: a.lnk so this node tests only output overlap."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T21:47:40.320471162Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implementation in kati 29933ef turns each trie hit into a candidate carrying the rule, the exact matched target pattern, and source order. Candidate search follows GNU implicit.c by trying the shortest stem first and source order on ties, and follows rule.c by removing an older exact repeat. Both chain discovery and final selection use that same order. When a grouped rule records its peer outputs, it now preserves any maker already selected for that peer goal instead of creating a duplicate generator. The focused divergence is removed and a neighboring same-prerequisite fixture proves both recipes and all three outputs survive. A manual case where one rule matches a.x through both %.x and a.% also agrees with GNU and selects stem a. Verification under scripts/sandboxed: nplan check green; focused build-intent 1 passed; workspace tests 664 passed and 1 ignored; kati clippy with warnings denied green; Make conformance completed 386 runs; direct/manifest equivalence completed 296 makefiles and 272 compared graphs. scripts/check-make-project.sh remains separately blocked by the explicit build/browse_py.h duplicate described in the correction note."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T21:47:55.758460953Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T22:02:06.477857397Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Independent review on 2026-08-10 found two correctness gaps in kati 29933ef and outer a1aecfe. First, expanded_pattern_inputs caches by rule and requested output, but separate target-pattern candidates of one rule can have different stems and second-expanded prerequisites; an unsuccessful a%.x candidate can cache b.ok and poison a succeeding %.x candidate that requires the existing ab.ok. Second, repeats_rule uses exact target-vector equality instead of GNU new_pattern_rule replacement: with equal prerequisites, a later grouped %.x %.y rule containing an earlier single %.x target replaces that earlier rule, while 29933ef keeps and selects the first recipe. Follow-up make-pattern-rule-candidate-identity will key expansion by candidate identity and model target-subsumption replacement during rule population, with GNU-recorded regressions. The completed commits remain append-only history and must not ship without that correction."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-target-specific-separator-is-literal
                title "Read the target-specific separator from the line as written"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-conformance-corpus)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:19:13.428803144Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from the GNU Make corpus triage in make-conformance-corpus, with two reproducers already recorded and marked as known divergences: tests/make/feature-expanded-equals-in-a-prerequisite and tests/make/feature-expanded-equals-alone-in-a-prerequisite-list.\n\nWhether a rule line carries a target-specific assignment is decided by GNU Make on the line as written, before the prerequisites are expanded. Kati decides it afterwards, so an `=` that only exists because a variable expanded to one is read as an assignment operator:\n\n    EQ = =\n    all: one$(EQ)two ; @printf '%s\\n' goal > out\n    one$(EQ)two: ; @printf '%s\\n' prerequisite > dep-out\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 writes both `out` and `dep-out`. Ronin writes only `dep-out`: the goal's own recipe never runs, and the run still exits 0, which is the worse half — a build that quietly did less than it was asked.\n\nThe second shape crashes rather than diverges. When the expansion produces a bare `=`, as `.PHONY: all $(EQ)` does, kati panics: `called Option::unwrap() on a None value` at kati/src-rs/eval.rs:814, in eval_rule_specific_assign, where an assignment with an empty right-hand side and no `;` recipe leaves `rhs` as None and it is unwrapped anyway. Even once the separator is read from the source line, that unwrap should not be there.\n\nThe suite's features/targetvars line 37 is where this was found — `.PHONY: one two ... ten $(EQ) one$(EQ)two` — and it is 21 of that script's residues. The area was last rewritten by make-assignment-modifiers (kati e4c114c), which moved the *modifier* words (`private`, `override`, `export`) onto the literal source text; the separator itself was left on the expanded text, and this is the rest of that job."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T21:49:31.090093892Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Implemented GNU Make's partial-expansion boundary for rule-specific assignment classification: literal source text decides separators after a written colon; when the colon is expanded, only the remainder of that colon-bearing source word can contribute an expanded assignment operator. Added Kati unit/corpus coverage plus outer GNU-recorded fixtures for later-word expanded equals and literal equals after a prior prerequisite. The Make corpus is now 261 raw / 299 normalized exact of 387 with 88 classified differences, and upstream targetvars evaluation residues fell from 23 to 18. Verified workspace tests/check/fmt, Kati -D warnings clippy, focused make_port, conformance, and graph equivalence (274 compared, zero disagreements)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T21:49:39.782024318Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T22:02:26.825213909Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Independent review found the delivered classifier incomplete in three connected cases: after an expanded first colon it forced a later literal equals into an assignment even when the literal suffix was not a one-word variable assignment; it lost target-specific modifier words when classification happened after expansion; and colon_in_source recorded any source colon rather than whether the first effective colon was literal, so a colon appended after a fully expanded rule changed the earlier colon's meaning. Corrective work is tracked by dependent node make-target-specific-separator-provenance; the original completion and commits remain append-only history."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makeflags-residue-classification
                title "Name the MAKEFLAGS share of the upstream residue"
                kind @test
                deps (make-conformance-corpus)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T18:21:26.533466517Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from the residue triage in make-conformance-corpus, sized rather than guessed: 129 of the 621 unclassified upstream cases are variables/MAKEFLAGS, the largest single group by a factor of four, and most of them are already-named `makeflags-content` that the recogniser misses on one detail.\n\n`is_flag_list` in examples/make_upstream.rs asks that every word be `--`, start with `-`, or contain `=`. GNU Make's MAKEFLAGS does not start that way: its first word is a dashless cluster of the single-letter switches, so a Makefile that prints it produces `rR -- hello:=world`, or `erR -- ...`, or on its own just `R` or `-w`. The leading cluster fails the test, the whole residue is therefore not a flag list, and the case falls through to `evaluation` — the family that means nothing more than \"not recognised\".\n\nFixing the recogniser is not simply loosening it. A bare `R` is a weak shape and a recipe could print it, and the classifier's stated standard for a weak shape is already set by `recipe-echo`: read the makefile the case ran rather than guess. `recipe_of` finds that makefile through the driver's `.run` file and keeps only its recipe lines; naming this family wants the same lookup keeping the whole text, so that a residue of option-shaped words counts as MAKEFLAGS content when the makefile actually mentions MAKEFLAGS, and stays unclassified when it does not.\n\nExpect this to move on the order of a hundred cases from unclassified to interface, and to shrink `evaluation` to something a person could work through. It changes no compiler behaviour: `makeflags-content` is an interface observation, and runner-only flags may deliberately be absent from ours."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T19:52:26.124447131Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The MAKEFLAGS share was 129 cases and the recogniser missed it for two reasons, not one. Both are named now; 97 cases left the unclassified residue and none entered it.\n\nWHAT GNU MAKE'S VALUE ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, probed on 4.4.1 rather than recalled:\n\n  -e -r -R                     erR\n  -r -R FOO=bar                rR -- FOO=bar\n  -e -r -R hello:=world FOO=bar  erR -- FOO=bar hello:=world\n  -B bye=moon                  B -- bye=moon\n  -w / -s / -k                 w / s / k\n  --no-print-directory          --no-print-directory   (leading space, empty cluster)\n  (nothing)                    (empty)\n\ndefine_makeflags in main.c writes it in three parts: the argument-less short switches run together with no dash in front, then every switch carrying an argument or having only a long name each with its own dash, then ` -- ` and the command-line variable definitions. The cluster's alphabet is exactly the `flag`/`flag_off` entries of `switches[]` with a single-letter name and `toenv` — `BdeikLnpqrRsStw`; `-h` and `-v` are excluded there and `-b`/`-m` are ignore switches.\n\nCHANGE 1 — `is_flag_list` reads the leading cluster, but only on the makefile's word.\nThe node's diagnosis was right: every word had to be `--`, start with `-`, or hold an `=`, and `erR` is none of those, so the whole residue failed the test and the case fell to `evaluation`. Loosening it outright was not on: `rR` is also a word, and the classifier's standard for a weak shape is already set by `recipe-echo` — read the makefile the case ran rather than guess. `recipe_of` became `Source::read`, which keeps the whole text and answers two questions instead of one: what the recipe lines are, and whether the makefile mentions MAKEFLAGS at all. The cluster counts only when it does, and only as the first word, and only when no letter repeats — which is what keeps `keep` and `hello` out.\n\nCHANGE 2 — `'all' is up to date.` is narration, and it was the larger blocker.\nNot predicted by the node, and found by dumping the residue instead of trusting the diagnosis. Most MAKEFLAGS cases print the variable twice and then end with `#MAKE#: 'all' is up to date.`, so even with the cluster read the residue was not \"only switches\" and the case stayed unclassified. Measured: GNU Make 4.4.1 says `make: 'all' is up to date.` for a goal whose recipe expands to no command; Ronin makes an edge for it and prints `[1/1] build all`. Both did nothing and said so differently, which is the definition of a narration family. New family `up-to-date-line`, recognised on Make's side only, like every other shape here — a line of that text from us would be something a recipe printed. It explains only itself: output Ronin produced and Make did not still leaves the case unclassified, which is asserted.\n\nMOVEMENT, whole suite, 1351 cases both times\n\n  class          before   after\n    unclassified    626     529\n    narration       536     578\n    interface       116     171\n    compiler         73      73\n\n  family              before   after\n    evaluation           626     529\n    makeflags-content    114     169\n    up-to-date-line        –     112\n    product-name         351     256\n    recipe-interleave      1       3\n    (all others unchanged)\n\nEvery case-level transition is one-way out of `unclassified`: 55 to interface, 42 to narration, 1254 unmoved. Nothing became unclassified, nothing left compiler. `product-name` falls because a line whose reason is the up-to-date announcement is now attributed to that rather than to the name in front of it, exactly as `recipe-error-line` already did. `recipe-interleave` is the family a rerun is allowed to decide differently and is excluded from the drift comparison.\n\nvariables/MAKEFLAGS itself: unclassified 129 → 65, makeflags-content 85 → 137. The 112 `up-to-date-line` cases are not all in that category — it also cleared residue in features/patternrules, features/include, features/se_explicit, targets/{INTERMEDIATE,NOTINTERMEDIATE,SECONDARY}, options/dash-W and options/dash-C, which is the point of recognising a shape rather than a category.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT IN variables/MAKEFLAGS, and why it stays unclassified\n65 cases. 28 are the -k/-S matrix, whose residue carries three unrelated things at once (a recipe echo Make renders as `#HELPER# -q fail 1` and Ronin as its whole `env … /bin/sh -c \"…\"` wrapper, Make's `Target 'all' not remade because of errors.`, and a `two` Ronin never prints). 20 are the `--debug=b`/`--debug=j` cases, whose expected output is a regex the driver never matched, because Ronin emits no --debug narration — a real family, but one that belongs with the existing `--debug=i`/`v` handoff rather than here. The remaining 17 are singletons: --trace narration, `.INCLUDE_DIRS`, a sub-make's captured output, and two command-line-variable parse errors. Naming any of these would have been naming something other than MAKEFLAGS.\n\nDEFECTS FOUND AND FILED — two, both reproduced against 4.4.1 with focused makefiles rather than inferred from the diff.\n\n  make-makefile-set-makeflags — a makefile assigning MAKEFLAGS writes a string, where GNU writes a switch table. `MAKEFLAGS=-k` in a makefile is inert: GNU builds `two` after `one` fails, Ronin does not, which is a build-outcome difference and the reproducer to run through the intent gate. A plain assignment also discards what the environment and command line put there (env `-S` + makefile `-w`: GNU `Sw`, Ronin `-w`; command line `hello=world`: GNU `w -- hello=world`, Ronin `-w`, losing MAKEOVERRIDES). `+=` appends text instead of adding a switch, so nothing is re-rendered and contradicting switches from different origins are never resolved. `make-switch-precedence`'s closing note carved out the parent-to-child half of this question; this is the half the makefile writes.\n\n  make-makeflags-in-recipe-environment — a recipe is told nothing. With `-k V=1` GNU gives a recipe `MAKEFLAGS=k -- V=1` and `MFLAGS=-k`; Ronin gives both empty, and passes an inherited value through raw (`-r` where GNU renders `r`). `compilation_context` puts MAKELEVEL in `recipe_environment` and nothing else; MAKEFLAGS reaches a recipe only as `publication_of`'s jobserver words. This is the value libtool, cmake and autotools sub-builds read.\n\nNeither is fixed here. The corpus decision's rule holds: a family names why outputs differ, and it becomes a defect only through a focused build-intent reproducer — which is what these two nodes carry.\n\nVERIFICATION\ncargo test --workspace green (475 + 92 + 70 + the integration binaries; the classifier's own suite 11 → 14 tests, the three new ones covering the cluster alphabet, the makefile guard, and the up-to-date line's side discipline and its refusal to explain anything else). cargo fmt --check clean for every file this node touched. scripts/check-make-upstream.sh --update recorded 1351 cases; a second run with no --update exits 0 against the recording, so the inventory is reproducible. All runs under scripts/sandboxed.\n\nTwo gate observations that are pre-existing on main and not this node's, recorded so the next person does not rediscover them: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` reports an import-order diff in kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs:39 at the pinned commit cf6b064, and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery` reports 593 findings in kati and 17 in examples/{alloc_metrics,baseline}.rs — none anywhere else, and none in examples/make_upstream.rs. `cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings`, the line check-release.sh scopes to the fork, is green."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T19:52:31.117510043Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

