                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented GNU Make's rule file-sequence blank quoting as a zero-copy scanner for ordinary names: an immediate backslash run before space or tab is halved, an odd run keeps the blank inside the name, and an even run leaves it as a separator. Rule targets, immediate prerequisites, static target-pattern words, and fully expanded prerequisite results use the same scanner. Explicit and static second expansion retain and expand the whole prerequisite list; implicit pattern rules first preserve GNU implicit.c get_next_word raw-word boundaries, skipping variable-reference contents and carrying order-only state, then scan each expanded word separately. This prevents a raw trailing backslash from quoting the boundary before the next implicit prerequisite while still accepting an escaped blank introduced by expansion. Five GNU Make 4.4.1-recorded fixtures cover immediate, explicit, implicit decline/success, and static paths; the stale escaped-space target divergence and both colon_ws inventory rows are removed. Independent review APPROVED. Green evidence: Kati all-target tests (101 library, 5 no-global); owned Make oracle 304/304; 387-run conformance (262 raw, 301 normalized, 43 defects and 11 recorded); workspace tests (671 passed, 1 ignored); formatting, diff checks, and clippy with warnings denied; direct/manifest equivalence over 297 Makefiles and 276 graphs with zero disagreements; reproducible 1351-case upstream inventory at 520 unclassified, 68 compiler, 172 interface, and 591 narration; nplan check, lint, comments, and audit. The real-project witness remains independently red only for multiple rules generating build/browse_py.h."
