                                after_done true
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-makeflags-in-recipe-environment
                        title "Give a recipe the MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS GNU Make gives it"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T19:50:38.792405765Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Measured on make-makeflags-residue-classification, 2026-08-09, against GNU Make 4.4.1 (/usr/bin/make).\n\n  all:; @echo \"MAKEFLAGS=[$$MAKEFLAGS] MFLAGS=[$$MFLAGS] MAKELEVEL=[$$MAKELEVEL]\"\n\n    invocation          GNU Make 4.4.1                              Ronin\n    (none)              MAKEFLAGS=[] MFLAGS=[] MAKELEVEL=[1]        identical\n    -k V=1              MAKEFLAGS=[k -- V=1] MFLAGS=[-k]            MAKEFLAGS=[] MFLAGS=[]\n    env MAKEFLAGS=-r    MAKEFLAGS=[r] MFLAGS=[-r]                   MAKEFLAGS=[-r] MFLAGS=[]\n    -j2 -k V=1          MAKEFLAGS=[k -j2 --jobserver-auth=fifo:… -- V=1]\n                        MFLAGS=[-k -j2 --jobserver-auth=fifo:…]     MAKEFLAGS=[] MFLAGS=[]\n\nMAKELEVEL agrees everywhere; the other two agree only when there is nothing to say. Three differences: the invocation's own switches never reach the recipe, an inherited value is passed through raw rather than re-rendered (`-r` where GNU writes `r`), and MFLAGS is never set at all.\n\nMECHANISM\n`compilation_context` in src/make/cli.rs builds `recipe_environment` from MAKELEVEL alone. MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS reach a recipe only through `publication_of` in src/jobserver.rs, which carries the budget words for a jobserver client and nothing else — and the -j2 row above shows that path did not fire here either. `compiler_flag_variables` already renders exactly the string that is wanted: it is what the makefile is told and what a semantic child is handed. It is simply not put in the recipe's environment.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND SPELLING\nThis is the value a nested tool reads. libtool, cmake, autotools sub-builds and the kernel's scripts branch on `$MAKEFLAGS` from the environment rather than on `$(MAKEFLAGS)`, and a recipe that shells out to one of them is told nothing about -k, -n, -s or the command-line variables. `make-export-to-recipes`'s opening note assumed MAKELEVEL, MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS were all literals already in BuildOptions::environment; only MAKELEVEL is.\n\nUPSTREAM SUITE\nExercised at tests/scripts/variables/MAKEFLAGS:54 (`\"\\$$MAKEFLAGS=$$MAKEFLAGS\"`) and :158 (`XX := $(shell echo \"$$MAKEFLAGS\")`). Both now land in makeflags-content, which is an interface observation; this node is the defect claim behind that share of it, and wants a focused reproducer on what a nested tool does differently rather than on the bytes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:01.269724374Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T07:39:04.98862772Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T07:44:47.806679836Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Fixed the package-proven shell-loop case by compiling each unit's final evaluated MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS into every recipe environment before the existing export/unexport delta. Jobserver publication therefore splices authorization into canonical flags instead of replacing them. Added an integration reproducer with -j2 -k and an empty command-line VALUE assignment; the real nested Make prints VALUE= and never the file default. cargo fmt --all -- --check and all 81 CLI integration tests pass."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T08:31:56.665735683Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

