        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-08T03:10:23.934566535Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Five nodes worked in parallel in isolated worktrees on 2026-08-08 and merged as one wave: make-second-expansion-patterns, make-option-output-sync, make-option-touch-and-what-if, make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent, make-option-debug-and-trace + make-option-shuffle.\n\nThe merged classification, measured after the merge rather than summed from the lanes — each lane measured against the same 767-semantic baseline, so their movements overlap and adding them would be wrong:\n\n                 before   after\n  capability       126      54\n  option-refused    93      20\n  narration        439     522\n  semantic         767     772\n  counted        1,332   1,348\n\nSemantic is up five and that is the honest number, but it is not the reading. 72 capability refusals were removed, which makes previously uncountable cases countable and lands most of them in semantic; against that, second expansion moved 65 cases from semantic to narration. `no-rule-to-make` fell 105 → 45 and `unsupported-feature` to 35, both from second expansion.\n\nThe real-project gate on a quiet host: Ronin 21s against GNU Make's 20s on Ninja's own CMake-generated Makefile at -j8. It was 64.4s against 22.9s on 2026-08-07.\n\nThree lanes found and fixed defects outside their own node, which is the argument for running them concurrently rather than serially: `|` did not end the word it fell in, so `%.o: %.c|bar.h` silently fell through to the built-in cc rule; `-O` arriving in an inherited MAKEFLAGS was read letter by letter and silently turned on -e and -r; and `-t` was undone by the next build, because an output with no build-log entry is dirty here.\n\nThree lanes also found the recorded defect on their node incomplete or wrong and said so. make-second-expansion-patterns named one of five causes, and the largest — that a prerequisite list should be deferred only when it still holds a `$`, as GNU's read.c does — was not among them.\n\nThe cost was contention on src/make/cli.rs, which four lanes edited. It ended three functions over the density gate; the extraction into src/make/report.rs (ea942c5) cleared it. One wave of this size is about the limit for one shared file.\n\nHandoffs recorded by the lanes and not yet noded: `$(MAKEFLAGS)` cannot report this build's own budget, because the fifo does not exist until after evaluation; `announcing()` implements only the -C half of GNU's should_print_dir, missing `makelevel > 0`; the directory announcement carries no `[N]` level; kati marks a recipe recursive only for a leading `+` and not for `$(MAKE)`; `*** No targets.` fires when only pattern rules exist even with a goal named; `--debug=i`/`v` narrate the dependency search and need kati; and SinkEdge::loc is now read, which recipe-error-line (78 cases) wants next."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-exit-status-parity
        title "Exit with the status Ninja exits with when a command fails"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:25:12.172102097Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Opened from the dangling referral on ronin-command-launcher's 2026-08-04 note: Ronin exits 1 where Ninja propagates the failing command's exit status. The claim about Ninja's behaviour must be verified against real Ninja before matching it — the oracle is Ninja, not the note. The failure-surface conformance scenarios from ronin-failure-surface-parity are the natural place for exit-status assertions."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:09.775678397Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Exit-status parity: the referral was stale, and the real defect was the interrupt.\n\nThe provenance claim — ronin-command-launcher's \"Ronin exits 1 when a command fails where Ninja exits with the command's own status\" — is REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT. It was true when written on 2026-08-03 and fixed hours later by ronin-failure-surface-parity, whose own note opens its defect list with it. The referral was never retracted, so it outlived its subject by six days. Pinned Ninja b51a1e3 does propagate the status (subprocess-posix.cc ParseExitStatus returns WEXITSTATUS transparently; build_result.h carries it out through BuildResult::exit_status), and Ronin already did the same before this node: `exit 7` was 7 on both sides, and compare_build_case had been comparing rc all along.\n\nProbing 66 shapes found three real divergences, none of them the one referred. All three are fixed.\n\nThe first was predicted, in writing, by ronin-failure-surface-parity: \"Ninja returns immediately when it cannot finish a command, where Ronin counts it as a keep-going failure and carries on. I could not produce a case where the two differ — the obvious one, a missing depfile under -k 0, behaves identically in both — so I did not restructure the scheduler on a hypothesis.\" The case exists; it is an interrupt rather than a finish failure. That note's discipline is why this one found it.\n\nPROBE TABLE (ninja / ronin, both after the fix; \"was\" is what Ronin did before it)\n\n  Direct command status\n    exit 0 (control)                      0 / 0\n    exit 1                                1 / 1\n    exit 3                                3 / 3\n    exit 111                          111 / 111\n    exit 255                          255 / 255\n    exit 130                          130 / 130   was 130, but with a FAILED: line and no early stop\n    command not found                 127 / 127\n\n  Signal-killed command (raw wait status + 128, truncated to & 0xff on exit)\n    SIGSEGV                             11 / 11   (FAILED: [code=267])\n    SIGBUS                                7 / 7\n    SIGQUIT                               3 / 3   (FAILED: [code=259])\n    SIGKILL                           137 / 137\n    SIGINT                            130 / 130\n    SIGTERM                           130 / 130\n    SIGHUP                            130 / 130\n\n  Multiple failures\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 0              5 / 5   (last non-zero wins)\n    exit 5 then exit 3, -k 0              3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, no -k             3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 2              5 / 5\n    exit 9 then success, -k 0             9 / 9\n    SEGV then exit 5, -k 0                5 / 5\n    SEGV then success, -k 0             11 / 11\n    exit 5 then SIGTERM, -k 0         130 / 130\n    SIGTERM then exit 5, -k 0         130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGINT then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGHUP then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    exit 130 then exit 5, -k 0        130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n\n  Dry run\n    -n over a failing command             0 / 0\n    -n over a manifest error              1 / 1\n\n  Manifest and graph errors\n    syntax error, unknown rule            1 / 1\n    missing input                         1 / 1\n    self-referencing phony (warns)        0 / 0\n    phony cycle, real-rule cycle          1 / 1\n    unknown target on the command line    1 / 1\n    missing manifest, unknown flag, bad -j  1 / 1\n    failing generator regen (stale)       1 / 1\n    generator killed by SIGSEGV (stale)   1 / 1\n\n  Tools and arguments\n    -t query on an unknown target         1 / 1\n    -t unknown tool, -t browse            1 / 1\n    -t targets/list/clean/deps/missingdeps/recompact  0 / 0\n    --version                             0 / 0\n    -h, -d list, -d bad, -w bad           1 / 1\n\n  Post-command paths\n    success, no depfile written (no deps) 0 / 0\n    success, garbage depfile (no deps)    0 / 0\n    deps=gcc, depfile never written       0 / 0   was 1    <-- DEFECT 2\n    deps=gcc, depfile in a missing dir    0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    plain depfile in a missing dir        0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    failure plus a garbage depfile        6 / 6\n    rspfile in a missing directory        1 / 1   was 0    <-- DEFECT 3\n    restat with the output untouched      0 / 0\n\n  Quiet, console, verbose, subninja\n    --quiet and -v over exit 7            7 / 7\n    console pool, exit 7 / exit 111   7 / 7, 111 / 111\n    console pool, SIGSEGV               11 / 11\n    failure inside a subninja           21 / 21\n\n  The tool itself signalled mid-build\n    SIGINT to the tool                130 / 130\n    SIGTERM to the tool               130 / 130\n    SIGHUP to the tool                130 / 130\n\nDEFECT 1 — an interrupt ends the build, and 130 is how Ninja spells one.\nNinja's ExitStatus enum spends 130 on ExitInterrupted and then reads every finished command's status back through that same number: `result.interrupted() || result.exit_status() == ExitInterrupted`, checked before the completion is counted as a failure at all. Two consequences Ronin missed. A command that plainly exits 130 is an interrupt — no FAILED: line, build over — because Ninja has no way to tell it from one killed by SIGINT and does not try. And an interrupt leaves the loop whatever -k still allowed, so no later command's status can overwrite the 130 that says why the build stopped. Ronin classified only signal deaths as interrupts and, under -k, counted them as ordinary failures and carried on: the whole point of the status was lost precisely when a user had asked for the build to stop. Fixed in src/build.rs as `Builder::command_interrupted`, plus a break on BuildError::Interrupted in the scheduler loop.\n\nGated behind a new BuildOptions field, `command_status_interrupts`, which the Make front end turns off. Measured GNU Make 4.4.1: a recipe exiting 130 is `*** [Makefile:2: a] Error 130` and make exits 2, with no interrupt semantics anywhere. Ronin's Make mode wraps recipes in an outer shell, so a signalled recipe already arrives as 128+N rather than as a signal and the existing classification never fired there — but an ungated 130 rule would have changed Make's narration and its -k behaviour for a number Ninja's enum merely happens to have spent. Make conformance output is byte-identical before and after, verified by reverting to base, rebuilding and re-running: the same six recorded-difference drifts, which are pre-existing on main and not this node's.\n\nDEFECT 2 — a depfile that is not there is an empty one.\nNinja's ExtractDeps clears NotFound and returns success on empty content, so a compiler that emits no depfile for a translation unit with no includes keeps the status it exited with. Ronin raised DependencyFileMissing and turned a successful command into exit 1 — samurai's behaviour, not Ninja's. The deps-log record the fix produces is identical to the one an existing-but-empty depfile produces (`#deps 0 … (VALID)`), checked against Ninja through `-t deps` rather than assumed.\n\nDEFECT 3 — the depfile's directory is made and the response file's is not.\nNinja MakeDirs the outputs and the depfile, then WriteFile the rspfile with no MakeDirs. Ronin had these exactly the wrong way round, and so was wrong in both directions: a compiler writing into a depfile directory that did not exist failed (2 against Ninja's 0), and an rspfile pointed at a missing directory succeeded (0 against Ninja's 1). The asymmetry is not an oversight upstream — a compiler writes its own depfile and cannot be asked to create the directory first, while a response file the build tool writes itself has nowhere to go and should say so.\n\nCONFORMANCE\nBUILD_CASES went 32 to 44, and every case, build and invocation alike, now records the Ninja status it must leave with as a literal. Comparing the two tools to each other was never the gap; what was missing is a check that a case still exercises what it is named after. Three did not. \"A killed command reports the shell's status\", \"a terminated command reads as an interrupt\" and \"a quit command is an ordinary failure\" were written `kill -KILL $$`, and `$$` is the manifest escape for one `$` — so all three ran `kill -KILL $`, a usage error exiting 2 that both tools agreed on perfectly. Two of the three were the only coverage of the interrupt path this node then found broken. Fixed to `$$$$`, and the recorded statuses (137, 130, 3) now hold them to it. New shapes added: SIGSEGV, SIGHUP, exit 130, exit 111, a missing command, an interrupt outranking -k in both orders, keep-going past a signal, a dry run over a failure, a missing depfile, the depfile directory, and a console command's status.\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT — recorded rather than fixed, status matches and bytes do not\nAn rspfile that cannot be written reports differently. Ninja: stderr `ninja: error: WriteFile(nodir/x.rsp): Unable to create file. No such file or directory` plus stdout `ninja: build stopped: .`; Ronin: stdout `ronin: build stopped: No such file or directory.` Both exit 1, so the status contract holds, but a conformance case cannot be added until the bytes agree. Filed as ronin-response-file-write-diagnostic.\n\nUncovered incidentally, off this node's surface, by a typo in a case manifest: an unexpected indent following a top-level binding is located at a different line by each tool — Ninja build.ninja:4 with no source context, Ronin build.ninja:3 with context. Filed as ronin-indent-error-location.\n\nVERIFICATION\ncargo test --all-targets 475 passing plus the integration binaries, clippy clean, fmt --check clean, scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh green: 425 upstream tests, 10 CLI/tool cases, 44 build-outcome cases each on its recorded exit status, 3 invocation cases likewise, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. All 66 probed shapes match. Probes and gates run under scripts/sandboxed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:53.863809098Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T19:05:47.569973822Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

