    {
        id ronin-indent-error-location
        title "Locate an unexpected indent where Ninja locates it"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:46.070001876Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Measured on ronin-exit-status-parity, 2026-08-09, against pinned Ninja b51a1e3. Found by a typo in a conformance manifest rather than by looking, which is why it is worth writing down: the existing case \"an unexpected indent carries no source context\" passes, so this shape is next to covered ground and still differs.\n\nManifest — a rule block ended by a top-level binding, with an indented line after it:\n\n  rule cc\n    command = x\n  depfile = y\n    deps = gcc\n  build a: cc\n\n  ninja  rc=1  stderr: ninja: error: build.ninja:4: unexpected indent\n  ronin  rc=1  stderr: ronin: error: build.ninja:3: unexpected indent\n                       depfile = y\n                                   ^ near here\n\nTwo differences. Ronin blames line 3, the top-level binding that closed the block, where Ninja blames line 4, the indented line that could not belong to anything. And Ronin prints source context where Ninja prints none for this error — the covered case establishes that Ninja gives none, so the divergence is in which line is reported deciding whether context is attached.\n\nExit status agrees (1), so this is a message-shape defect, not an outcome one. A conformance BuildCase should be added with the manifest above when it is fixed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T20:01:16.67040671Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "An indent is a *token*, and Ronin was not reading one. Fixing that closed 41 divergent manifest shapes and 6 dyndep ones, not the 1 referred.\n\nThe referral was right and small: `unexpected indent` named the line above the indent and dragged that line's source context onto a diagnostic Ninja shows none for. The cause is one line of Ninja's lexer that Ronin had transcribed as control flow instead of as a rule. `ReadToken` ends with `last_token_ = start`, where `start` is the beginning of whatever the re2c match consumed — for `[ ]+` that is the *first space*, at column zero of the indented line. Column zero is exactly what `Lexer::Error` uses to suppress context. So \"names the indented line\" and \"carries no caret\" are not two behaviours; they are one anchor. Ronin raised the error without marking a token at all, so it reported against whatever had been read last, and the context followed from that.\n\n**What made this worth more than a `begin_token` call.** Reading the three rules that can match where a line begins, as a longest-match set rather than as a chain of tests, is what the rest of it turned on:\n\n    [ ]*\"#\"[^\\000\\n]*\"\\n\"     skipped entirely\n    [ ]*\"\\r\\n\" | [ ]*\"\\n\"     one newline\n    [ ]+                      an indent\n\nRonin's `scankeyword` and `scanindent` had each open-coded a different approximation of these using `space()` and `comment()`, and both approximations were wrong in ways the referral did not mention.\n\n`space()` is `EatWhitespace`, which crosses `$`-escaped line endings. None of the three rules does — Ninja eats a continuation *after* a token, never in front of one. Using it as the indent recogniser meant a line holding only `  $` was read as whitespace running into the next line: `x = 1\\n  $\\n\\ny = 2\\n` parsed clean and built where Ninja stops with `unexpected indent` on line 2, and `rule cc\\n  command = x\\n  $\\n  # comment\\n  deps = gcc\\nbuild a: cc\\n` did not merely misreport — it built the graph and *ran the command*, exiting 127 where Ninja exits 1.\n\n`comment()` had no end-of-file case, so it swallowed a comment that never got its newline. The rule wants that newline: without it the comment matches nothing and the spaces in front of it are all that matched. `  # c` at the end of a file is an *indent*, and `# c` at the end of a file is the byte no token begins with — `lexing error`. Ronin accepted both files. Upstream's own `Lexer.CommentEOF` asserts `ERROR` here; Ronin's port test, adapted from it, asserted `None`. That is the second time this session a port test encoded the opposite of the case it was named after, and like the `kill -KILL $$` three it was found by probing rather than by reading.\n\n`scanindent` also *consumed* the newline it peeked at instead of putting it back. Ninja's `PeekToken` unreads, so after a block ends the lexer stands at the start of the line that ended it — leading spaces included. Every check a block defers is located there, so an indented blank line closing a rule is `build.ninja:3: expected 'command =' line` in Ninja and was `:4` in Ronin. That is the same one-line-off defect as the referral, in the opposite direction, on a path the referral never looked at.\n\n**The fix** is one function, `linestart`, that returns Indent / Newline / Other with the token marked at the first space, plus `Scanner::unread_token` for the peek. `scankeyword` and `scanindent` are now both expressed through it; `comment()` is gone, its spec annotations moved to the function that does its work. `singlespace` reuses the new `Mark` for its own backtrack. Net: src/scan.rs, and two stale comments in src/parse.rs that described the deferred-check anchor as \"the line after the statement\" when it is the line that ended the block.\n\n**PROBE TABLE** — 78 shapes through pinned Ninja b51a1e3 and Ronin, stdout, stderr and status compared byte for byte after normalising the product name. Columns: ninja / ronin-before → ronin-after. All rc 1 unless stated.\n\n  Where the indent is\n    first line of the file                        1 / 1            (already agreed)\n    after a top-level binding                     2 / 1 → 2\n    after a blank line                            3 / 1 → 3\n    after a comment line                          3 / 1 → 3\n    after a binding that closed a rule            4 / 3 → 4        <- the referred shape\n    after an indented blank inside a rule         4 / 2 → 4        (upstream ParseTest)\n    after a build statement's block               6 / 5 → 6\n    after a default statement                     5 / 4 → 5\n    after a pool block                            4 / 3 → 4\n    below a line too long to quote                2 / 1 → 2\n    at EOF with no trailing newline               2 / 1 → 2\n    inside a subninja                             2 / 1 → 2\n    in a subninja after a binding                 4 / 3 → 4\n    two consecutive indented lines                2 / 1 → 2\n    after an empty first line                     2 / 1 → 2\n    an indented line that is itself unparseable   2 / 1 → 2\n    spaces then a tab                             2 / 1 → 2\n    CRLF binding then an indent                   2 / 1 → 2\n    CRLF indented blank then an indent            4 / 2 → 4\n\n  Continuations, which are not indents\n    `  $` then a statement                        2 / 1 → 2\n    `  $` then EOF                                2 / 1 → 2\n    `  $` then a blank line then statements       err / built, rc 0 → err\n    `  $`, spaces, a comment, then statements     err / built, rc 0 → err\n    `  $` in a block landing on a blank line      expected variable name :4 / unexpected indent :2 → match\n    `  $` in a block landing on a comment         expected variable name :4 / built and ran it, rc 127 → match\n\n  Comments that never got their newline\n    `  # c` at statement position                 unexpected indent :2 / built, rc 0 → match\n    `# c` at statement position                   lexing error :2 / built, rc 0 → match\n    `  # c` inside a block                        expected variable name :3, with caret / built, rc 0 → match\n    `  # c` ending a block                        expected variable name :3 / expected 'command =' line :3 → match\n    `# c` ending a block                          expected 'command =' line :3, no context / same line, with context → match\n\n  Carriage returns\n    spaces then a lone CR at statement position   unexpected indent :2 / lexing error :2 → match\n    spaces then a lone CR inside a block          expected variable name :3 / lexing error :3 → match\n    spaces then CRLF, and a lone CR alone         already agreed\n\n  Checks a block defers, located where the failed peek left the scanner\n    rule with no command, indented blank ends it  :3 / :4 → :3\n    the same with two indented blanks             :3 / :4 → :3\n    the same with a CRLF indented blank           :3 / :4 → :3\n    rspfile-only rule, indented blank ends it     :4 / :5 → :4\n    unknown pool, indented blank ends the block   :5 / :6 → :5\n    plain blank line, comment line, EOF           already agreed\n\n  Dyndep files, read by the same lexer\n    indent after the version binding              2 / 1 → 2\n    indent after a build statement                4 / 3 → 4\n    indented blank then an indent                 4 / 2 → 4\n    a continuation-only indented line             2 / 1 → 2\n    a comment ending the file                     lexing error :3 / accepted, rc 0 → match\n    an indented comment ending the file           expected variable name :3 / accepted, rc 0 → match\n\n**Still divergent, recorded rather than fixed.** Dyndep diagnostics now name the same line as Ninja and say the same words, but carry no source context and no terminating blank line — `DyndepError`'s `Display` prints `path:line: message` and never reaches `write_located`, which has been true of every dyndep diagnostic since it was written and is not this node's doing. Filed as ronin-dyndep-diagnostic-context, with the shapes.\n\n**CONFORMANCE.** BUILD_CASES 65 → 81. The 16 new ones are chosen one per mechanism rather than one per shape: five that only a wrong line number distinguishes (including the one below a line too long to quote, where *both* tools print no context and the number is the only signal), the two continuation shapes that used to build, the three comment-at-EOF shapes, the lone carriage return, the three deferred-block anchors, and upstream's own indented-blank-line case. Two scanner unit tests hold the anchor at column one and the put-back at the start of the line that ended the block, for the shapes a corpus case cannot isolate. The port test adapted from `Lexer.CommentEOF` now asserts what upstream asserts.\n\n**VERIFICATION.** 477 lib tests plus the integration binaries, workspace green; clippy `-D warnings` clean; `cargo fmt --check -p ronin` clean; scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh green — 425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 81 build-outcome cases each on its recorded status, 3 invocation cases, the persistence round trip, the log-content checks. All 78 probed shapes match. Everything ran under scripts/sandboxed.\n\nTwo things a reader should not mistake for this node's work. `cargo fmt --all --check` is *not* clean: `kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs` has an import-ordering nit at the pinned kati cf6b064, present on main before this branch and left alone because fixing it means a submodule commit and a pointer bump against a deliberately pinned revision. And `cli::tests::rust_cli_rebuilds_and_reloads_manifest_before_targets` flakes with `MissingInput` on its own template file: 2 failures in the two runs taken immediately after a full recompile, 0 in 22 runs on a warm cache — 12 on this branch and 10 on the pristine tree, so it is pre-existing and load-sensitive, and it is untouched by anything here. Worth its own node if it recurs."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T20:01:21.749088376Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-10T16:22:00.024462701Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "Recovery audit on 2026-08-10: the prior Doing → Done log and delivery commit 5d625c5 are valid, and 5d625c5 is already an ancestor of current main, but the node's completion assertion did not survive later plan integration, so nplan exposed the node as Todo again. The delivered scanner, parser, port-test, and indent conformance code has not drifted since 5d625c5; subsequent changes to examples/conformance.rs only added unrelated response-file cases. Revalidation on current main passed the focused indent/peek tests and upstream CommentEOF port test; all 425 pinned Ninja tests; 10 differential cases; 86 build-outcome cases; 3 invocation cases; persistence and log-content checks; and the forced nplan workspace check. The recovered slice also corrects the nearby LineStart::Indent comment from 'no longer rule' to 'no other rule'. Restoring the manual completion assertion records existing shipped reality rather than delivering a second implementation."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-10T16:22:04.10916719Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-dyndep-diagnostic-context
        title "Show the source context Ninja shows for a dyndep file"
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T20:00:25.622201031Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Measured on ronin-indent-error-location, 2026-08-09, against pinned Ninja b51a1e3. Not fixed there because that node's subject was where an indent is located, and the located line now agrees for every dyndep shape probed; what is left is the rendering.\n\nNinja reads a dyndep file with the same lexer and reports through the same `Lexer::Error`, so a dyndep diagnostic gets the quoted source line and the caret whenever its column is non-zero, and the terminating blank line when it is zero. Ronin's `DyndepError` (src/dyndep.rs:175) prints `{path}:{line}: {kind}` and stops — it never reaches `write_located` in src/error.rs, so *no* dyndep diagnostic has ever carried context.\n\n  dd file: `ninja_dyndep_version = 1\\nbuild out: dyndep\\n  # c`\n    ninja  rc=1  stderr: ninja: error: dd:3: expected variable name\n                         `  # c`\n                         `  ^ near here`\n    ronin  rc=1  stderr: ronin: error: dd:3: expected variable name\n\n  dd file: `  ninja_dyndep_version = 1\\n`\n    ninja  rc=1  stderr: ninja: error: dd:1: unexpected indent  + a blank line\n    ronin  rc=1  stderr: ronin: error: dd:1: unexpected indent  + no blank line\n\nThe span is already there and already correct: `DyndepError` holds a `SourceSpan`, and `scanner_error` carries the scanner's own span with its column. Only `error(scanner, line, kind)` forces `column = 0`, which is right for the checks it raises — those are Ninja's zero-column ones. So the change is to render through the located printer rather than to find the location. It is small; what it needs is a sweep of every dyndep diagnostic against real Ninja before and after, because it moves the bytes of all of them, and conformance BuildCases for the ones that gain a caret. 8 dyndep shapes are already probed in this node's scratch sweep; they are not the whole set.\n\nStatus agrees throughout (1), so this is a message-shape defect. No conformance case can be added for a dyndep diagnostic until it is fixed."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id kati-format-nit-blocks-the-fmt-gate
        title "Make the pinned kati satisfy the format gate"
